"""`Dashboard`: a live dashboard of a task and its builds in place of the
console and Bazel's terminal UI, on the terminal's alternate screen, for
`build`, `test`, `lint`, `gazelle` and `format` (`_KINDS`). Off by default:

    aspect build //... --dashboard

It takes the screen when the task starts and shows:

- a status bar with the task, how long it has run, and Bazel's status
  while a build runs, once the task has ended only what its ✔/✗ cannot
  say, else the task's phase and its latest update;
- each build that ended folded to a row (its verdict, command, actions,
  failures and time, the newest last, `+N earlier` past three), and while a
  build runs, or `p` asks, its panes:
  - a bar of the build's actions (`action_split`, one unit throughout):
    ran `█`, disk and remote cache hits `▓`, the local action cache `▒`,
    internal `░`, other `▞`, failed `╳`, blocked `○`, done but not reported
    yet `┈`,
    then not done `─`, each kind
    its own glyph so they stay apart without colour; while it runs from
    the actions Bazel reports with every one published
    (`--dashboard:actions=all`, the default: internal ones have no command
    line), the execution log's cache hits and the done count's gap (the
    local action cache, estimated), and with only failures published
    (`failed`, or a build whose events Bazel sends out itself:
    `publish_all_actions_unless_sent_out`), `other` for what is neither ran
    nor a hit; at its right end how far it is (`3,830 of 8,563+ actions`,
    `+` while Bazel analyses), at the end its actions and cache hits
    (`8,590 actions · 3,148 cache hits (37%)`); a build `<`/`>` picked,
    its own;
  - the actions running now, from Bazel's own progress area, oldest first,
    yellow past a minute and red past five; once the build has finished,
    Bazel's critical path and the slowest actions seen running;
  - links to the invocation's results pages: the Aspect Build Results page
    the CLI streams to, and the BES results page Bazel streams to itself
    with `--bes_results_url`, each when there is one (OSC 8 hyperlinks);
  - a Results pane: targets, tests with those finished a cache gave whole
    (`7 passed (5 cached)`) and, while a `test` build runs, the test
    targets running and pending (`test_progress`), each kind of action
    there is any of in words, the bar's glyph a coloured key before its
    count, the cache hits as one group with their total (`action_results`;
    while the build runs, ran, internal and failed from the events, disk
    and remote settling as Bazel writes its execution log; at its end the
    split from its `BuildMetrics`), the critical path, and the failures
    counted (a click shows them);
- the main pane's tabs, always in one order, the shown one named by what is
  open in it (`Failures ✗1 ▹ //pkg:t ▹ attempt 1/2 ▹ a.py:12`):
  - Console: the task's whole output, from its start (what the CLI wrote
    before Bazel's console began, then Bazel's, then the CLI's own lines,
    as written), scrollable, in the colours it was written in (the theme's,
    Bazel's recoloured by it), the
    lines under each `INFO: From` folded to a count;
  - Failures: a row for each of the failure summary's entries, as it heads
    them (`failure_entries`: every failed action and test, every root cause
    Bazel's events report; a test's other attempts, shards and runs a `}`
    `{` away), the chosen one's excerpt under it (`record_excerpt`; every
    one's with Ctrl+O), then the targets a failed dependency blocked, then,
    once the build has ended, Bazel's other errors (`console_ties`);
    Enter opens one's log in the tab (its stderr and
    stdout, a test's `test.log`, found where Bazel left it, a remote cache's
    `bytestream://` one in the output tree); `a` lists the actions that
    succeeded and wrote output instead (`--dashboard:actions=all`, the
    default);
  - Metrics: the actions, each kind with what makes it up (the runners'
    spawns, remote and disk cache hits apart, the internal kinds), Bazel's
    action cache with why it missed, the execution log's spawns; tests,
    targets, time and memory, the slowest actions;
  - Invocation: the command, ids, Bazel's version, the flags given,
    workspace status and build metadata, what may be a secret redacted;
  - Tips: the task's tips the screen shows (`SURFACE_DASHBOARD`), the
    most urgent first, counted in the tab in the most urgent one's colour;
    the chosen one's body under it, and `s` silences it on this machine
    (`~/.aspect/config.json`, `silence_tip`) or undoes that, the row left
    muted saying so; the scrollback names what was silenced and how to undo
    it;
  - before any build, Failures, Metrics and Invocation muted, saying `No
    build yet`; with several builds, Metrics and Invocation show the newest,
    `<`/`>` another (`Metrics ▹ 1 of 2`), and Failures the newest's;
- a row of the keys for what has focus, and `?` for those that apply now,
  grouped (both from `KEYS`, which `on_key` dispatches through), then what
  the actions' glyphs are.

The keys, as in `less`, lazygit and Claude Code's transcript: `↑`/`↓` or
`k`/`j` scroll a line and the wheel three; Shift or Option with `↑`/`↓`,
`u`/`d` and Ctrl+`u`/`d` half a page; Page Up/Down, `b` and Space a page;
Shift+`K` or Home the top, Shift+`J` or End back to following (a list's
first and last row), as in Claude Code's transcript. Tab and
Shift+Tab cycle the tabs (or a click); `]`/`[` show the next or previous
failure's log in Failures, `}`/`{` the next or previous attempt, shard or
run of the same target; Esc goes back a crumb, then to the tab's own pane,
then to the console. A click on a path shows the file in place (a peek,
highlighted by `std.syntax`), `o` opens what is shown in the editor
`editor` in `~/.aspect/config.json` or `--console:file-hyperlink-format`
names, and `O` picks another; a click on a web link opens it. The screen
takes the mouse: a drag selects and copies, a double click a word; `m`
(or `--dashboard:mouse=false`) gives it back to the terminal. `e` cycles
every line, errors and warnings, errors only; `w` cuts long lines rather
than wrap them; Ctrl+O unfolds every fold and folds them all again
(forgetting what clicks opened), a click on one (its `INFO: From` or its
count) just that one; `z` gives the main pane the whole screen;
`q` cancels the build, as Ctrl+C does.

Ctrl+F or `/` opens a search bar for a regular expression (`std.re`),
smart case unless Ctrl+T (or Alt+C) makes it match case, whole words with
Ctrl+B (or Alt+W). As you type it jumps to the newest match at or above the
view; Tab in the bar filters to the matching lines instead. Enter keeps it,
and `n`/`N` then step to the next and previous match, round to the other
end past the last; Esc clears it and
goes back to where the bar opened.

When the task ends the screen stays up, its tabs still browsable, until
`q`, Enter or Esc with nothing open, or Ctrl+C, whatever the outcome
(`--dashboard:stay=always`, the default); with `failure` it stays when
anything failed and otherwise counts down ten seconds and leaves unless a
key stops it; with `never` it leaves at once. `build` and `test` keep their
build's panes open on it, the others their builds folded; a task that
failed before any build leaves at once, its error last in the scrollback.
Then the scrollback gets the console as the screen showed it
(`replay_lines`), the flaky tests, the results pages, and the failure
summary the plain console prints too (`failure_summary`: `── Failures
(N) ──`, each failure Bazel's build events report once, what Bazel's
console ties to it exactly, its log's path (the history's copy, which
outlives Bazel's) and the targets it blocked, then Bazel's other errors,
the counts, Bazel's raw log and `aspect history show <id>` for the rest;
`report_lines`). The summary is written only there, never into the
screen's Console tab nor the task's output it dumps, so a run replayed
from the history shows it no more than the screen did.

How it is built:

1. `BazelTrait.output_display = "none"`: Bazel's console is still captured
   and run through every stage, but nothing of it is shown.
2. A `pre_task` hook takes the terminal (`ctx.std.term.take()`, the mouse
   taken with `screen.mouse(True)` unless asked not to, the wheel sent as
   arrows with `screen.wheel_keys(True)` while it is the terminal's), what
   the task wrote before it first in the console (`screen.earlier()`).
   Until a build draws, an idle hook (`ctx.hooks.idle`), which the runtime
   calls about every 100ms while the task waits on a child or sleeps,
   reads the CLI's lines and the keys and redraws.
3. A `BazelTrait.output_frame` hook, which the runtime calls about every
   100ms while a build's console is open, reads what the console would
   have shown (`build.output_feed()`, through `add_lines`), reads the keys
   (`screen.key()`, through `on_key`), and redraws the whole screen in one
   write (`render`) when it changed (Ctrl+L redraws it anyway); its first
   frame starts the build's own counts (`new_build`), a retry's too. The
   finished screen, idle, reads a key a second.
4. `build_event` hooks (`collect_event`, the plain console's too) count
   targets and tests from the build event stream, gather the failures and
   their root causes (`note_event`, `note_blocked`) and the actions that
   wrote output with their logs (`output_of`), and what finding a test's
   log in the output tree takes (the exec root, each configuration's output
   folder, each test's run and shard counts and last attempts).
5. An `output_end` hook folds the build to a row (`fold_build`), keeping
   what its tabs show for `<`/`>`, and draws.
6. A `post_task` hook keeps the screen up (or, with `stay=failure`, counts
   down, the task's exit code 0 and nothing failed), with Ctrl+C read as a
   key
   (`screen.interrupts(False)`) since the task has already ended, then gives
   the terminal back (`screen.release(replay_stderr = False, dump_lines = …)`,
   the task's output dumped to the scrollback as part of it, or, with no
   history to dump from, the console as it showed it after,
   `replay_lines`), then the summary.

A prompt reading stdin on the terminal, and a child marked
`interactive(True)` (`ctx.std.process.command`), get the terminal back for
as long as they run: the task's output so far goes to the scrollback, and
the screen comes back, redrawn, after. An unmarked child gets no terminal
while the screen is up (stdin from `/dev/null`, no controlling terminal), so
mark a spawn interactive if it may prompt.

On a terminal only, and never on CI; any other task keeps its console,
saying so in a line on stderr. Under a coding agent, with `TERM` dumb or
unset, or on a terminal under 40x12, it stays off even when enabled, saying
why in a line on stderr (`refusal`): an agent's pty has nobody to press the
keys the finished screen waits for.
"""

load("@aspect//bazel.axl", "BazelTrait", "ExecLogHook")
load("@aspect//helpers.axl", "file_uri_to_path")
load("@aspect//traits.axl", "Phases")
load("@std//base64.axl", "base64")
load("@std//theme.axl", "theme")
load("@std//time.axl", "monotonic")
load("../private/lib/agent.axl", "under_agent")
load("../private/lib/aspect_flags.axl", "publish_all_actions_unless_sent_out")
load("../private/lib/results_pages.axl", "bes_results_url", "results_pages")
load("../private/lib/tips.axl", "SURFACE_DASHBOARD", "collect_tips_sorted", "severity_rank", "silence_tip", "unsilence_tip")

# The screen's colours, by the run's theme (`@std//theme.axl`), looked up
# as they are drawn: the theme the run has then (the background the screen
# learns, a config's), whatever the stream's own colour.
def _c(role: str) -> str:
    return theme.sgr(role, stream = True)

_RESET = theme.reset

# Console lines kept for scrolling back; older ones are counted in the
# pane's title, and Bazel's console log has them all.
_MAX_LOG = 20000

# The console's text kept, in characters, whatever its lines' length (a
# minified bundle's), and the most of one line: the rest is on disk.
_MAX_LOG_SIZE = 16 * 1024 * 1024
_MAX_LINE = 8 * 1024

# The lines a record's logs share in its view (`fit_logs`).
_MAX_VIEW = 5000

# The most of a failure's log read into its view: the end, where the failure
# usually is.
_MAX_LOG_BYTES = 1024 * 1024

# Escape sequences a log file may hold (colour, cursor moves, OSC links,
# character set choices, two-byte ones such as saving the cursor).
ANSI = "\\x1b\\[[0-?]*[ -/]*[@-~]|\\x1b\\][^\\x07\\x1b]*(?:\\x07|\\x1b\\\\)|\\x1b[ -/]+[0-~]|\\x1b[0-~]"

# Control characters a log line may still hold once its escape sequences are
# gone, C1's 8-bit ones among them, which would move the cursor off the row
# (a tab is spaced by `fit`).
CONTROLS = "[\\x00-\\x08\\x0b-\\x1f\\x7f-\\x9f]"

# The tasks whose screen stays up after their build: the others go on to
# run something that needs the terminal.
# The tasks the screen is for, taken from their start; any other keeps the
# console.
_KINDS = ["build", "test", "lint", "gazelle", "format"]

# The tasks whose finished screen keeps their last build's panes open; the
# others keep their builds folded to a row each.
_ONE_BUILD_KINDS = ["build", "test"]

# Folded builds shown above the tabs, the older counted on a row.
_MAX_FOLDED = 3

# Errors repeated in the scrollback once the screen goes.
_MAX_ERRORS = 20

# Test statuses that are failures; `no_status`, `incomplete` and the like are
# not.
_FAILED_TEST_STATUSES = ["failed", "timeout", "failed_to_build", "remote_failure", "tool_halted_before_testing"]

# The Aborted reasons a target counts as failed for: the rest (skipped, an
# interrupt) are skipped.
_FAILED_ABORTS = ["analysis_failure", "loading_failure"]

_SPINNER = "⠋⠙⠹⠸⠼⠴⠦⠧⠇⠏"

# Alt (Option) with an arrow scrolls half a page, not an Esc and the arrow.
_ALT_SCROLL = ["alt-up", "alt-down"]

# The most keys a frame applies before it draws, so the screen keeps up
# with a burst of wheel or drag reports.
_MAX_KEYS = 512

# How many lines a turn of the mouse wheel scrolls.
_WHEEL = 3

# How long a finished screen with nothing failed stays up (`stay =
# "failure"`), counting down.
_COUNTDOWN_MS = 10000

# How old, in seconds, a running action is coloured slow, and stuck.
_SLOW_S = 60
_STUCK_S = 300

# The keys, for the `?` overlay.
# The code points a terminal draws two cells wide (East Asian Wide and
# Fullwidth, emoji among them) and none (combining marks, zero-width spaces,
# variation selectors), as `(first, last, cells)`, in order.
_CELLS = [
    (0x0300, 0x036F, 0),
    (0x1100, 0x115F, 2),
    (0x200B, 0x200F, 0),
    (0x20D0, 0x20FF, 0),
    (0x231A, 0x231B, 2),
    (0x2329, 0x232A, 2),
    (0x23E9, 0x23EC, 2),
    (0x23F0, 0x23F0, 2),
    (0x23F3, 0x23F3, 2),
    (0x25FD, 0x25FE, 2),
    (0x2614, 0x2615, 2),
    (0x2648, 0x2653, 2),
    (0x267F, 0x267F, 2),
    (0x2693, 0x2693, 2),
    (0x26A1, 0x26A1, 2),
    (0x26AA, 0x26AB, 2),
    (0x26BD, 0x26BE, 2),
    (0x26C4, 0x26C5, 2),
    (0x26CE, 0x26CE, 2),
    (0x26D4, 0x26D4, 2),
    (0x26EA, 0x26EA, 2),
    (0x26F2, 0x26F3, 2),
    (0x26F5, 0x26F5, 2),
    (0x26FA, 0x26FA, 2),
    (0x26FD, 0x26FD, 2),
    (0x2705, 0x2705, 2),
    (0x270A, 0x270B, 2),
    (0x2728, 0x2728, 2),
    (0x274C, 0x274C, 2),
    (0x274E, 0x274E, 2),
    (0x2753, 0x2755, 2),
    (0x2757, 0x2757, 2),
    (0x2795, 0x2797, 2),
    (0x27B0, 0x27B0, 2),
    (0x27BF, 0x27BF, 2),
    (0x2B1B, 0x2B1C, 2),
    (0x2B50, 0x2B50, 2),
    (0x2B55, 0x2B55, 2),
    (0x2E80, 0x303E, 2),
    (0x3041, 0x33FF, 2),
    (0x3400, 0x4DBF, 2),
    (0x4E00, 0x9FFF, 2),
    (0xA000, 0xA4CF, 2),
    (0xAC00, 0xD7A3, 2),
    (0xF900, 0xFAFF, 2),
    (0xFE00, 0xFE0F, 0),
    (0xFE20, 0xFE2F, 0),
    (0xFE30, 0xFE4F, 2),
    (0xFF00, 0xFF60, 2),
    (0xFFE0, 0xFFE6, 2),
    (0x16FE0, 0x18AFF, 2),
    (0x1B000, 0x1B2FF, 2),
    (0x1F004, 0x1F004, 2),
    (0x1F0CF, 0x1F0CF, 2),
    (0x1F18E, 0x1F18E, 2),
    (0x1F191, 0x1F19A, 2),
    (0x1F200, 0x1F251, 2),
    (0x1F300, 0x1F320, 2),
    (0x1F32D, 0x1F335, 2),
    (0x1F337, 0x1F37C, 2),
    (0x1F37E, 0x1F393, 2),
    (0x1F3A0, 0x1F3CA, 2),
    (0x1F3CF, 0x1F3D3, 2),
    (0x1F3E0, 0x1F3F0, 2),
    (0x1F3F4, 0x1F3F4, 2),
    (0x1F3F8, 0x1F43E, 2),
    (0x1F440, 0x1F440, 2),
    (0x1F442, 0x1F4FC, 2),
    (0x1F4FF, 0x1F53D, 2),
    (0x1F54B, 0x1F54E, 2),
    (0x1F550, 0x1F567, 2),
    (0x1F57A, 0x1F57A, 2),
    (0x1F595, 0x1F596, 2),
    (0x1F5A4, 0x1F5A4, 2),
    (0x1F5FB, 0x1F64F, 2),
    (0x1F680, 0x1F6C5, 2),
    (0x1F6CC, 0x1F6CC, 2),
    (0x1F6D0, 0x1F6D2, 2),
    (0x1F6D5, 0x1F6D7, 2),
    (0x1F6DC, 0x1F6DF, 2),
    (0x1F6EB, 0x1F6EC, 2),
    (0x1F6F4, 0x1F6FC, 2),
    (0x1F7E0, 0x1F7EB, 2),
    (0x1F7F0, 0x1F7F0, 2),
    (0x1F90C, 0x1F93A, 2),
    (0x1F93C, 0x1F945, 2),
    (0x1F947, 0x1F9FF, 2),
    (0x1FA70, 0x1FAFF, 2),
    (0x20000, 0x3FFFD, 2),
    (0xE0100, 0xE01EF, 0),
]

def _cells(c: int) -> int:
    """How many cells a terminal draws code point `c` in (see `_CELLS`)."""
    if c < 0x0300:
        return 1
    for first, last, cells in _CELLS:
        if c < first:
            return 1
        if c <= last:
            return cells
    return 1

def _widths(text: str) -> list:
    """Each code point of `text` and the cells it adds: as `_cells` has it,
    but for the emoji selector (U+FE0F), which widens a narrow character
    before it (`⚠️`) to two."""
    out = []
    last = 0
    for c in text.codepoints():
        n = 1 if c < 0x0300 else _cells(c)
        if c == 0xFE0F and last == 1:
            n = 1
        out.append((c, n))
        last = n if n else last
    return out

def text_width(text: str) -> int:
    """How many cells a terminal draws `text` in: a wide character (`✅`,
    `⚠️`) two, a combining mark none."""
    n = 0
    last = 0
    for c in text.codepoints():
        if c < 0x0300:
            n += 1
            last = 1
            continue
        cells = _cells(c)
        if c == 0xFE0F and last == 1:
            cells = 1
        n += cells
        last = cells if cells else last
    return n

def fit(text: str, width: int) -> str:
    """`text` cut or padded with spaces to `width` cells: a row wider than
    the screen would wrap and scroll the screen up. Only as much of a long
    line is measured as could show."""
    if width <= 0:
        return ""

    # A tab would move the cursor off the row.
    text = text.replace("\t", "  ")

    # Every code point but a zero-width one is a cell or more, so a line
    # longer than this is cut, but for one of zero-width marks, measured
    # whole.
    head = text[:2 * width + 2]
    cells = text_width(head)
    if len(head) < len(text) and cells <= width:
        head = text
        cells = text_width(text)
    if cells <= width and len(head) == len(text):
        return text + " " * (width - cells)
    room = width - 1 if width > 1 else width
    kept, used = [], 0
    for c, n in _widths(head):
        if used + n > room:
            break
        kept.append(chr(c))
        used += n
    if width > 1:
        kept.append("…")
        used += 1
    return "".join(kept) + " " * (width - used)

def progress_counts(status: str):
    """`(done, total)` from Bazel's status line (`[5,619 / 13,381] …`), or
    None when it has none."""
    status = bazel_line(status)
    if not status.startswith("[") or " / " not in status or "]" not in status:
        return None
    inner = status[1:status.index("]")]
    done, _, total = inner.partition(" / ")
    done = done.replace(",", "").strip()
    total = total.replace(",", "").strip()
    if not done.isdigit() or not total.isdigit():
        return None
    return (int(done), int(total))

def status_row(progress: list) -> int:
    """Where Bazel's `[done / total]` status line is in its progress area:
    first, or under its `Analyzing:` line while it analyses and runs actions
    at once; -1 for none."""
    for i in range(min(2, len(progress))):
        if bazel_line(progress[i]).startswith("["):
            return i
    return -1

def analysing(progress: list) -> bool:
    """Whether Bazel's progress area says it is still analysing, and so may
    find more actions: its `Analyzing:` line, above the status line once it
    runs actions too."""
    return any([bazel_line(r).startswith("Analyzing") for r in progress[:2]])

def running_actions(progress: list) -> list:
    """The running actions in Bazel's progress area: the rows under its
    status line (`status_row`), or, when there are none, the one action a
    status line names itself (`[n / m] Compiling a.cc; 3s …`), as Bazel
    draws a single action."""
    at = max(0, status_row(progress))
    rows = [r.strip() for r in progress[at + 1:] if r.strip()]
    if rows or not progress:
        return rows
    status = bazel_line(progress[at])
    if not status.startswith("[") or "] " not in status:
        return []
    rest = status.partition("] ")[2].strip()
    words = rest.split(" ")
    if not rest or rest.startswith("no action") or (len(words) > 1 and words[0].isdigit() and words[1].startswith("action")):
        return []
    return [rest]

def _count(word: str) -> int:
    """`word` as a count, its thousands separators (`1,204`) and a trailing
    comma dropped; -1 for a word that is none."""
    digits = word.rstrip(",").replace(",", "")
    return int(digits) if digits.isdigit() else -1

def action_counts(progress: list):
    """`(running, started)` from Bazel's status line: `[n / m] 300 actions
    running` (300 of each), or `[n / m] 400 actions, 300 running` with some
    started but not yet running (remote execution, a full local pool), each
    word perhaps followed by `,` or `;` (`… 15 running; last test: …`); -1 for
    what the line does not name. Bazel lists only `--ui_actions_shown` of the
    running ones under it, 8 by default."""
    at = status_row(progress)
    if at < 0 or "] " not in progress[at]:
        return (-1, -1)
    words = [w.rstrip(",;") for w in bazel_line(progress[at]).partition("] ")[2].split()]
    running, started = -1, -1
    for i in range(len(words) - 1):
        n = _count(words[i])
        if n < 0:
            continue
        after = words[i + 1]
        if after.startswith("action"):
            started = n
            if i + 2 < len(words) and words[i + 2] == "running":
                running = n
        elif after == "running":
            running = n
    return (running, started)

def _total(ns: list) -> int:
    """The sum of `ns`."""
    whole = 0
    for n in ns:
        whole += n
    return whole

def shares(counts: list, width: int) -> list:
    """`width` cells shared out to `counts` in proportion, the cells
    flooring leaves over going to the largest remainders, and every count
    there is any of given a cell, from the widest, while it can spare one."""
    whole = _total(counts)
    if whole <= 0 or width <= 0:
        return [0 for _ in counts]
    cells = [n * width // whole for n in counts]
    left = width - _total(cells)
    order = sorted([i for i in range(len(counts)) if counts[i]], key = lambda i: -(counts[i] * width % whole))
    for i in order[:left]:
        cells[i] += 1
    for i in range(len(counts)):
        if counts[i] and not cells[i]:
            widest = 0
            for j in range(len(cells)):
                if cells[j] > cells[widest]:
                    widest = j
            if cells[widest] > 1:
                cells[widest] -= 1
                cells[i] = 1
    return cells

# What `e` cycles the console through, in order.
_FILTERS = ["all", "warnings", "errors"]

_FILTER_TITLES = {"all": "", "warnings": " (errors and warnings)", "errors": " (errors only)"}

def new_search() -> dict:
    """The search bar's state: closed, empty, smart case, jumping to matches
    (`mode`, or `filter` to show only them). `origin` is where the console
    was scrolled when the bar opened, `anchored` when that was not following
    the newest line (scrolled up, or held on a match), and `back` the current
    match, as lines from the newest shown (-1 for none): both counted from
    the end, so new lines arriving keep them on the same line."""
    return {"open": False, "query": "", "case": False, "word": False, "pattern": None, "error": "", "mode": "jump", "origin": 0, "anchored": False, "back": -1}

def has_capital(query: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `query` has a capital letter of its own: not one naming an
    escape (`\\W`, `\\pL`, `\\p{Lu}`), a hex digit of one (`\\x4A`,
    `\\u{00C9}`, `\\U0001F600`), a flag (`(?U)`, `(?iR:…)`) or a group
    (`(?P<Name>…)`, `(?<Name>…)`)."""
    at = ""
    end = ""
    need = 0
    full = 0
    recent = ""
    for c in query.elems():
        prev = recent
        recent = (recent + c)[-3:]
        if at == "skip":
            if c == end:
                at = ""
            continue
        if at == "flags":
            if c in "imsxURu-":
                continue
            at = ""
            if c in "):":
                continue
        if at == "hex":
            if c == "{" and need == full:
                at = "skip"
                end = "}"
                continue
            if need > 0 and c in "0123456789abcdefABCDEF":
                need -= 1
                if need == 0:
                    at = ""
                continue
            at = ""
        if at == "escape":
            # An escaped character opens nothing: `\\(?U` is no flag group.
            at = ""
            recent = ""
            if c in ("p", "P"):
                at = "class"
            elif c in ("x", "u", "U"):
                at = "hex"
                need = {"x": 2, "u": 4, "U": 8}[c]
                full = need
            continue
        if at == "class":
            at = ""
            if c == "{":
                at = "skip"
                end = "}"
            continue
        if c == "\\":
            at = "escape"
            continue
        if prev.endswith("(?") or prev.endswith("(?P"):
            if c == "<":
                at = "skip"
                end = ">"
                continue
            if c == "P" and prev.endswith("(?"):
                continue
            if c in "imsxURu-" and prev.endswith("(?"):
                at = "flags"
                continue
        if c != c.lower():
            return True
    return False

def case_sensitive(search: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether the search matches case: when Ctrl+T says so, else, smart case
    as in fzf and ripgrep, when its query has a capital (`has_capital`)."""
    return search["case"] or has_capital(search["query"])

def _search_update(state: dict):
    """Recompile the search's pattern from its query and toggles. A query
    that does not compile (one still being typed) keeps the last pattern that
    did, its error shown in the bar."""
    search = state["search"]
    if not search["query"]:
        search["pattern"] = None
        search["error"] = ""
        return
    case = case_sensitive(search)
    error = state["re"].check(search["query"], case_sensitive = case, whole_word = search["word"])
    if error != None:
        search["error"] = error
        return
    search["error"] = ""
    search["pattern"] = state["re"].compile(search["query"], case_sensitive = case, whole_word = search["word"])

def _search_key(state: dict, key: str, page: int) -> bool:
    """Apply `key` to an open search bar; False for a key it leaves to the
    console (the arrows and the wheel, which scroll). A change finds anew:
    jumping, the newest match at or above where the bar opened (`_find`);
    filtering, the newest line, or with no query where the bar opened."""
    search = state["search"]
    if key == "enter":
        search["open"] = False
        return True
    if key == "esc":
        state["search"] = new_search()
        state["scroll"] = search.get("origin_rows", search["origin"]) if folding(state) else search["origin"]
        return True
    if key == "tab":
        search["mode"] = "filter" if search["mode"] == "jump" else "jump"
        search["back"] = -1
    elif key in ("ctrl-t", "alt-c"):
        search["case"] = not search["case"]
    elif key in ("ctrl-b", "alt-w"):
        search["word"] = not search["word"]
    elif key in ("ctrl-w", "alt-backspace"):
        # The last word, as readline deletes it: the spaces after it too.
        search["query"] = search["query"].rstrip(" ").rpartition(" ")[0] + (" " if " " in search["query"].rstrip(" ") else "")
    elif key == "ctrl-u":
        search["query"] = ""
    elif key == "backspace":
        search["query"] = search["query"][:-1]
    elif key.startswith("paste:"):
        search["query"] += paste_text(key)
    elif len(key) == 1:
        search["query"] += key
    else:
        return key not in ("up", "down", "shift-up", "shift-down", "wheel-up", "wheel-down")
    _search_update(state)
    if search["mode"] == "jump":
        _find(state, page)
    else:
        # Filtering shows the newest match; with no query, where it opened.
        state["scroll"] = 0 if search["pattern"] != None else _origin_scroll(state)
    return True

def shown_lines(state: dict) -> list:
    """What the console pane lists: a log's lines or the console's, through
    the `e` filter and a filtering search (`console_lines`)."""
    view = state["view"]
    if view != None:
        return console_lines(state, view["lines"])
    if folding(state) and state.get("fold_seen", 0) == len(state.get("log", [])) and state.get("folded"):
        # The folded view, when it is of the lines kept (`add_lines`).
        return state["folded"]
    log, kinds = state.get("log", []), state.get("kinds")
    early = state.get("early")
    if early and state["filter"] != "all" and kinds != None:
        # Errors and warnings from before `_MAX_LOG`, kept for `e`.
        log = [line for line, _ in early] + log
        kinds = [kind for _, kind in early] + kinds
    return console_lines(state, log, kinds)

def _match_from(pattern, lines: list, start: int, step: int) -> int:
    """The first of `lines` from `start` on, going `step` (-1 older, 1
    newer), that `pattern` matches; -1 for none."""
    i = start
    for _ in range(len(lines)):
        if i < 0 or i >= len(lines):
            break
        if pattern.search(lines[i]):
            return i
        i += step
    return -1

def _show_match(state: dict, lines: list, i: int, page: int):
    """Make line `i` of `lines` the current match, scrolled to the middle of
    a console `page` lines high."""
    state["search"]["back"] = len(lines) - 1 - i
    state["scroll"] = max(0, len(lines) - i - 1 - (page - 1) // 2)

    # Wrapped, `render` centres it in rows on its next frame, once.
    state["search"]["centre"] = True

def _origin_scroll(state: dict) -> int:
    """Where the bar opened, as the view now scrolls: in folded rows when it
    is folded (no query shown), else in lines."""
    search = state["search"]
    return search.get("origin_rows", search["origin"]) if folding(state) else search["origin"]

def _find(state: dict, page: int):
    """Jump to the newest match at or above the bottom of the console as the
    bar found it (its `origin`), else the newest of all; with none, or no
    query, back to that `origin`. A query that does not compile moves
    nothing."""
    search = state["search"]
    if search["error"]:
        return
    lines = shown_lines(state)
    at = -1
    if search["pattern"] != None:
        at = _match_from(search["pattern"], lines, len(lines) - 1 - search["origin"], -1)
        if at < 0:
            at = _match_from(search["pattern"], lines, len(lines) - 1, -1)
    if at < 0:
        search["back"] = -1
        state["scroll"] = _origin_scroll(state)
        return
    _show_match(state, lines, at, page)

def step_match(state: dict, step: int, page: int):
    """`N` (`step` -1) and `n` (1): the next older or newer match from the
    current one, or from the bottom of the console without one; past the
    newest or the oldest, round to the other end, the keys row saying so."""
    search = state["search"]
    if search["pattern"] == None or search["mode"] != "jump":
        return
    lines = shown_lines(state)
    back = search["back"]
    if back >= 0 and back < len(lines):
        start = len(lines) - 1 - back + step
    else:
        # The bottom of the console as `render` draws it, a scroll past the
        # top (`K`) at the top page.
        bottom = max(min(len(lines) - state["scroll"], len(lines)), min(len(lines), page)) - 1
        start = bottom + (1 if step > 0 else 0)
    at = _match_from(search["pattern"], lines, start, step)
    if at < 0:
        at = _match_from(search["pattern"], lines, 0 if step > 0 else len(lines) - 1, step)
        if at >= 0:
            say_note(state, "wrapped round to the oldest match" if step > 0 else "wrapped round to the newest match")
    if at >= 0:
        _show_match(state, lines, at, page)

def paste_text(key: str) -> str:
    """A `paste:` key's text for the search bar: on one line, without control
    characters, which would move the cursor or count as columns they are not."""
    out = []
    for c in key[len("paste:"):].elems():
        if c in "\r\n\t":
            out.append(" ")
        elif c >= " " and not ("\x7f" <= c and c <= "\x9f"):
            out.append(c)
    return "".join(out)

def failure_of(kind: str, event_id, payload):
    """A record for the Failures list from a build event, or None: a failed
    action (`action_completed`, which Bazel sends for every failure) with its
    stderr and stdout, or a test attempt that did not pass (`test_result`;
    flaky ones did not) with its `test.log`. Each log is a BES `File.file`: a
    URI, or the contents inline."""
    label = repaired(str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or ""))
    if kind == "action_completed":
        if _get(payload, "success", False):
            return None
        return _action_record(label, payload, event_id)
    if kind == "test_result":
        if str(_get(payload, "status", "") or "").lower() not in _FAILED_TEST_STATUSES:
            return None
        attempt = _get(event_id, "attempt", 1) or 1
        return {
            "kind": "test",
            "label": label,
            "detail": "attempt %d" % attempt,
            "run": _get(event_id, "run", 1) or 1,
            "shard": _get(event_id, "shard", 1) or 1,
            "attempt": attempt,
            "config": str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""),
            "status": str(_get(payload, "status", "") or "").lower(),
            "duration_ms": _attempt_ms(payload),
            "files": [("test.log", bounded_log(_get(f, "file", None))) for f in (_get(payload, "test_action_output", None) or []) if str(_get(f, "name", "") or "") == "test.log"],
        }
    return None

def _attempt_ms(payload) -> int:
    """How long a test attempt ran, in milliseconds."""
    ms = _get(payload, "test_attempt_duration_millis", 0) or 0
    if ms:
        return ms
    d = _get(payload, "test_attempt_duration", None)
    return (_get(d, "seconds", 0) or 0) * 1000 + (_get(d, "nanos", 0) or 0) // 1000000 if d != None else 0

def _action_record(label: str, payload, event_id = None) -> dict:
    """An action's record for a list: its label, mnemonic, logs, primary
    output (which the history keeps its logs by), whether it succeeded and
    what its command exited with (`spawn_exit_code`) and what Bazel's
    `failure_detail` says of it (`failure`, decoded, `repaired`)."""
    files = []
    for name in ("stderr", "stdout"):
        f = _get(payload, name, None)
        if f != None:
            files.append((name, bounded_log(_get(f, "file", None))))
    return {
        "kind": "action",
        "label": repaired(label),
        "detail": str(_get(payload, "type", "") or "") or "Action",
        "failure": repaired(str(_get(_get(payload, "failure_detail", None), "message", "") or "")),
        "files": files,
        "output": str(_get(event_id, "primary_output", "") or ""),
        "config": str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""),
        "success": bool(_get(payload, "success", False)),
        "exit_code": spawn_exit_code(payload),
        "counted_internal": counted_internal(payload),
    }

def spawn_exit_code(payload) -> int:
    """What a failed action's command exited with (its `failure_detail`'s
    `spawn_exit_code`, as `(Exit 3)` says), else 0. The event's own
    `exit_code` is 1 for every failed spawn."""
    category = _get(_get(payload, "failure_detail", None), "category", None)
    return _get(category, "spawn_exit_code", 0) or 0

def counted_internal(payload):
    """Whether Bazel counts a failed action among `runner_count`'s internal
    ones: every failure but one whose outputs were not created
    (`failure_detail`'s `ACTION_OUTPUTS_NOT_CREATED`), which it counts
    under the runner its spawn ran in; None when it said nothing."""
    category = _get(_get(payload, "failure_detail", None), "category", None)
    if category == None:
        return None
    return str(_get(category, "code", "") or "").upper() != "ACTION_OUTPUTS_NOT_CREATED"

def output_of(kind: str, event_id, payload):
    """A record for the actions list from a build event, or None: an action
    that succeeded and wrote a stdout or stderr, which Bazel reports only
    with `--build_event_publish_all_actions` (`actions = "all"`)."""
    if kind != "action_completed" or not _get(payload, "success", False):
        return None
    record = _action_record(str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or ""), payload, event_id)
    return record if record["files"] else None

def cut_text(text: str) -> str:
    """An inline log's end, cut anywhere, the byte before the cut first (see
    `bounded_log`), started where `std.fs.try_read_tail` starts a file's: at
    the cut when it falls at a line's start, else the next line when one
    follows, else the first whole character."""
    if text.startswith("\n"):
        return text[1:]
    head, newline, rest = text.partition("\n")
    return rest if newline and rest else text[1:].lstrip("\ufffd")

def bounded_log(value, cap = _MAX_LOG_BYTES):
    """A log's BES `File.file`: a URI as it is, or inline contents as
    `(bytes, cut)`, cut to their last `cap` bytes and the one before (to see
    whether the cut falls at a line's start, as `cut_text` reads it), so the
    rest of a failed action's tens of MB never stays on the heap."""
    if type(value) == "bytes" and len(value) > cap:
        return (value[-(cap + 1):], True)
    if type(value) == "bytes":
        return (value, False)
    return value

def local_test_logs(state: dict, record: dict) -> list:
    """Where Bazel's output tree has a test attempt's `test.log`, for one a
    remote cache named by `bytestream://`: `<exec root>/bazel-out/<output
    dir>/testlogs/[external/<repo>/]<package>/<name>/[shard_<s>_of_<n>][_]
    [run_<r>_of_<m>]/`, `test_attempts/attempt_<a>.log` for an attempt
    Bazel retried, `test.log` for its last. Both the folder (its shard and
    run counts) and which attempt was last come from the test's summary,
    sent after its last attempt: before it, no path, rather than a guess
    that could name an older build's log."""
    root = state["exec_root"]
    out = state["output_dirs"].get(record["config"], "")
    label = record["label"].lstrip("@")
    key = (record["label"], record["config"])
    if not root or not out or key not in state["test_counts"] or "//" not in label or ":" not in label.partition("//")[2]:
        return []
    repo, _, rest = label.partition("//")
    package, _, name = rest.partition(":")
    path = root + "/bazel-out/" + out + "/testlogs" + ("/external/" + repo if repo else "")
    for part in (package + "/" + name).split("/"):
        if part:
            path += "/" + part
    runs, shards = state["test_counts"][key]
    run, shard, attempt = record["run"], record["shard"], record["attempt"]
    sub = (["shard_%d_of_%d" % (shard, shards)] if shards > 1 else []) + (["run_%d_of_%d" % (run, runs)] if runs > 1 else [])
    if sub:
        path += "/" + "_".join(sub)
    last = state["last_attempts"].get((record["label"], record["config"], run, shard), attempt)
    return [path + ("/test_attempts/attempt_%d.log" % attempt if attempt < last else "/test.log")]

def log_paths(state: dict, record: dict, uri) -> list:
    """Where a log named by `uri` may be on disk, likeliest first; a
    `file://` one's path alone before the screen has `std.env`, decoded
    (`percent_decoded`, then `repaired` too)."""
    if type(uri) != "string" or not uri:
        return []
    paths = []
    if uri.startswith("bytestream://") and record["kind"] == "test":
        paths.extend(local_test_logs(state, record))
    if state.get("env") != None:
        paths.append(file_uri_to_path(state["env"], uri))
    elif uri.startswith("file://"):
        paths.append(uri[len("file://"):])

    # A `file://` path is percent-encoded, a non-ASCII name's UTF-8 in it
    # spelled as Latin-1 (`Ñ%C2%82` for `т`), and the file on disk is named
    # that way or as meant, by Bazel version: both, as written first.
    out = []
    for p in paths:
        if p and uri.startswith("file://"):
            decoded = percent_decoded(p)
            for candidate in (decoded, repaired(decoded)):
                if candidate not in out:
                    out.append(candidate)
        elif p and p not in out:
            out.append(p)
    return out

def log_view(state: dict, record: dict) -> dict:
    """The view of a failure's logs: its title, the lines (the end of each
    log, escape sequences stripped, a divider between an action's stderr and
    stdout) and whether any was cut. The logs share `_MAX_VIEW` lines (`fit_logs`).
    An action's log is read from the history's copy (`kept_action_log()`,
    kept as it failed) before Bazel's own file, which Bazel replaces as the
    build goes on: a log gone from both says so, and how to keep one when
    the history keeps no action logs."""
    blocks = []
    cut = False
    build = state.get("build")
    copies = build.kept_action_log(record.get("output", "")) if build != None and record["kind"] == "action" else None
    keeping = copies != None
    copies = copies or {}
    for name, value in record["files"]:
        text = None
        if type(value) == "tuple":
            contents, inline_cut = value
            text = str(contents)
            cut = cut or inline_cut
            if inline_cut:
                text = cut_text(text)
        for path in ([copies[name]] if name in copies else []) + log_paths(state, record, value):
            got = state["read"](path, _MAX_LOG_BYTES)
            if got != None:
                text, skipped = got
                cut = cut or skipped > 0
                break
        if text == None:
            pending = record["kind"] == "test" and (record["label"], record["config"]) not in state["test_counts"]
            remote = type(value) == "string" and value.startswith("bytestream://")
            if pending and remote:
                blocks.append((name, ["(%s is found once the test has finished: a remote cache names it)" % name]))
            elif remote:
                blocks.append((name, ["(%s is not on this machine: only in the remote cache)" % name]))
            elif record["kind"] == "action" and not keeping:
                blocks.append((name, ["(%s is gone: Bazel removed it, and the history keeps no action logs; --history:action-logs=failed keeps a copy)" % name]))
            elif record["kind"] == "action" and record.get("success"):
                blocks.append((name, ["(%s is gone: Bazel removed it; the history keeps a successful action's only with --history:action-logs=all)" % name]))
            elif record["kind"] == "action":
                blocks.append((name, ["(%s is gone: Bazel removed it, and the history has no copy)" % name]))
            else:
                blocks.append((name, ["(%s is gone: Bazel removed it)" % name]))
            continue
        if not text.strip():
            blocks.append((name, ["(empty)"]))
            continue

        # A line's last carriage return wins, as on a terminal (a progress
        # bar redrawing itself, a CRLF line end); no control is left to move
        # the cursor off its row.
        own = []
        for line in state["ansi"].replace(text, "").rstrip("\n").split("\n"):
            line = line.rstrip("\r").rpartition("\r")[2]
            own.append(state["controls"].replace(line, ""))
        blocks.append((name, own))
    kept = fit_logs([own for _, own in blocks], _MAX_VIEW)
    lines = []
    for (name, own), keep in zip(blocks, kept):
        if len(blocks) > 1:
            lines.append("── " + name + " ──")
        if keep < len(own):
            cut = True
        lines.extend(own[len(own) - keep:])
    if not record["files"]:
        lines.append("(Bazel reported no log for it)")
    return {"title": record["label"] + " · " + failure_detail(state, record), "lines": lines, "cut": cut, "record": record}

def failure_detail(state: dict, record: dict, flaky_note: bool = True) -> str:
    """What tells a failure apart from others of its label: an action's
    mnemonic; a test attempt's number, after its shard and run when the
    test has (its summary's counts) or has been seen to have (`spread`, the
    highest of each its results named) more than one, and that the test was
    flaky, but where `flaky_note` is false (under a heading that says so)."""
    if record["kind"] != "test":
        return record["detail"]
    detail = record["detail"]
    if record.get("status") == "timeout":
        ms = record.get("duration_ms", 0)
        detail += " · timeout" + (" after %d.%ds" % (ms // 1000, ms % 1000 // 100) if ms else "")
    if flaky_note and state.get("counts", {}).get("flaky", {}).get(record["label"]):
        detail += " · flaky, passed on a later attempt"
    key = (record["label"], record["config"])
    runs, shards = state["test_counts"].get(key, (0, 0))
    seen_runs, seen_shards = state["spread"].get(key, (1, 1))
    parts = []
    if max(shards, seen_shards) > 1:
        parts.append("shard %d" % record["shard"])
    if max(runs, seen_runs) > 1:
        parts.append("run %d" % record["run"])
    return " · ".join(parts + [detail])

def fit_logs(logs: list, room: int) -> list:
    """How many of each log's last lines to keep for `room` lines in all:
    every log whole when they fit, else each over a fair share cut to it,
    the share of a log under it passed to the others."""
    keep = [len(log) for log in logs]
    total = 0
    for n in keep:
        total += n
    if total <= room:
        return keep
    left = room
    order = sorted(range(len(logs)), key = lambda i: keep[i])
    for n, i in enumerate(order):
        share = left // (len(order) - n)
        keep[i] = min(keep[i], share)
        left -= keep[i]
    return keep

def attempt_state(now_ms: int) -> dict:
    """What a Bazel attempt starts with: its own counts, console, failures
    and actions, nothing open, timed from `now_ms`."""
    return dict(
        fresh = False,
        phase = "Starting Bazel…",
        fails = [],
        outputs = [],
        selected = 0,
        picked = 0,
        cache = None,
        unseen = 0,
        focus = "console",
        view = None,
        last_attempts = {},
        spread = {},
        test_counts = {},
        counts = new_counts(),
        errors = [],
        error_count = 0,
        log = [],
        log_size = 0,
        views = [],
        parked = {},
        seeded = 0,
        seed_trimmed = 0,
        inv = {},
        tab_scroll = 0,
        hover = None,
        select = None,
        kinds = [],
        block = "",
        closing = [],
        closing_tail = [],
        closing_cut = 0,
        early = [],
        dropped = 0,
        unseen_errors = 0,
        slowest = {},
        critical = "",
        spawn_slowest = [],
        folded = [],
        fold_src = [],
        fold_head = [],
        fold_open = {},
        log_base = 0,
        fold_n = -1,
        fold_seen = 0,
        scroll = 0,
        start_ms = now_ms,
        bazel_uuid = "",
        exec_log_read = False,
        done_total = None,
        bep_done_total = None,
        settled = 0,
        excerpts = {},
    )

def reset_attempt(state: dict, now_ms: int):
    """Start a retried attempt afresh: its own counts, console, failures and
    actions, no log open nor the list focused, timed from `now_ms`. The
    search (but its current match and where it opened), the `e` filter, the
    list shown, zoom and the mouse are the user's, kept."""
    state["search"]["back"] = -1
    state["search"]["origin"] = 0
    state["search"]["anchored"] = False
    state.update(attempt_state(now_ms))
    _clear_build(state)

def _clear_build(state: dict):
    """What a build keeps beside `attempt_state`'s, which the next build and a
    retried attempt alike start without: the root causes it noted."""
    state["root_causes"] = {}
    for key in ("blocked_cache", "entries_cache", "own_cache", "ties_cache"):
        state[key] = None

# What a build has of its own, which the next build (or a retried attempt)
# starts afresh; the console and what is open in it are the task's.
_PER_BUILD = ["fails", "outputs", "selected", "picked", "cache", "last_attempts", "spread", "test_counts", "counts", "inv", "closing", "closing_tail", "closing_cut", "slowest", "spawn_slowest", "critical", "bazel_uuid", "exec_log_read", "done_total", "bep_done_total", "settled", "excerpts"]

def new_build(state: dict, now_ms: int):
    """Start the next build (or a retried attempt) in the task's screen: its
    own counts, failures and invocation, timed from `now_ms`; the console,
    the search and what is open in it kept. The Failures list, if focused,
    goes back to the console, as its rows are the old build's."""
    fresh = attempt_state(now_ms)
    for key in _PER_BUILD:
        state[key] = fresh[key]
    _clear_build(state)
    state["progress"] = []
    state["fresh"] = False
    state["build_failed"] = False
    state["build_start_ms"] = now_ms
    state["picked_build"] = None
    if state.get("focus") == "failures":
        state["focus"] = "console"

def note_event(state: dict, kind: str, event_id, payload, aborted: bool):
    """Gather from one build event Bazel's last count (a `progress` one's
    stderr, `bep_progress`), and what the Failures list needs: a failure's
    record (`failure_of`), and what finding a test's log in the output tree
    takes (the exec root, each configuration's output folder, each test's
    run and shard counts, each run and shard's last attempt, and the most
    runs and shards it has been seen to have, `spread`). A summary
    the build announced but did not get to (`aborted`, an interrupt) has no
    counts. A test's summary arriving shows its open log anew, now
    findable, from its end. An Aborted event of a root cause is a target's
    failure record (`kind` `target`), once per label, its `description`
    Bazel's (decoded, `repaired`): one that failed analysis (a
    `configured_label`, or a target's own with a description) or loading
    (an `unconfigured_label`, a pattern with a description)."""
    if kind == "progress":
        counts = bep_progress(str(_get(payload, "stderr", "") or ""))
        if counts:
            state["bep_done_total"] = counts
    if aborted:
        reason = str(_get(payload, "reason", "") or "").lower()
        pattern = kind == "pattern_expanded"
        label = " ".join([str(p) for p in (_get(event_id, "pattern", None) or [])]) if pattern else repaired(str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or ""))
        description = repaired(str(_get(payload, "description", "") or ""))
        failing = reason in _FAILED_ABORTS
        detail = None
        if kind == "unconfigured_label":
            detail = "loading"
        elif kind == "configured_label" and failing:
            detail = "analysis"
        elif pattern and failing and description:
            detail = "loading"
        elif kind in ("target_configured", "target_completed") and failing and description and not _get(event_id, "aspect", ""):
            detail = "analysis"
        if detail != None and label and not any([r["kind"] == "target" and r["label"] == label for r in state["fails"]]):
            _add_failure(state, {
                "kind": "target",
                "label": label,
                "detail": detail,
                "description": description,
                "pattern": pattern,
                "files": [("error", (description, False))] if description else [],
                "output": "",
                "config": str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""),
                "success": False,
            })
        return
    if kind == "workspace_config":
        state["exec_root"] = str(_get(payload, "local_exec_root", "") or "")
    elif kind == "configuration":
        state["output_dirs"][str(_get(event_id, "id", "") or "")] = str(_get(payload, "mnemonic", "") or "")
    elif kind == "test_summary":
        key = (repaired(str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or "")), str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""))
        state["test_counts"][key] = (_get(payload, "run_count", 0) or 0, _get(payload, "shard_count", 0) or 0)
        view = state["view"]
        if view != None and view.get("record") != None and view["record"]["kind"] == "test" and (view["record"]["label"], view["record"]["config"]) == key:
            state["view"] = log_view(state, view["record"])
            state["scroll"] = 0
            state["search"]["back"] = -1
    elif kind == "test_result":
        key = (
            repaired(str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or "")),
            str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""),
            _get(event_id, "run", 1) or 1,
            _get(event_id, "shard", 1) or 1,
        )
        state["last_attempts"][key] = max(state["last_attempts"].get(key, 0), _get(event_id, "attempt", 1) or 1)
        runs, shards = state["spread"].get(key[:2], (1, 1))
        state["spread"][key[:2]] = (max(runs, key[2]), max(shards, key[3]))
    record = failure_of(kind, event_id, payload)
    if record != None:
        _add_failure(state, record)
    record = output_of(kind, event_id, payload)
    if record != None:
        state["outputs"].append(record)

def collect_event(state: dict, event):
    """Gather one build event into `state` as the failure summary and the
    Failures list read it, the same in the plain console, the Dashboard and
    the replay test: the invocation's own events (`_INVOCATION_KINDS`), the
    counts (`count_event`), the failures (`note_event`) and the root
    causes (`note_blocked`). An event is Aborted when its payload is (the
    replay test's events say so as `payload_kind`)."""
    kind = event.kind
    event_id, payload = _get(event, "id"), _get(event, "payload")
    aborted = (_get(event, "payload_kind") or type(payload)) == "aborted"
    if kind in _INVOCATION_KINDS:
        state["inv"][kind] = payload
    count_event(state["counts"], kind, event_id, payload, aborted)
    note_event(state, kind, event_id, payload, aborted)
    note_blocked(state, event)

def _test_key(record: dict):
    """Which attempt of which test a record is: one Bazel posts twice is one."""
    return (record["label"], record["config"], record["run"], record["shard"], record["attempt"])

def _test_runner(record: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether `record` is a test's own failed action (`TestRunner`)."""
    return record["kind"] == "action" and record["detail"] == "TestRunner"

def _testlogs_dir(label: str):
    """`<package>/<name>` under `testlogs/` for a test's label, with an
    external repository's `external/<repo>/` first; None for a label it
    can't read."""
    label = label.lstrip("@")
    if "//" not in label or ":" not in label.partition("//")[2]:
        return None
    repo, _, rest = label.partition("//")
    package, _, name = rest.partition(":")
    parts = (["external", repo] if repo else []) + [p for p in package.split("/") if p] + [name]
    return "/".join(parts)

def _runner_place(record: dict):
    """The run and shard a `TestRunner` action ran, `(run, shard)`, from the
    one folder its output (always that run's `test.log`) has after
    `testlogs/<package>/<name>/`: `shard_<s>_of_<n>` and `run_<r>_of_<m>`,
    either or both (`shard_1_of_2_run_2_of_2`), 1 for one not named; None
    where its output is no such test log."""
    own = _testlogs_dir(record["label"])
    marker = "/testlogs/" + own + "/" if own else None
    out = "/" + record.get("output", "")
    if marker == None or marker not in out:
        return None
    rest = out.partition(marker)[2].split("/")
    if rest[-1] != "test.log" or len(rest) > 2:
        return None
    words = rest[0].split("_") if len(rest) == 2 else []

    def number(word):
        for i in range(len(words) - 3):
            if words[i] == word and words[i + 1].isdigit() and words[i + 2] == "of" and words[i + 3].isdigit():
                return int(words[i + 1])
        return 1

    return (number("run"), number("shard"))

def _runner_attempt(state: dict, record: dict):
    """The attempt a `TestRunner` action ran, `(label, config, run, shard,
    attempt)`:
    its run and shard from its output (`_runner_place`), and, as Bazel
    sends an attempt's action before its result, the attempt after the
    last whose result came; None where its output names no test log."""
    place = _runner_place(record)
    if place == None:
        return None
    run, shard = place
    config = record.get("config", "")
    seen = [n for key, n in state.get("last_attempts", {}).items() if key == (record["label"], config, run, shard)]
    return (record["label"], config, run, shard, max(seen + [0]) + 1)

def _same_attempt(runner: dict, test: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether a `TestRunner` action failure is `test`'s attempt."""
    at = runner.get("attempt_key")
    return at != None and at == (test["label"], test["config"], test["run"], test["shard"], test["attempt"])

def _add_failure(state: dict, record: dict):
    """List a failure once. A test attempt Bazel posts again (Bazel 9 posts
    attempt 1 failed with its log, then failed to build without one) is one
    row, the one with a log kept. A failed `TestRunner` action is listed as
    it comes, with the attempt it ran (`_runner_attempt`), and dropped once
    that attempt's own record comes with a log; one whose attempt has none
    (an attempt Bazel cut off, a shard that failed to build) stays, its log
    the only one, and that record is not listed beside it."""
    fails = state["fails"]
    if _test_runner(record):
        record["attempt_key"] = _runner_attempt(state, record)
        fails.append(record)
        return
    if record["kind"] == "test":
        if not record["files"] and any([_test_runner(r) and _same_attempt(r, record) for r in fails]):
            return
        key = _test_key(record)
        for i, r in enumerate(fails):
            if r["kind"] == "test" and _test_key(r) == key:
                if record["files"] and not r["files"]:
                    fails[i] = record
                return
        if record["files"] and any([_test_runner(r) and _same_attempt(r, record) for r in fails]):
            fails = [r for r in fails if not (_test_runner(r) and _same_attempt(r, record))]
            state["fails"] = fails
            state["selected"] = min(state.get("selected", 0), max(0, len(fails)))
    fails.append(record)

def _items(state: dict) -> list:
    """The records of the list the Results pane shows: the failures, or,
    after `a`, the actions that wrote output."""
    return state.get("outputs", []) if state.get("list") == "actions" else state["fails"]

def _pick(state: dict) -> str:
    """The key of `state` that holds the chosen row of the list shown: each
    list keeps its own."""
    return "picked" if state.get("list") == "actions" else "selected"

def _keep_view(state: dict):
    """Before showing a view deeper in the shown tab's stack (a file opened
    from a log): keep the one shown, and where it was scrolled, for Esc and
    the breadcrumb to go back to."""
    view = state["view"]
    if view != None:
        back = state.setdefault("views", [])
        back.append((view, state["scroll"]))
        if len(back) > 32:
            state["views"] = back[-32:]

def _show_failure_view(state: dict, view: dict):
    """`view`, a failure's log, as the Failures tab's: the tab shown, its
    stack only this log (stepping is sideways, so a peek from the last one
    goes)."""
    show_tab(state, "failures")
    state["views"] = []
    state["view"] = view
    state["scroll"] = 0
    state["search"]["back"] = -1
    state["focus"] = "console"

def _open_failure(state: dict, index: int):
    """Show the record at `index` of the list shown in the console pane, at
    its end."""
    state[_pick(state)] = index
    _show_failure_view(state, log_view(state, _items(state)[index]))

def note_blocked(state: dict, event):
    """Keep a failed target's root causes (`event.children`, which Bazel
    gives every failed target, in its `target_completed` or, failed in
    analysis, its Aborted `target_configured`), itself among them where
    Bazel names it (an attribute error's target, one whose package is in
    error), for `blocked_targets` and `failure_entries` to decide from the
    build's failures as they end up, whatever came first. A target that
    failed with no failed action among its causes (a missing source file)
    is a failure record of its own (`kind` `target`, `detail` `target`),
    its `failure_detail`'s message its description."""
    kind = _get(event, "kind")
    payload = _get(event, "payload")
    event_id = _get(event, "id")
    if kind == "target_completed":
        if _get(payload, "success", False) or _get(event_id, "aspect", ""):
            return
    elif kind != "target_configured" or (_get(event, "payload_kind") or type(payload)) != "aborted":
        # A configured target's only child is its own completion; one
        # Aborted in analysis has its root causes.
        return
    label = repaired(str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or ""))
    roots = []
    acted = False
    for child in _get(event, "children") or []:
        child_id = _get(child, "id")
        if _get(child_id, "primary_output", ""):
            acted = True
        root = repaired(str(_get(child_id, "label", "") or ""))
        if root and root not in roots and (root != label or not _get(child_id, "primary_output", "")):
            roots.append(root)
    roots = [r for r in roots if r != label or len(roots) == 1]
    if label and roots:
        if state.get("root_causes") == None:
            state["root_causes"] = {}
        state["root_causes"][label] = roots
    message = repaired(str(_get(_get(payload, "failure_detail", None), "message", "") or ""))
    if kind == "target_completed" and label and message and not acted and not any([r["kind"] == "target" and r["label"] == label for r in state["fails"]]):
        _add_failure(state, {
            "kind": "target",
            "label": label,
            "detail": "target",
            "description": message,
            "pattern": False,
            "files": [("error", (message, False))],
            "output": "",
            "config": str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""),
            "success": False,
        })

def blocked_targets(state: dict) -> dict:
    """The targets not built for a failed dependency, by label, each with
    its root causes as Bazel names them (`event.children` of its failed
    `target_completed` or Aborted `target_configured`, `note_blocked`),
    itself left out: every target whose roots are others and that has no
    entry of its own (`_own_labels`). Cached until the root causes or the
    failures change."""
    causes = state.get("root_causes") or {}
    key = (len(causes), len(state["fails"]))
    cached = state.get("blocked_cache")
    if cached != None and cached[0] == key:
        return cached[1]
    own = _own_labels(state)
    blocked = {}
    for label, roots in causes.items():
        others = [r for r in roots if r != label]
        if others and label not in own:
            blocked[label] = others
    state["blocked_cache"] = (key, blocked)
    return blocked

def blocked_text(label: str, roots: list) -> str:
    """`blocked by :broken`: a root cause in `label`'s package by its name,
    another by its label, and how many more there are."""
    pkg = label.rpartition(":")[0]
    first = roots[0]
    shown = ":" + first.rpartition(":")[2] if first.rpartition(":")[0] == pkg else first
    more = len(roots) - 1
    return "blocked by " + shown + ((_counts(" and %d more", more)) if more else "")

def _failure_total(state: dict) -> int:
    """How many rows the Failures list has: its records, then the rows of
    no record of their own (`_tab_extras`)."""
    return len(state["fails"]) + len(_tab_extras(state))

def _open_unlogged(state: dict, i: int):
    """Enter on a row of no record of its own (`_tab_extras`): a blocked
    target shows what blocks it (its first root cause's record, chosen in
    the list); another shows what Bazel said of it."""
    extras = _tab_extras(state)
    k = i - len(state["fails"])
    if k < 0 or k >= len(extras):
        return
    what, item = extras[k]
    if what == "blocked":
        for root in blocked_targets(state).get(item, []):
            for j, rec in enumerate(state["fails"]):
                if rec["label"] == root:
                    _open_failure(state, j)
                    return
    state["selected"] = i
    if what == "blocked":
        lines = ["(" + blocked_text(item, blocked_targets(state).get(item, [])) + ", which has no log here)"]
        title = item
    elif what == "other":
        lines = item["lines"]
        title = "Other error from Bazel"
    else:
        said = entry_text(state, item)
        lines = said["lines"] if item.get("exited") != None else ["(" + _NO_REASON + ")"]
        title = item["label"] or said["kind"]
    _show_failure_view(state, {"title": title, "lines": lines, "cut": False, "record": None})

# The entries of the failure summary with their excerpt, the lines of each,
# and the rows a line wraps to before it is cut; a line's longest quote.
_EXCERPTED = 5
_EXCERPT_LINES = 5
_EXCERPT_ROWS = 3
_MAX_QUOTED_BYTES = 300

# Bazel's lines at the top of a test's log, before the test's own.
_TEST_LOG_PREAMBLE = ["exec ${PAGER", "Executing tests from", "-----"]

# An excerpt's row standing for lines left out between two it shows.
_GAP_ROW = "\x00gap"

def failure_rows(state: dict, room: int, width: int) -> list:
    """The Failures list's rows, `room` at most, each `width` wide: a row per
    failed action and per failed test (`listed_failures`: a test's chosen
    attempt stands for it), headed as the failure summary heads it (`✗
    //pkg:t  Genrule · exit 1 · pkg/BUILD:3:8`, `✗ //t:x  test · failed 2 of
    2 attempts`, `record_excerpt`), the selected one in reverse video while
    the list has focus and kept in sight with its excerpt under it behind a
    `│` (every row's with Ctrl+O, `expand_failures`); then the rows of no
    record of their own (`_tab_extras`): a failed target no event says
    more of, each blocked target (`○`), Bazel's other errors (`!`). What
    is hidden above and below is
    counted in rows of the list, an excerpt never cut from its row."""
    fails = state["fails"]
    listed = listed_failures(state)
    extras = _tab_extras(state)
    total = len(listed) + len(extras)
    if room <= 0 or total == 0:
        return []
    selected = state["selected"]
    at = _listed_position(state, listed, selected)
    expand_all = state.get("expand_failures", False)

    def block(pos):
        # The rows of the list's `pos`th entry: its row, its excerpt under
        # it when shown.
        if pos >= len(listed):
            return [unlogged_row(pos)]
        i = listed[pos]
        rows = [row(i, pos == at)]
        if expand_all or pos == at:
            rows.extend([_excerpt_row(line, width) for line in record_excerpt(state, i)[1]])
        return rows

    blocked = blocked_targets(state)

    def unlogged_row(pos):
        what, label = extras[pos - len(listed)]
        if what == "other":
            text = _middle("! " + label["lines"][0], width)
            if pos == at and state["focus"] == "failures":
                return _c("active") + fit(text, width) + _RESET
            return _c("warning") + "!" + _RESET + _c("muted") + text[1:] + _RESET
        if what == "entry":
            said = entry_text(state, label)
            text = _middle("✗ " + " · ".join(([label["label"]] if label["label"] else []) + ([said["kind"]] if said["kind"] else []) + said["details"]) + ("" if label.get("exited") != None else "  no log"), width)
            if pos == at and state["focus"] == "failures":
                return _c("active") + fit(text, width) + _RESET
            return _c("error") + "✗" + _RESET + text[1:]
        roots = blocked.get(label)
        text = _middle(("○ " + label + "  " + blocked_text(label, roots)) if roots else ("✗ " + label + "  no log"), width)
        if pos == at and state["focus"] == "failures":
            return _c("active") + fit(text, width) + _RESET
        return _c("muted") + text + _RESET if roots else _c("error") + "✗" + _RESET + text[1:]

    def row(i, chosen):
        rec = fails[i]
        flaky = rec["kind"] == "test" and state["counts"].get("flaky", {}).get(rec["label"])
        mark = "~" if flaky else "✗"
        said = " · ".join(record_excerpt(state, i)[0])
        text = _middle(mark + " " + rec["label"] + "  " + said, width)
        if chosen and state["focus"] == "failures":
            return _c("active") + fit(text, width) + _RESET
        return (_c("warning") if flaky else _c("error")) + mark + _RESET + " " + _c("strong") + rec["label"] + _RESET + _c("muted") + text[2 + len(rec["label"]):] + _RESET

    # A window of whole entries around the chosen one, as many as fit with
    # a line each for what is hidden above and below; too short for those,
    # the chosen one and what follows.
    chosen = block(at)
    if room < 3:
        rows = list(chosen)
        for pos in range(at + 1, total):
            if len(rows) >= room:
                break
            rows.extend(block(pos))
        for pos in range(at - 1, -1, -1):
            if len(rows) >= room:
                break
            rows = block(pos)[-(room - len(rows)):] + rows
        return rows[:room]
    start, end = at, at + 1
    used = len(chosen)
    for _ in range(total):
        grew = False
        if start > 0:
            more = len(block(start - 1)) if expand_all else 1
            if used + more + (1 if start - 1 > 0 else 0) + (1 if end < total else 0) <= room:
                used += more
                start -= 1
                grew = True
        if end < total:
            more = len(block(end)) if expand_all else 1
            if used + more + (1 if start > 0 else 0) + (1 if end + 1 < total else 0) <= room:
                used += more
                end += 1
                grew = True
        if not grew:
            break
    rows = []
    for pos in range(start, end):
        rows.extend(chosen if pos == at else block(pos))
    rows = rows[:max(1, room - (1 if start > 0 else 0) - (1 if end < total else 0))]
    return _counted(rows, start, total - end)

def _excerpt_row(line: str, width: int) -> str:
    """An excerpt's line in the Failures list, behind its `│`; a gap `│ …`."""
    if line == _GAP_ROW:
        return "  " + _c("muted") + "│ …" + _RESET
    return "  " + _c("muted") + "│" + _RESET + " " + fit(_expand_tabs(line), max(1, width - 4)).rstrip()

def listed_failures(state: dict) -> list:
    """The indexes of `state["fails"]` the Failures list has a row for, in
    the failure summary's order (`failure_entries`): every entry's record,
    a flaky test's too (marked, never counted); a test's other attempts,
    shards and runs are reached with `}` `{` from its row."""
    return [e["index"] for e in failure_entries(state)["entries"] if e["index"] != None]

def _listed_position(state: dict, listed: list, selected: int) -> int:
    """Where the chosen record is in the list: its own row, its test's row
    for another attempt of it, past the records for a target with none."""
    fails = state["fails"]
    if selected >= len(fails):
        return len(listed) + selected - len(fails)
    if selected in listed:
        return listed.index(selected)
    label = fails[selected]["label"] if selected < len(fails) else ""
    for pos, i in enumerate(listed):
        if fails[i]["label"] == label:
            return pos
    return 0

def _tail_text(state: dict, path: str):
    """The last 64 KiB of the file at `path` (`state["read"]`), or None."""
    if not path or state.get("read") == None:
        return None
    got = state["read"](path, 64 * 1024)
    return got[0] if got != None else None

def record_excerpt(state: dict, i: int) -> tuple:
    """`(details, rows)` of the Failures list's row for record `i`, as the
    failure summary heads and quotes its entry (`entry_text`): `([], [])`
    for a record that is no entry's."""
    for e in failure_entries(state)["entries"]:
        if e["index"] == i:
            text = entry_text(state, e)
            details = ([text["kind"]] if text["kind"] else []) + text["details"]
            if e["flaky"]:
                details.append("flaky, passed on a later attempt")
            return (details, excerpt_rows(text["lines"])[0])
    return ([], [])

def _window(total: int, room: int, selected: int):
    """Which of a list's `total` rows `room` lines show: `(start, shown,
    above, below)`, a window around the `selected` row, a line on each side
    counting what is hidden there (`above`, `below`); one with nothing to
    count gives its line back."""
    if total <= room:
        return (0, total, 0, 0)
    if room < 3:
        return (min(max(0, selected - room + 1), total - room), room, 0, 0)
    shown = room - 2
    start = min(max(0, selected - shown // 2), total - shown)
    if start == 0:
        shown += 1
    elif start + shown >= total:
        start -= 1
        shown += 1
    return (start, shown, start, total - start - shown)

def _counted(rows: list, above: int, below: int) -> list:
    """A list's `rows` with how many are hidden above and below them."""
    return ([_c("muted") + _counts("… %d earlier", above) + _RESET] if above else []) + rows + ([_c("muted") + _counts("… %d more", below) + _RESET] if below else [])

def action_rows(state: dict, room: int, width: int) -> list:
    """The actions list's rows, `room` at most, each `width` wide: a • and
    the label with its mnemonic, the chosen one in reverse video while the
    list has focus, kept in sight."""
    outputs = state.get("outputs", [])
    if room <= 0 or not outputs:
        return []
    picked = state.get("picked", 0)
    start, shown, above, below = _window(len(outputs), room, picked)
    rows = []
    for i in range(start, start + shown):
        rec = outputs[i]
        text = fit("• " + rec["label"] + "  " + rec["detail"], width).rstrip(" ")
        if i == picked and state["focus"] == "failures":
            rows.append(_c("active") + fit(text, width) + _RESET)
        else:
            rows.append(_c("accent") + "•" + _RESET + text[1:])
    return _counted(rows, above, below)

def console_lines(state: dict, log: list, kinds = None) -> list:
    """The console's lines `state` shows: by its `e` filter, by `kinds`
    (`classify`'s, of `log`, worked out when not given), then its search
    when that filters (a search that jumps shows every line)."""
    if state["filter"] != "all":
        kinds = kinds if kinds != None and len(kinds) == len(log) else classify(log)[0]
        log = [l for l, k in zip(log, kinds) if _shows(state["filter"], k)]
    search = state["search"]
    return search["pattern"].filter(log) if search["pattern"] != None and search["mode"] == "filter" else log

def _console_row(state: dict, pattern, current = False):
    """How a console row is painted, for the piece of a line `at` characters
    into it: the line as written, its colour kept (`colour_of`), cut to the
    cells `fit` kept of it; then its search matches, found in the plain
    text and laid over those cells (`mark_cells`)."""
    colour_of = state.get("colour_of", {})
    on = _c("search.current" if current else "search.match")

    def paint_for(line, at = 0):
        coloured = colour_of.get(line)

        def paint(shown):
            base = shown
            if coloured != None:
                cut = shown.endswith("…")
                base = _slice_escaped(coloured, at, text_width(shown) - (1 if cut else 0)) + ("…" if cut else "")
            if pattern == None:
                return base
            marked = pattern.highlight(shown, "\x01", "\x02")
            if marked == shown:
                return base
            marks = []
            cell = 0
            start = 0
            for c in marked.elems():
                if c == "\x01":
                    start = cell
                elif c == "\x02":
                    marks.append((start, cell, on, _RESET, on))
                else:
                    cell += text_width(c)
            return mark_cells(base, marks)

        return paint

    return paint_for

def wrap_line(text: str, width: int) -> list:
    """`text` as the rows it wraps to at `width` cells, each `(piece,
    start)`, `start` the character of `text` it starts at; a tab two cells,
    as `fit` draws it; one row for an empty line."""
    width = max(1, width)
    rows = []
    start = 0
    used = 0
    i = 0
    for c, n in _widths(text):
        n = 2 if c == 0x09 else n
        if used + n > width and i > start:
            rows.append((text[start:i], start))
            start = i
            used = 0
        used += n
        i += 1
    rows.append((text[start:], start))
    return rows

def _piece_links(links, start: int, length: int):
    """`links` (`[start, end, url]` by character of a line) as those of its
    piece of `length` characters from `start`."""
    if not links:
        return None
    out = []
    for a, b, url in links:
        a, b = max(a, start) - start, min(b, start + length) - start
        if a < b:
            out.append([a, b, url])
    return out

def _tail(text: str, cells: int) -> str:
    """The longest end of `text` a terminal draws in `cells` cells."""
    widths = _widths(text)
    used = 0
    for i in range(len(widths) - 1, -1, -1):
        if used + widths[i][1] > cells:
            return "".join([chr(c) for c, _ in widths[i + 1:]])
        used += widths[i][1]
    return text

def _search_bar(search: dict, matches: int, cols: int) -> str:
    """The bar's row, `cols` wide at most: whether it finds or filters, the
    query, its toggles lit when on (case when it matches case, by Ctrl+T or
    a capital), what it found (nothing for an empty query) or what is wrong
    with it, and its keys while they fit (the query's start goes before the
    toggles do)."""
    found = search["error"] or ("%d match%s" % (matches, "" if matches == 1 else "es") if search["query"] else "")
    jump = search["mode"] == "jump"
    hints = " · Tab %s · ^T case · ^B word · Enter keep · Esc back" % ("filter" if jump else "find")
    toggles = " Aa W "
    head = " Find: " if jump else " Filter: "

    # Measured as plain text, the escape sequences added after.
    room = cols - len(toggles) - text_width(found) - 1
    if room - len(hints) >= len(head) + text_width(search["query"]) + 1:
        room -= len(hints)
    else:
        hints = ""
    query = search["query"]
    if len(head) + text_width(query) + 1 > room:
        head = " /"
        keep = max(0, room - len(head) - 2)
        if text_width(query) > keep + 1:
            query = ("…" + _tail(query, keep)) if keep > 0 else ""
    if len(head) + text_width(query) + 1 > room:
        return fit(" /" + search["query"], cols)

    def toggle(on, label):
        return (_c("active") if on else _c("muted")) + label + _RESET

    return (
        head + query + "▏" + " " + toggle(case_sensitive(search), "Aa") + " " + toggle(search["word"], "W") + " " +
        (_c("error") + found + _RESET if search["error"] else found) + _c("muted") + hints + _RESET
    )

def shown_by(filter: str, line: str) -> bool:
    """Whether the console's `filter` (one of `_FILTERS`) shows `line`, by
    itself (`severity`)."""
    return _shows(filter, severity(line))

def bazel_line(line: str) -> str:
    """`line` without the `(23:04:25) ` (or `(23:04:25.123) `) Bazel puts
    before it under `--show_timestamps`."""
    if not line.startswith("("):
        return line
    close = line.find(") ")
    if close < 2 or close > 14:
        return line
    for c in line[1:close].elems():
        if c not in "0123456789:.":
            return line
    return line[close + 2:]

# What starts a line of Bazel's own: a line that starts none, a tool's
# (a compiler's diagnostic under an `ERROR:`), takes the kind of the one
# it follows (`classify`).
_HEADERS = ["INFO:", "WARNING:", "ERROR:", "DEBUG:", "FAIL:", "TIMEOUT:", "Target ", "Loading:", "Analyzing:", "Executed ", "Build ", "//", "@", "["]

def severity(line: str) -> str:
    """`error`, `warning` or `` for a console line by itself: Bazel's
    `ERROR:`, `FAIL:` and `TIMEOUT:` (indented too, as the console's recap
    of the errors lists them), a test summary's `FAILED`, `TIMEOUT` and
    `FAILED TO BUILD`, and its `WARNING:`; with or without the timestamp
    `--show_timestamps` puts before it."""
    line = bazel_line(line).lstrip(" ")
    for head in ("ERROR:", "FAIL:", "TIMEOUT:"):
        if line.startswith(head):
            return "error"
    if " FAILED " in line or line.endswith(" FAILED") or " TIMEOUT " in line or " FAILED TO BUILD" in line:
        return "error"
    if line.startswith("WARNING:"):
        return "warning"
    return ""

def _starts_block(line: str) -> bool:
    line = bazel_line(line).lstrip(" ")
    for head in _HEADERS:
        if line.startswith(head):
            return True
    return False

def classify(lines: list, block = "") -> tuple:
    """Each of `lines`' kind (`severity`), the lines after an `ERROR:`
    taking its kind until another line of Bazel's own, so a compiler's
    diagnostic under its action's `ERROR:` is an error too; a `WARNING:`
    (Bazel's are one line, a tool's own among its output, `INFO: From`'s)
    is its line alone; and the kind the last line leaves for the next, from
    `block`, the one the lines before them left."""
    kinds = []
    for line in lines:
        kind = severity(line)
        if _starts_block(line):
            plain = bazel_line(line).lstrip(" ")
            block = kind if plain.startswith("ERROR:") else ""
        elif not kind:
            kind = block
        kinds.append(kind)
    return kinds, block

def _shows(filter: str, kind: str) -> bool:
    return filter == "all" or kind == "error" or (kind == "warning" and filter == "warnings")

def elapsed(ms: int) -> str:
    """`ms` as `1m02s` or `12s`."""
    s = ms // 1000
    if s < 60:
        return "%ds" % s
    sec = s % 60
    return "%dm%s%ds" % (s // 60, "0" if sec < 10 else "", sec)

def _get(obj, name: str, default = None):
    """`obj.name`, or `default` when `obj` is None or has no such field."""
    return getattr(obj, name, default) if obj != None else default

def new_counts() -> dict:
    """The tallies `count_event` keeps for one Bazel attempt."""
    return {
        "configured": 0,
        "built": 0,
        "failed": 0,
        "skipped": 0,
        "failures": [],
        "failed_labels": {},
        "aborted": {},
        "tests_passed": 0,
        "tests_failed": 0,
        "tests_flaky": 0,
        "tests_skipped": 0,
        "flaky": {},
        "failed_attempts": {},
        "test_targets": {},
        "test_unbuilt": {},
        "testing": False,
        "failed_roots": {},
        "test_runs": 0,
        "test_cached": 0,
        "tests_cached": 0,
        "test_resulted": {},
        "test_finished": {},
        "test_uncached": {},
        "spawns": 0,
        "spawn_hits": 0,
        "runners": {},
        "log_actions": {},
        "log_kinds": {"ran": 0, "disk": 0, "remote": 0, "mixed": 0},
    }

def count_spawn(counts: dict, spawn):
    """Tally one spawn of the execution log (Bazel's compact one): the
    source of truth for what ran and what a cache (remote or disk) served,
    by runner, and by action (`log_kinds`): the spawns of one action share
    its outputs, so its first output's id names it, and an action is a
    disk or remote hit where all its spawns were, `mixed` where they
    differ (a retried test's attempts are one action's spawns). A spawn
    with no output is an action of its own."""
    counts["spawns"] += 1
    hit = bool(_get(spawn, "cache_hit", False))
    if hit:
        counts["spawn_hits"] += 1
    runner = str(_get(spawn, "runner", "") or "") or "unknown"
    counts["runners"][runner] = counts["runners"].get(runner, 0) + 1
    kind = ("remote" if runner.startswith("remote") else "disk") if hit else "ran"
    outputs = _get(spawn, "outputs", None) or []
    key = _get(outputs[0], "type", None) if outputs else None
    kinds = counts["log_kinds"]
    if type(key) != "int":
        kinds[kind] += 1
        return
    was = counts["log_actions"].get(key)
    if was == None:
        counts["log_actions"][key] = kind
        kinds[kind] += 1
    elif was != kind and was != "mixed":
        counts["log_actions"][key] = "mixed"
        kinds[was] -= 1
        kinds["mixed"] += 1

def _fail(counts: dict, label: str):
    """Count a failed target once, however often Bazel says so (one per
    configuration, its analysis aborted by an aspect and its action failed
    too), and list it."""
    if label not in counts["failed_labels"]:
        counts["failed"] += 1
    _list_failure(counts, label)

def _list_failure(counts: dict, label: str):
    """List `label` as failed, once: a dict beside the list keeps it O(1)."""
    if label not in counts["failed_labels"]:
        counts["failed_labels"][label] = True
        counts["failures"].append(label)

def count_aborted_test(counts: dict, label: str, reason: str, config: str = ""):
    """Tally a test whose summary is Aborted for `reason`. Bazel gives one it
    skipped (a build without `--keep_going` stopped first, a later attempt
    of a flaky one cut off) and one that failed to build the same empty
    reason; only the second has a result `FAILED_TO_BUILD`
    (`failed_attempts`, by label and configuration), so it is failed, as
    Bazel counts it, and the first skipped. An interrupt is no verdict, and
    a test counted failed already (`test_unbuilt`) is not counted again."""
    if not label or reason.lower() == "user_interrupted" or label in counts["test_unbuilt"]:
        return
    if (label, config) in counts["failed_attempts"]:
        counts["tests_failed"] += 1
        _list_failure(counts, label)
    else:
        counts["tests_skipped"] += 1

def count_aborted(counts: dict, label: str, reason: str):
    """Tally a target that did not build, its Aborted `reason` given: failed
    when its loading or analysis did (`_FAILED_ABORTS`), else skipped; once
    per label, as Bazel can report one under two ids."""
    if not label or label in counts["aborted"]:
        return
    counts["aborted"][label] = True
    if reason.lower() in _FAILED_ABORTS:
        _fail(counts, label)
    else:
        counts["skipped"] += 1

def count_event(counts: dict, kind: str, event_id, payload, aborted = None):
    """Tally one build event into `counts`. `build_started` says whether the
    command tests (`testing`: `test` or `coverage`), which gates counting a
    test target that failed to build. A target that failed to build arrives
    as `target_completed` without `success`; one that never got that far
    (failed in loading or analysis, skipped, interrupted) as an Aborted
    payload under its own id (`count_aborted`). An Aborted `configured_label`
    with a failing reason is a root cause not asked for that failed
    analysis: in `failed_roots`, and a failed target, once per label. An
    aspect's own completion is not a target. A test's `overall_status` is
    lowercase (`passed`, `flaky`, `failed`, …); only `_FAILED_TEST_STATUSES`
    count as failed. A test whose summary is Aborted is failed or skipped by
    `count_aborted_test`. A label is listed in `failures` once. `aborted`
    says whether `payload` is an Aborted one, its type by default."""
    if aborted == None:
        aborted = type(payload) == "aborted"
    label = repaired(str(_get(event_id, "label", "") or ""))
    if kind == "action_completed" and not aborted and _get(payload, "success", False):
        # Every action Bazel executed, with `--build_event_publish_all_actions`:
        # one that ran no spawn (internal) has no command line.
        counts["actions_seen"] = counts.get("actions_seen", 0) + 1
        if not (_get(payload, "command_line", None) or []):
            counts["actions_internal"] = counts.get("actions_internal", 0) + 1
        return
    if kind == "build_started":
        # Only a test or coverage run tests what it builds.
        counts["testing"] = str(_get(payload, "command", "") or "") in ("test", "coverage")
        return

    # A target's own Aborted payload, under its `target_configured` or
    # `target_completed` id; under `configured_label` or `unconfigured_label`
    # it names a root cause (a dependency), not a target asked for.
    if aborted:
        reason = str(_get(payload, "reason", "") or "")
        if kind in ("target_configured", "target_completed") and not _get(event_id, "aspect", ""):
            count_aborted(counts, label, reason)
        elif kind == "test_summary":
            count_aborted_test(counts, label, reason, str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""))
        elif kind == "configured_label" and label and reason.lower() in _FAILED_ABORTS and label not in counts["failed_roots"]:
            # A root cause that failed analysis, not asked for itself: what
            # a target it blocks failed for (`blocked_targets`), and a
            # failure of its own.
            counts["failed_roots"][label] = True
            count_aborted(counts, label, reason)
        elif kind == "unconfigured_label" and label and label not in counts["failed_roots"]:
            # One that failed loading (its package broken, or a source file
            # missing): Bazel gives no reason, but it is a root cause all
            # the same; a label no one asked for, it counts as no target.
            counts["failed_roots"][label] = True
        return
    if kind == "target_configured":
        if not _get(event_id, "aspect", ""):
            counts["configured"] += 1
            if str(_get(payload, "target_kind", "") or "").endswith("_test rule"):
                counts["test_targets"][label] = True
    elif kind == "target_completed":
        if _get(event_id, "aspect", ""):
            return
        if _get(payload, "success", False):
            counts["built"] += 1
        else:
            _fail(counts, label)
            if counts["testing"] and label in counts["test_targets"] and label not in counts["test_unbuilt"]:
                # A test that failed to build (or was blocked): Bazel posts
                # no summary for it, and counts it as failing to build.
                counts["test_unbuilt"][label] = True
                counts["tests_failed"] += 1
    elif kind == "test_result":
        # A test run, and whether a cache (Bazel's own, or a remote one)
        # gave it rather than running it.
        counts["test_runs"] += 1
        if str(_get(payload, "status", "") or "").lower() == "failed_to_build":
            counts["failed_attempts"][(label, str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""))] = True
        info = _get(payload, "execution_info")
        key = (label, str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""))
        counts["test_resulted"][key] = True
        if _get(payload, "cached_locally", False) or _get(info, "cached_remotely", False):
            counts["test_cached"] += 1
        else:
            counts["test_uncached"][key] = True
    elif kind == "test_summary":
        counts["test_finished"][label] = True
        key = (label, str(_get(_get(event_id, "configuration", None), "id", "") or ""))
        if key in counts["test_resulted"] and key not in counts["test_uncached"]:
            # Every run of the target a cache gave.
            counts["tests_cached"] += 1
        status = str(_get(payload, "overall_status", "") or "")
        if status == "passed":
            counts["tests_passed"] += 1
        elif status == "flaky":
            # Passed in the end: not a failure, nor a pass to count as one.
            counts["tests_flaky"] += 1
            counts["flaky"][label] = True
        elif status in _FAILED_TEST_STATUSES:
            counts["tests_failed"] += 1
            _list_failure(counts, label)

def link(url: str, text: str) -> str:
    """`text` as an OSC 8 hyperlink to `url`, which a terminal that knows the
    sequence opens on a click and any other shows as `text`."""
    return "\x1b]8;;" + url + "\x1b\\" + text + "\x1b]8;;\x1b\\"

# The link under the pointer is lit in its text (`link.hover`), a
# selection in its background (`selection`): each turned off alone, so
# the rest of the cell's colour stays.
_NO_FOREGROUND = "\x1b[39m"
_NO_BACKGROUND = "\x1b[49m"

def _selection_colour(state: dict):
    """How a selection is drawn, `(on, off)`: its background, or where the
    theme draws it otherwise (reverse, at 16 colours or in `mono`), that,
    then a full reset."""
    bg = theme.bg("selection", stream = True)
    return (bg, _NO_BACKGROUND) if bg else (_c("selection"), _RESET)

def _hover_colour(state: dict):
    """How the link under the pointer is drawn, `(on, off)`, as a
    selection's is."""
    fg = theme.fg("link.hover", stream = True)
    return (fg, _NO_FOREGROUND) if fg else (_c("link.hover"), _RESET)

# How a link the console made is drawn: an OSC 8 link, which a terminal
# with the mouse opens on a click, underlined so it shows as one where the
# terminal does not draw them (the macOS Terminal).
_UNDERLINE = "\x1b[4m"
_NO_UNDERLINE = "\x1b[24m"

def _escape_state(state: int, c: int) -> int:
    """Where an escape sequence is after code point `c`: 0 outside one, 1
    just after ESC, 2 in a CSI (`ESC [`), 3 in an OSC (`ESC ]`), 4 in an
    OSC after an ESC, which a `\\` ends."""
    if state == 0:
        return 1 if c == 0x1b else 0
    if state == 1:
        return 2 if c == 0x5b else (3 if c == 0x5d else 0)
    if state == 2:
        return 0 if c >= 0x40 and c <= 0x7e else 2
    if state == 3:
        return 0 if c == 0x07 else (4 if c == 0x1b else 3)
    return 0 if c == 0x5c else 3

def mark_cells(row: str, marks: list) -> str:
    """`row`, a drawn row with its escapes, with each of `marks`, `(start,
    end, open, close, again)` in cells from 0 and sorted, wrapped: `open`
    before the first cell it covers, `close` after the last, and `again`
    after every colour change inside it (a reset would undo `open`)."""
    if not marks:
        return row
    out = []
    at = 0
    k = 0
    inside = None
    esc = 0
    sgr = False
    for c in row.codepoints():
        if esc != 0 or c == 0x1b:
            out.append(chr(c))
            before = esc
            esc = _escape_state(esc, c)
            if before == 2 and esc == 0 and c == 0x6d and inside != None:
                out.append(inside[4])
            continue
        if inside != None and at >= inside[1]:
            out.append(inside[3])
            inside = None
        if inside == None and k < len(marks) and marks[k][0] <= at:
            inside = marks[k]
            k += 1
            out.append(inside[2])
        out.append(chr(c))
        at += 1 if c < 0x0300 else _cells(c)
    if inside != None:
        out.append(inside[3])
    return "".join(out)

def _drawn_cell(c: int, prev: int):
    """How a code point of a coloured line is drawn, `(cells, text)`, as
    `fit` and `_widths` draw the plain line: a tab as two spaces, U+FE0F
    widening the narrow character before it."""
    if c == 0x09:
        return (2, "  ")
    if c == 0xFE0F:
        return (1 if prev == 1 else 0, chr(c))
    return (1 if c < 0x0300 else _cells(c), chr(c))

def _slice_escaped(text: str, skip: int, cells: int) -> str:
    """`text`, escapes and all, from its `skip`th visible character on, cut
    after `cells` cells: the colour set before the cut kept, and ended."""
    out = []
    seen = 0
    at = 0
    esc = 0
    prev = 0
    for c in text.codepoints():
        if esc != 0 or c == 0x1b:
            out.append(chr(c))
            esc = _escape_state(esc, c)
            continue
        if seen < skip:
            seen += 1
            n, _ = _drawn_cell(c, prev)
            prev = n if n else prev
            continue
        n, shown = _drawn_cell(c, prev)
        if at + n > cells:
            break
        out.append(shown)
        at += n
        prev = n if n else prev
    return "".join(out) + _RESET

def _cut_escaped(text: str, cells: int) -> str:
    """`text`, escapes and all, cut after `cells` visible cells, its colour
    ended."""
    out = []
    at = 0
    esc = 0
    prev = 0
    for c in text.codepoints():
        if esc != 0 or c == 0x1b:
            out.append(chr(c))
            esc = _escape_state(esc, c)
            continue
        n, shown = _drawn_cell(c, prev)
        if at + n > cells:
            break
        out.append(shown)
        at += n
        prev = n if n else prev
    return "".join(out) + "\x1b[39m"

def _peek_paint(highlighted: str):
    """A colour for `_box_row` drawing a peek's row: its highlighted line,
    as wide as the text `fit` kept of it (its `…` too)."""

    def paint(shown):
        cut = shown.endswith("…")
        return _cut_escaped(highlighted, text_width(shown) - (1 if cut else 0)) + ("…" if cut else "")

    return paint

# How many lines a peek reads around the line it shows, and before them for
# the highlighter to start a comment or a string where the file does.
_PEEK_LINES = 5000
_PEEK_CONTEXT = 40

# What a peek highlights, at most: past it (generated or minified code) the
# lines are shown plain, as highlighting them would hold the screen.
_HIGHLIGHT_BYTES = 512 * 1024
_HIGHLIGHT_LINE = 1000

def highlightable(lines: list) -> bool:
    """Whether `lines` are few and short enough to highlight at once."""
    total = 0
    for text in lines:
        if len(text) > _HIGHLIGHT_LINE:
            return False
        total += len(text) + 1
        if total > _HIGHLIGHT_BYTES:
            return False
    return True

def open_peek(state: dict, path: str, line: int) -> bool:
    """Show `path` in the console pane around `line` (`_PEEK_LINES` of it),
    highlighted (unless too large to, `highlightable`), numbered, `line` marked and in the middle; `o` opens it in
    the editor. Each
    line is drawable, as a log's: escapes stripped, a carriage return's
    redraw resolved, other controls dropped, a tab two spaces. Whether it
    could be read (a directory, a binary file past reading, cannot)."""
    fs = state.get("fs")
    syntax = state.get("syntax")
    if fs == None:
        return False
    span = _PEEK_LINES
    first = max(1, line - span // 4)
    start = max(1, first - _PEEK_CONTEXT)
    got = fs.try_read_lines(path, start, (first - start) + span)
    if got == None:
        return False
    lines, more = got
    ansi, controls = state.get("ansi"), state.get("controls")
    clean = []
    for text in lines:
        if ansi != None:
            text = ansi.replace(text, "")
        text = text.rstrip("\r").rpartition("\r")[2].replace("\t", "  ")
        if controls != None:
            text = controls.replace(text, "")
        clean.append(text)
    lines = clean
    env = state.get("env")
    truecolor = env != None and (env.var("COLORTERM") or "") in ("truecolor", "24bit")
    hl = syntax.highlight(lines, path, truecolor = truecolor) if syntax != None and highlightable(lines) else lines
    plain = []
    painted = []
    for i in range(first - start, len(lines)):
        n = start + i
        mark = "▸" if n == line else " "
        number = " " * max(0, 5 - len(str(n))) + str(n)
        plain.append(mark + number + "  " + lines[i])
        painted.append((_c("marker") + "▸" + _RESET if n == line else " ") + _c("border") + number + "  " + _RESET + hl[i])
    if not plain:
        plain, painted = ["(line %d is past the end of the file)" % line], ["(line %d is past the end of the file)" % line]
    name = path.rpartition("/")[2]
    _keep_view(state)
    state["view"] = {"title": "%s:%d" % (name, line), "lines": plain, "hl": painted, "cut": more, "record": None, "path": path, "line": line, "peek": True}
    state["search"]["back"] = -1
    state["focus"] = "console"

    # The line in the middle of the pane.
    target = line - first
    state["scroll"] = max(0, len(plain) - 1 - target - state.get("page_h", 20) // 2)
    return True

def link_spans(row: str, url: str) -> list:
    """The cells `(start, end)` of each OSC 8 link to `url` drawn in `row`."""
    out = []
    at = 0
    esc = 0
    seq = []
    current = ""
    start = 0
    for c in row.codepoints():
        if esc != 0 or c == 0x1b:
            seq.append(chr(c))
            esc = _escape_state(esc, c)
            if esc == 0:
                text = "".join(seq)
                seq = []
                if text.startswith("\x1b]8;"):
                    if current == url and start < at:
                        out.append((start, at))
                    current = text[len("\x1b]8;"):].rstrip("\x07").removesuffix("\x1b\\").partition(";")[2]
                    start = at
            continue
        at += 1 if c < 0x0300 else _cells(c)
    if current == url and start < at:
        out.append((start, at))
    return out

def link_span_at(row: str, col: int):
    """The cells `(start, end)` of the OSC 8 link drawn at cell `col` of
    `row`, or None."""
    at = 0
    esc = 0
    seq = []
    url = ""
    start = -1
    for c in row.codepoints():
        if esc != 0 or c == 0x1b:
            seq.append(chr(c))
            esc = _escape_state(esc, c)
            if esc == 0:
                text = "".join(seq)
                seq = []
                if text.startswith("\x1b]8;"):
                    body = text[len("\x1b]8;"):].rstrip("\x07").removesuffix("\x1b\\")
                    if url and start <= col and col < at:
                        return (start, at)
                    url = body.partition(";")[2]
                    start = at
            continue
        at += 1 if c < 0x0300 else _cells(c)
    if url and start <= col and col < at:
        return (start, at)
    return None

def link_at(row: str, col: int):
    """The URL of the OSC 8 link drawn at cell `col` of `row`, or None."""
    at = 0
    esc = 0
    seq = []
    url = ""
    for c in row.codepoints():
        if esc != 0 or c == 0x1b:
            seq.append(chr(c))
            esc = _escape_state(esc, c)
            if esc == 0:
                text = "".join(seq)
                seq = []
                if text.startswith("\x1b]8;"):
                    body = text[len("\x1b]8;"):].rstrip("\x07").removesuffix("\x1b\\")
                    url = body.partition(";")[2]
            continue
        n = 1 if c < 0x0300 else _cells(c)
        if url and at <= col and col < at + n:
            return url
        at += n
    return None

def _cell_slice(text: str, start: int, end: int, overhang = False) -> str:
    """The characters of plain `text` that start at cell `start` or after
    and end by cell `end`: never wider than `end - start`, a wide character
    the end would split left out. With `overhang`, one that starts before
    `end` is kept whole (a selection's copy, which draws nothing)."""
    out = []
    at = 0
    for c, n in _widths(text):
        if at >= end or (at + n > end and not overhang):
            break
        if at >= start:
            out.append(chr(c))
        at += n
    return "".join(out)

def link_marks(text: str, links, room: int) -> list:
    """`links` (`[start, end, url]` by character of `text`) as `mark_cells`
    marks in cells, as `fit` draws `text` (a tab as two spaces), clipped to
    `room` cells."""
    if not links:
        return []
    cells = [0]
    for c, n in _widths(text):
        cells.append(cells[-1] + (2 if c == 0x09 else n))
    marks = []
    for start, end, url in sorted(links):
        if start >= len(text) or end > len(text) or start >= end:
            continue
        c0, c1 = cells[start], min(cells[end], room)
        if c0 < c1 and (not marks or c0 >= marks[-1][1]):
            # A web page's link in the theme's `link`; a file's underlined
            # alone, in the line's own colour.
            web = url.startswith("http://") or url.startswith("https://")
            on = _UNDERLINE + (_c("link") if web else "")
            off = _NO_UNDERLINE + (_NO_FOREGROUND if web else "")
            marks.append((c0, c1, "\x1b]8;;" + url + "\x1b\\" + on, off + "\x1b]8;;\x1b\\", on))
    return marks

def keep_links(state: dict, line: str, links):
    """Note the console's `links` on `line` (`[start, end, url]` by
    character), for its rows to draw them: by the line's text, so a filtered
    or scrolled view finds them as the console pane does."""
    if links:
        state.setdefault("line_links", {})[line] = links

def selection_rows(state: dict, cols: int) -> list:
    """The selection as `(row, start, end)` in cells, its rows in order:
    from where the drag began to where it is, across rows as text reads,
    within the console pane's text when it began there."""
    sel = state.get("select")
    if sel == None or not sel["dragged"]:
        return []
    a, b = sel["from"], sel["to"]
    if (a[1], a[0]) > (b[1], b[0]):
        a, b = b, a
    left, right = sel["bounds"]
    top, bottom = sel.get("rows", (0, 1 << 30))
    out = []
    for row in range(max(a[1], top), min(b[1], bottom) + 1):
        start = a[0] if row == a[1] else left
        end = b[0] + 1 if row == b[1] else right
        start, end = max(start, left), min(end, right)
        if start < end:
            out.append((row, start, end))
    return out

def selected_text(state: dict, cols: int) -> str:
    """What the selection covers, as the rows were drawn, each row's
    trailing spaces dropped and a wrapped line's rows joined again."""
    frame = state.get("frame") or []
    continued = state.get("continued", {})
    lines = []
    for row, start, end in selection_rows(state, cols):
        if row < len(frame):
            plain = state["ansi"].replace(frame[row], "") if state.get("ansi") != None else frame[row]
            text = _cell_slice(plain, start, end, overhang = True).rstrip(" ")
            if lines and row in continued:
                # A wrapped line's next row: the same line.
                lines[-1] += text
            else:
                lines.append(text)
    return "\n".join(lines)

def _remote(env) -> bool:
    return env != None and (env.var("SSH_CONNECTION") != None or env.var("SSH_TTY") != None)

def copy_text(state: dict, text: str) -> str:
    """Put `text` on the clipboard: by OSC 52 with the next frame, which a
    terminal that allows it takes (over SSH too), and on this machine's own
    (`pbcopy`, `wl-copy`, `xclip`, `xsel`) unless it is a remote session.
    What happened, for the keys row."""
    state["osc"] = "\x1b]52;c;" + base64.encode(text) + "\x07"
    process = state.get("process")
    if process == None or _remote(state.get("env")):
        return "copied"
    env = state["env"]
    wayland = env.var("WAYLAND_DISPLAY") != None
    x11 = env.var("DISPLAY") != None
    for argv in (["pbcopy"], ["wl-copy"] if wayland else [], ["xclip", "-selection", "clipboard"] if x11 else [], ["xsel", "--clipboard", "--input"] if x11 else []):
        if argv and process.which(argv[0]) != None:
            child = _detached(state, process.command(argv[0]).args(argv[1:]).stdin("piped").stdout("null").stderr("null"))
            stdin = child.stdin()
            stdin.write(text)
            stdin.close()
            child.wait()
            return "copied"
    return "copied (if the terminal allows OSC 52)"

# What a click opens: a web page, or a file in an editor, by the URLs
# `--console:file-hyperlink-format`'s editors open a file at (not their
# other handlers, an extension's or a clone's). Any other link, which output
# a build ran could have written (a `file://` script, `-a App`), is copied
# rather than handed to the system's opener.
_OPENED_PREFIXES = [
    "http://",
    "https://",
    "vscode://file/",
    "vscode-insiders://file/",
    "vscodium://file/",
    "cursor://file/",
    "zed://file/",
    "idea://open?file=",
    "mvim://open?url=file://",
    "txmt://open?url=file://",
]

def opens(url: str) -> bool:
    """Whether a click opens `url`: one of `_OPENED_PREFIXES` and more,
    printable ASCII with no space, so the opener reads it as one URL."""
    for c in url.codepoints():
        if c <= 0x20 or c >= 0x7f:
            return False
    for prefix in _OPENED_PREFIXES:
        if url.startswith(prefix) and len(url) > len(prefix):
            return True
    return False

def _detached(state: dict, command):
    """`command` spawned in a process group of its own, on a token nothing
    cancels, so the screen's `q` (its group's SIGINT) and a cancel leave a
    browser it started or a clipboard daemon running. Its streams are set
    (`"null"`), as a process that outlives the CLI sets them."""
    command = command.process_group(0)
    cancellation = state.get("cancellation")
    return command.spawn(cancellation = cancellation.new()) if cancellation != None else command.spawn()

def _editor(state: dict):
    """The editor `o` opens a file in, `(format, name)`: the one `O` chose
    this session, else the console's (`--console:file-hyperlink-format`)."""
    io = state.get("io")
    if io == None:
        return ("none", "")
    choice = state.get("editor_choice")
    if choice:
        for alias, name in io.editors():
            if alias == choice:
                return (alias, name)
    return io.editor()

_KEEP_EDITOR = "to keep it, set ctx.features[Console].args.file_hyperlink_format = \"%s\" in .aspect/config.axl (or ASPECT_FILE_HYPERLINK_FORMAT=%s)"

def open_in_editor(state: dict, path: str, line = 1) -> str:
    """Open `path` at `line` in `_editor`'s editor; what happened, for the
    keys row. An editor's link only: never a `file://` one, which would have
    the system run the file."""
    io = state.get("io")
    if io == None or not path:
        return "no file to open"
    alias, name = _editor(state)
    url = io.editor_url(path, line = line, format = "" if alias in ("none", "template") else alias) if alias != "none" else None
    if url == None:
        return "no editor to open it in: O picks one, or set --console:file-hyperlink-format"
    if url.startswith("file:"):
        return "no editor to open it in: O picks one, or set --console:file-hyperlink-format"
    return open_link(state, url, trusted = True).replace("opened " + url, "opened in " + name)

def next_editor(state: dict) -> str:
    """`O`: the next installed editor for `o`, this session; how to keep it."""
    io = state.get("io")
    editors = io.editors() if io != None else []
    if not editors:
        return "no editor found: install one, or set --console:file-hyperlink-format"
    aliases = [alias for alias, _ in editors]
    current = _editor(state)[0]
    i = (aliases.index(current) + 1) % len(aliases) if current in aliases else 0
    state["editor_choice"] = aliases[i]
    return "o opens in %s now; %s" % (editors[i][1], _KEEP_EDITOR % (aliases[i], aliases[i]))

def _view_file(state: dict):
    """The file `o` opens, `(path, line)`: the peek's, the log shown's, or
    for the console the task's whole output (`output.log` in its history
    folder, beside its builds' `bazel/<n>/`), else Bazel's console log."""
    view = state["view"]
    if view != None and view.get("path"):
        return (view["path"], view.get("line", 1))
    if view != None and view.get("record") != None:
        paths = _log_paths(state, view["record"])
        return (paths[0][1] if paths else "", 1)
    console = state.get("log_path", "")
    task_dir = console.rpartition("/bazel/")[0]
    output = task_dir + "/output.log" if task_dir else ""
    if output and state.get("read") != None and state["read"](output, 0) != None:
        return (output, 1)
    return (console, 1)

def open_link(state: dict, url: str, trusted = False) -> str:
    """Open `url` as this machine does (`open`, `xdg-open`) when a click
    opens it (`opens`); copy it instead in a remote session, which would
    open it there, and for any other link. The opener is waited on for
    `_OPEN_WAIT_MS` at most: one that fails by then (no app for a
    `cursor://` link) has the link copied instead; one still running (a
    browser taking it) has opened it. What happened, for the keys row."""
    process = state.get("process")
    if process == None or _remote(state.get("env")) or not (trusted or opens(url)):
        copy_text(state, url)
        return "link copied: " + url
    for opener in ("open", "xdg-open"):
        if process.which(opener) != None:
            child = _detached(state, process.command(opener).args([url]).stdin("null").stdout("null").stderr("null"))
            status = None
            for _ in range(_OPEN_WAIT_MS // _OPEN_POLL_MS):
                status = child.try_wait(timeout_ms = _OPEN_POLL_MS)
                if status != None:
                    break
            if status != None and not status.success:
                copy_text(state, url)
                return "couldn't open %s, copied it" % url
            return "opened " + url
    copy_text(state, url)
    return "link copied: " + url

# How long a click waits to see whether the opener failed, in slices.
_OPEN_WAIT_MS = 300
_OPEN_POLL_MS = 50

# How soon a second press on the same cell makes a double click.
_DOUBLE_CLICK_MS = 400

# What ends a word a double click selects: a space, a bracket, a quote, a
# comma, so a path (`src/a.cc:12`), a label or a URL is one word.
_WORD_ENDS = " \t()[]{}<>\"'`,;|"

def _select_word(state: dict, col: int, row: int) -> bool:
    """Select the word drawn at `col` of `row` and copy it, as a drag would."""
    frame = state.get("frame") or []
    if row >= len(frame):
        return True
    plain = state["ansi"].replace(frame[row], "") if state.get("ansi") != None else frame[row]
    cells = []
    at = 0
    for c, n in _widths(plain):
        cells.append((at, at + n, chr(c)))
        at += n
    hit = [i for i, (a, b, _) in enumerate(cells) if a <= col and col < b]
    if not hit or cells[hit[0]][2] in _WORD_ENDS:
        return True
    first = hit[0]
    for i in range(hit[0], -1, -1):
        if cells[i][2] in _WORD_ENDS:
            break
        first = i
    last = hit[0]
    for i in range(hit[0], len(cells)):
        if cells[i][2] in _WORD_ENDS:
            break
        last = i
    word = "".join([c for _, _, c in cells[first:last + 1]])
    cols = state.get("cols", 80)
    state["select"] = {"from": (cells[first][0], row), "to": (cells[last][1] - 1, row), "dragged": True, "done": True, "bounds": (0, cols), "rows": (0, 1 << 30)}
    say_note(state, "%s %d character%s" % (copy_text(state, word), len(word), "" if len(word) == 1 else "s"))
    return True

def mouse_key(state: dict, key: str) -> bool:
    """A left button's `mouse-press`, `mouse-drag` or `mouse-release` key:
    a drag selects (within the console pane's text when it starts there)
    and its release copies, the selection then shown until the next press;
    a click on a link opens it (`open_link`). Whether `key` was one."""
    kind, _, at = key.partition(":")
    if kind not in ("mouse-press", "mouse-drag", "mouse-release", "mouse-move"):
        return False
    col, _, row = at.partition(":")
    if not col.isdigit() or not row.isdigit():
        return True
    col, row = int(col), int(row)
    if kind == "mouse-move":
        # Where the pointer is, for `render` to light the link under it.
        state["hover"] = (col, row)
        return True
    cols = state.get("cols", 80)
    if kind == "mouse-press":
        # A second press on the cell within `_DOUBLE_CLICK_MS`: the word
        # under it, selected and copied.
        now = int(monotonic() * 1000)
        last = state.get("last_press")
        state["last_press"] = (col, row, now)
        if last != None and last[0] == col and last[1] == row and now - last[2] <= _DOUBLE_CLICK_MS:
            state["last_press"] = None
            return _select_word(state, col, row)
        top, bottom = state.get("pane", (-1, -1))
        inside = top <= row and row <= bottom
        state["select"] = {
            "from": (col, row),
            "to": (col, row),
            "dragged": False,
            "bounds": (2, max(2, cols - 2)) if inside else (0, cols),
            "rows": (top, bottom) if inside else (0, 1 << 30),
        }
        return True
    sel = state.get("select")
    if sel == None or sel.get("done"):
        # No press since the last release (a legacy report names any
        # button's release as the left's).
        return True
    if kind == "mouse-drag":
        sel["to"] = (col, row)
        sel["dragged"] = sel["dragged"] or (col, row) != sel["from"]
        return True
    sel["to"] = (col, row)
    sel["done"] = True
    if sel["dragged"]:
        text = selected_text(state, cols)
        if text:
            say_note(state, "%s %d character%s" % (copy_text(state, text), len(text), "" if len(text) == 1 else "s"))
        return True
    state["select"] = None
    if row == state.get("tab_row"):
        for a, b, tab in state.get("tab_spans", []):
            if a <= col and col < b:
                if type(tab) == "tuple":
                    back_to(state, tab[1])
                else:
                    show_tab(state, tab)
                return True
    failed_at = state.get("failed_at")
    if failed_at != None and row == failed_at[0] and col >= failed_at[1]:
        show_tab(state, "failures")
        return True
    frame = state.get("frame") or []
    url = link_at(frame[row], col) if row < len(frame) else None
    if url != None:
        # A file's link shows the file here (`o` opens it in the editor);
        # any other opens.
        io = state.get("io")
        target = io.editor_target(url) if io != None else None
        if target != None and open_peek(state, target[0], target[1]):
            say_note(state, "o opens it in " + (_editor(state)[1] or "your editor") + ", Esc goes back")
        else:
            say_note(state, open_link(state, url))
        return True
    toggle_fold(state, row)
    return True

def links_row(pages: list, cols: int) -> str:
    """One row of `(label, url)` results pages, each cut to its share of the
    width but linked to its whole url; `""` without pages."""
    if not pages:
        return ""
    lead = " "
    room = max(0, cols - len(lead) - 3 * (len(pages) - 1))
    each = room // len(pages)
    parts = []
    for label, url in pages:
        shown = fit(label + ": " + url, each).rstrip(" ")
        parts.append(_c("link") + link(url, shown) + _RESET)
    return lead + " · ".join(parts)

def _box_top(title: str, width: int) -> str:
    title = fit(title, width - 5).rstrip(" ")
    return _c("border") + "┌─ " + _RESET + _c("strong") + title + _RESET + _c("border") + " " + "─" * max(0, width - text_width(title) - 5) + "┐" + _RESET

def _box_row(text: str, width: int, coloured = None, mark = False, edge = None, links = None) -> str:
    """A row of a box `width` wide, `coloured` (a function of its text) when
    given, `mark`ed in its left margin (the current match), its right border
    `edge` when given (a scrollbar's), `links` (`[start, end, url]` by
    character of `text`) drawn as links."""
    body = fit(text, width - 4)
    if coloured != None:
        # Coloured before the padding, so a highlight never lights it.
        shown = body.rstrip(" ")
        body = coloured(shown) + " " * (len(body) - len(shown))
    body = mark_cells(body, link_marks(text, links, width - 4))

    # The current match marked by its row's left edge, as the scrollbar's
    # thumb marks the view: `▹` begins the CLI's own update lines.
    left = (_c("marker") + "┃" if mark else _c("border") + "│") + _RESET + " "
    return left + body + (" " + edge if edge != None else _c("border") + " │" + _RESET)

def scrollbar(total: int, height: int, up: int):
    """The right border of a pane `height` rows tall showing the lines
    `up` from the end of `total`: a thumb as tall as the share shown, at the
    bottom when following, at the top at the oldest line; None, the plain
    border, when everything fits."""
    if total <= height or height < 1:
        return None
    size = max(1, height * height // total)
    top = height - size - (up * (height - size)) // max(1, total - height)
    return [(_c("scrollbar") + "┃" + _RESET) if top <= i and i < top + size else (_c("border") + "│" + _RESET) for i in range(height)]

def _box_bottom(width: int) -> str:
    return _c("border") + "└" + "─" * max(0, width - 2) + "┘" + _RESET

def _word_seconds(word: str):
    """The seconds a time word says, or None for a word that is no time:
    Bazel's (`12s`, `2.5s`, `1m`, `1h`) and `elapsed`'s (`1m05s`)."""
    total = 0
    number = ""
    for c in word.elems():
        if c.isdigit() or (c == "." and "." not in number):
            number += c
        elif c in ("h", "m", "s") and number and number != ".":
            total += int(number.partition(".")[0] or "0") * {"h": 3600, "m": 60, "s": 1}[c]
            number = ""
        else:
            return None
    return total if word and not number else None

def action_seconds(row: str) -> int:
    """How long a running action has run, in whole seconds, from the time
    Bazel's progress row gives it (`Compiling a.cc; 12s remote`, `…; 1m 30s
    local`, `1m05s` as `elapsed` writes it); 0 without one."""
    if "; " not in row:
        return 0
    total = 0
    for word in row.rpartition("; ")[2].split(" "):
        seconds = _word_seconds(word)
        if seconds == None:
            break
        total += seconds
    return total

def _middle(text: str, width: int) -> str:
    """`text` in `width` cells, cut in its middle (`…`) when longer, so its
    start and its end both show."""
    if text_width(text) <= width:
        return text
    if width < 8:
        return fit(text, width).rstrip(" ")
    left = (width - 1) * 2 // 3
    return _cell_slice(text, 0, left) + "…" + _tail(text, width - 1 - left)

def action_row(row: str, width: int) -> str:
    """A running action's row in `width` cells: how long it has run and
    where (`2s darwin-sandbox`, after its last `; `) at the right, its
    description cut in the middle to make room, as the part that tells a
    stuck action is its age."""
    head, sep, tail = row.rpartition("; ")
    if not sep or text_width(row) <= width or text_width(tail) + 4 > width:
        return _middle(row, width)
    room = width - text_width(tail) - 1
    head = _middle(head, room)
    return head + " " * (room - text_width(head)) + " " + tail

# The slowest actions the finished screen lists, of the most it keeps.
# The slowest actions kept for the finished pane, which scrolls through them.
_SLOWEST_KEPT = 50
_MAX_SLOW = 200

def note_progress(state: dict, progress: list):
    """Note how long each action Bazel shows running has run, the most
    seen, for the finished screen's slowest (`slowest_rows`), the last
    `[done / total]` with whether Bazel was still analysing
    (`build_progress`), and, as the count moves on, how much of the count
    before it no event has come for (`settled`: the local action cache's,
    for `_published_split`)."""
    at = status_row(progress)
    counts = progress_counts(progress[at]) if at >= 0 else None
    if counts:
        before = state.get("done_total")
        if before and before[0] != counts[0]:
            state["settled"] = max(0, before[0] - _events_seen(state))
        state["done_total"] = (counts[0], counts[1], analysing(progress))
    slowest = state.setdefault("slowest", {})
    for row in running_actions(progress):
        what = row.rpartition("; ")[0] or row
        slowest[what] = max(slowest.get(what, 0), action_seconds(row))
    if len(slowest) > _MAX_SLOW:
        kept = sorted(slowest.items(), key = lambda kv: -kv[1])[:_MAX_SLOW // 2]
        state["slowest"] = {what: secs for what, secs in kept}

def slowest_rows(state: dict) -> list:
    """What the finished screen's left pane shows in place of the running
    actions, the longest first: from the execution log, every spawn that ran
    (`note_spawn_time`), its mnemonic, target and wall time; without one, the
    slowest actions Bazel's progress area showed, in whole seconds
    (`slowest_title` says which), or, for a successful build its metrics
    say ran nothing, that nothing ran. Bazel's critical path, which they
    need not be on, is the Results pane's."""
    timed = state.get("spawn_slowest") or []
    if timed:
        return ["%s %s; %s" % (mnemonic, label, _tenths(ms)) for ms, mnemonic, label in sorted(timed, key = lambda t: -t[0])[:_SLOWEST_KEPT]]
    rows = []
    for what, secs in sorted(state.get("slowest", {}).items(), key = lambda kv: -kv[1])[:_SLOWEST_KEPT]:
        if secs > 0:
            rows.append("%s; %ds" % (what, secs))
    if not rows and _nothing_ran(state):
        return [_NOTHING_RAN]
    return rows

# The finished pane's row for a build that ran nothing, muted where drawn.
_NOTHING_RAN = "nothing ran: every action was cached"

# Run on every build whatever is cached (`UNCONDITIONAL_EXECUTION`): not a
# sign that anything of the build's own ran.
_ALWAYS_RUN = ["BazelWorkspaceStatusAction"]

def _nothing_ran(state: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether the build finished, successfully, with nothing of its own
    run: its `BuildMetrics` say no action executed but those Bazel runs on
    every build (`_ALWAYS_RUN`, the workspace status)."""
    inv = state.get("inv") or {}
    if state.get("building") or not build_succeeded(inv):
        return False
    summary = _get(inv.get("build_metrics"), "action_summary")
    executed = _get(summary, "actions_executed", None)
    if executed == None:
        return False
    for data in _get(summary, "action_data", None) or []:
        if str(_get(data, "mnemonic", "") or "") in _ALWAYS_RUN:
            executed -= _get(data, "actions_executed", 0) or 0
    return executed <= 0

def slowest_title(state: dict) -> str:
    """The finished pane's title: `Slowest` from the execution log, `Slowest
    seen` from what Bazel's progress area happened to show."""
    return "Slowest" if state.get("spawn_slowest") else "Slowest seen"

def note_spawn_time(state: dict, spawn):
    """Keep `spawn`, from the execution log, among the build's slowest by
    wall time (its execution's, else its whole time), when it ran, locally
    or remotely: a cache hit's time is its fetch, not the work, and one that
    did not execute has none. The `_SLOWEST_KEPT` longest are kept."""
    if _get(spawn, "cache_hit", False) or "cache hit" in str(_get(spawn, "runner", "") or ""):
        return
    metrics = _get(spawn, "metrics")
    ms = _get(metrics, "execution_wall_time_ms") or _get(metrics, "total_time_ms") or 0
    if ms <= 0:
        return
    timed = state.setdefault("spawn_slowest", [])
    timed.append((ms, str(_get(spawn, "mnemonic", "") or "action"), str(_get(spawn, "target_label", "") or "")))
    if len(timed) > 2 * _SLOWEST_KEPT:
        state["spawn_slowest"] = sorted(timed, key = lambda t: -t[0])[:_SLOWEST_KEPT]

def _tenths(ms: int) -> str:
    """`ms` as seconds to a tenth (`12.4s`), or minutes and seconds past a
    minute as `elapsed` writes them (`1m05s`)."""
    if ms >= 60000:
        return elapsed(ms)
    return "%d.%ds" % (ms // 1000, (ms % 1000) // 100)

def aged_time(row: str):
    """`(time, role)` for a running action's row: its time as Bazel wrote it
    (`12s`, `1m 30s`), or as `elapsed` does (`1m05s`), and the role it is drawn in by its age, as
    BuildBuddy's theme colours the seconds in Bazel's progress area:
    `muted` while young, `warning` once slow, `error` once stuck. None for a
    row with no time."""
    words = row.rpartition("; ")[2].split(" ") if "; " in row else []
    kept = []
    for word in words:
        # The words `action_seconds` reads: `1m`, `30s`, `2.5s`, `1m05s`.
        if _word_seconds(word) == None:
            break
        kept.append(word)
    if not kept:
        return None
    seconds = action_seconds(row)
    return (" ".join(kept), "error" if seconds >= _STUCK_S else ("warning" if seconds >= _SLOW_S else "muted"))

def _aged(row: str):
    """How a running action's row is coloured: its time alone, in the role
    `aged_time` gives it; the rest as it is. None for a row with no time."""
    aged = aged_time(row)
    if aged == None:
        return None
    took, role = aged

    def colour(text):
        at = text.rfind(took)
        if at < 0:
            return text
        return text[:at] + _c(role) + took + _RESET + text[at + len(took):]

    return colour

def cache_hits(line: str):
    """`(hits, processes)` from Bazel's closing tally (`INFO: 1,204
    processes: 1,000 remote cache hit, 200 internal, 4 darwin-sandbox.`):
    the cache hits of every kind, of the processes not internal to Bazel;
    None for any other line."""
    line = bazel_line(line)
    if not line.startswith("INFO: "):
        return None
    for sep in (" processes: ", " process: "):
        head, found, rest = line[len("INFO: "):].partition(sep)
        if found:
            break
    total = head.replace(",", "")
    if not found or not total.isdigit():
        return None
    hits = 0
    internal = 0
    for part in rest.rstrip(".").split(", "):
        n, _, what = part.partition(" ")
        n = n.replace(",", "")
        if not n.isdigit():
            return None
        if what.endswith("cache hit"):
            hits += int(n)
        elif what == "internal":
            internal += int(n)
    return (hits, int(total) - internal)

def _plain(s: str) -> str:
    """`s` without its colour sequences (`ESC [ ... m`)."""
    parts = s.split("\x1b[")
    out = parts[0]
    for part in parts[1:]:
        end = part.find("m")
        out += part[end + 1:] if end >= 0 else part
    return out

def note_phase(state: dict, line: str):
    """The task's phase and its latest update from one of the CLI's own
    lines, for the header while no build runs: `▸ Title` starts a phase (not
    the task's own `▸ Running …` line), `▹ text` updates it."""
    text = _plain(line).strip(" ")
    if text.startswith("▸ ") and not text.startswith("▸ Running "):
        state["phase_title"] = text[len("▸ "):]
        state["phase_text"] = ""
    elif text.startswith("▹ "):
        state["phase_text"] = text[len("▹ "):]

def phase_status(state: dict, bar = False) -> list:
    """What the header's right end may say, the most first, for it to show
    the first that fits. While a build runs and the bar is not drawn, how
    far it is, `4 of 14 actions done` from Bazel's progress line, `+` after
    the total while Bazel is still analysing and so may find more (it grows
    after too, as Bazel walks the graph), or before it counts, what it is
    doing (`loading`, `analyzing`, else `starting`); with the bar drawn,
    which has the count, Bazel's own status (`bazel_status`). Once the task
    has ended, only what its ✔/✗ cannot say, from the last build's
    `BuildFinished` (`interrupted`, or `ended without a result` when none
    came); the bar has its actions. Otherwise the task's phase and its
    latest update."""
    if state.get("building"):
        if bar:
            return bazel_status(state)
        done, total, more = build_progress(state)
        if done >= 0:
            return ["%s of %s%s actions done" % (_thousands(done), _thousands(total), "+" if more else "")]
        return [phase_word(state)]
    if state["ended"] and state.get("builds"):
        outcome = build_outcome(state["builds"][-1]["snapshot"].get("inv") or {})
        return [outcome] if outcome else []
    title = state.get("phase_title", "")
    text = state.get("phase_text", "")
    if title and text:
        return [title + " · " + text]
    return [title or text] if title or text else []

def phase_word(state: dict) -> str:
    """What a build is doing before it counts actions, from Bazel's
    progress area: `loading`, `analyzing`, else `starting`."""
    lower = bazel_line(state["progress"][0]).lower() if state["progress"] else ""
    for word in ("analyzing", "loading"):
        if word in lower:
            return word
    return "starting"

def bazel_status(state: dict) -> list:
    """Bazel's own status, the most first: its status line without the
    count (`16 actions, 15 running`), after its `Analyzing:` line while it
    analyses too; before it counts, its progress area's first line
    (`Loading: 3 packages loaded`), else nothing (the bar says
    `phase_word`)."""
    progress = state["progress"]
    at = status_row(progress)
    if at < 0:
        # Before it counts the bar says what it is doing; this, Bazel's line.
        first = bazel_line(progress[0]).strip() if progress else ""
        return [first] if first else []
    rest = bazel_line(progress[at]).partition("] ")[2].strip()
    if not analysing(progress):
        return [rest] if rest else []
    first = bazel_line(progress[0]).strip()
    return [first + " · " + rest, "analyzing · " + rest, "analyzing"] if rest else [first, "analyzing"]

def bep_progress(text: str):
    """The last `(done, total)` Bazel's console wrote in `text` (a build
    event `progress`'s stderr: every status line it drew, coloured), or
    None."""
    last = None
    for line in _plain(text).split("\n"):
        line = line.rpartition("\r")[2]
        at = line.find("[")
        counts = progress_counts(line[at:]) if at >= 0 else None
        if counts:
            last = counts
    return last

def build_progress(state: dict):
    """`(done, total, analysing)`: how far a build is, from Bazel's status
    line while it runs, else the last count in the event stream's progress
    (`bep_done_total`: every count Bazel drew, its last whole, where a
    frame may have caught an early one), else the last a frame drew
    (`note_progress`); `(-1, -1, False)` before it counts. Once it has
    ended, never analysing."""
    progress = state.get("progress") or []
    at = status_row(progress)
    counts = progress_counts(progress[at]) if state.get("building") and at >= 0 else None
    if counts:
        return (counts[0], counts[1], analysing(progress))
    bep = state.get("bep_done_total")
    if bep and not state.get("building"):
        return (bep[0], bep[1], False)
    kept = state.get("done_total")
    if not kept:
        return (-1, -1, False)
    return (kept[0], kept[1], kept[2] and bool(state.get("building")))

# Mnemonics of Bazel's actions that spawn nothing, for Metrics' list of
# internal kinds: some only (and `action_data` keeps Bazel's top twenty).
_INTERNAL_MNEMONICS = ["BazelWorkspaceStatusAction", "CppModuleMap", "ExecutableSymlink", "FileWrite", "Middleman", "RepoMappingManifest", "RunfilesTree", "SolibSymlink", "SourceSymlinkManifest", "Symlink", "SymlinkTree", "TemplateExpand"]

# Runners in `BuildMetrics` that ran nothing of their own: the total, the
# actions with no spawn, and Bazel's own action cache (counted apart).
_NOT_RUNNERS = ["total", "internal", "action cache hit"]

def _cache_runner(name: str) -> bool:
    """Whether a runner is a cache (`remote cache hit`, `disk cache hit`),
    not one that ran the spawn, nor Bazel's own action cache."""
    return name.endswith("cache hit") and name not in _NOT_RUNNERS

def running_count(state: dict) -> int:
    """How many actions a running build has running now, as its Running
    pane counts them (Bazel's status line, else the rows it lists); 0 once
    it has ended."""
    if not state.get("building"):
        return 0
    progress = state.get("progress") or []
    return max(len(running_actions(progress)), action_counts(progress)[0])

def target_rows(failed_role: str, failed: int, built: int, blocked: int, configured: int, room: int) -> list:
    """The narrow Targets rows in `room` cells: `Targets ✗5 ✓6 ○10/21`,
    whole parts only, every count `short_count` where whole ones don't
    fit a row, then the parts wrapped onto a second row under the first."""
    for short in [False, True]:
        n = short_count if short else _thousands
        parts = [
            (failed_role + "✗" + n(failed) + _RESET, "✗" + n(failed)),
            (_c("success") + "✓" + n(built) + _RESET, "✓" + n(built)),
        ] + ([("○" + n(blocked), "○" + n(blocked))] if blocked else [])
        parts[-1] = (parts[-1][0] + "/" + n(configured), parts[-1][1] + "/" + n(configured))
        rows = [[]]
        for drawn, plain in parts:
            if rows[-1] and 8 + text_width(" ".join([p for _, p in rows[-1]] + [plain])) > room:
                rows.append([])
            rows[-1].append((drawn, plain))
        if len(rows) == 1 or short:
            return [("Targets " if i == 0 else " " * 8) + " ".join([d for d, _ in row]) for i, row in enumerate(rows)]
    return []

def _has_tests(c: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether the build has test targets (configured, or with a result)."""
    return bool(c.get("test_targets") or c["tests_passed"] or c["tests_failed"] or c.get("tests_flaky") or c.get("tests_skipped"))

def _tests_words(c: dict) -> str:
    """The Tests row's counts in words, a part there is none of left out,
    then how many of them a cache gave (`2 passed, 1 failed (3 cached)`);
    `0 passed` before one finished."""
    parts = []
    if c["tests_passed"]:
        parts.append(_counts("%s%d passed%s", (_c("success"), c["tests_passed"], _RESET)))
    if c["tests_failed"]:
        parts.append(_counts("%s%d failed%s", (_c("error"), c["tests_failed"], _RESET)))
    if c.get("tests_flaky"):
        parts.append(_counts("%s%d flaky%s", (_c("warning"), c["tests_flaky"], _RESET)))
    if c.get("tests_skipped"):
        parts.append(_counts("%d skipped", c["tests_skipped"]))
    return (", ".join(parts) or "0 passed") + tests_cached_text(c)

def tests_cached_text(counts: dict) -> str:
    """` (5 cached)`, as Bazel says a test's result came from a cache: of
    the test targets finished so far (passed, failed or flaky), those every
    run of which a cache gave; empty for none."""
    finished = counts.get("tests_passed", 0) + counts.get("tests_failed", 0) + counts.get("tests_flaky", 0)
    cached = min(counts.get("tests_cached", 0), finished)
    return _counts(" (%d cached)", cached) if cached else ""

def _tests_said(row: str):
    """The tests Bazel's status line says it runs (`[10 / 52] 3 / 9 tests;
    …` gives 9), else None."""
    at = row.find(" tests")
    if at < 0 or "[" not in row[:at]:
        return None
    words = row[:at].split(" ")
    if len(words) < 3 or words[-2] != "/":
        return None
    total = words[-1].replace(",", "")
    return int(total) if total.isdigit() else None

def test_progress(state: dict):
    """`(running, pending)` test targets while a `test` build runs, None
    otherwise: running, the targets with a test action in Bazel's running
    list (`Testing //t:a (shard 2 of 3)`, a run or an attempt alike, each
    target once, not one with its result in: Bazel's list can lag it by a
    frame), at most the `--ui_actions_shown` Bazel lists (8 by default;
    the rest count as pending; `[Sched]` ones wait); pending, the tests
    Bazel's status line says it runs (`3 / 9 tests`, a total that grows
    as Bazel finds test targets, so at least those finished and running)
    less those with a result (passed, failed, flaky, skipped, or failed to
    build) and those running, never below 0; None while Bazel analyses
    and while its status line has given no test total (`--curses=no`
    between its lines, `--noshow_progress`)."""
    c = state.get("counts") or {}
    if not state.get("building") or not c.get("testing"):
        return None
    labels = {}
    done = c.get("test_finished") or {}
    unbuilt = c.get("test_unbuilt") or {}
    for row in running_actions(state.get("progress") or []):
        if row.startswith("Testing "):
            label = row[len("Testing "):].split(";")[0].split(" ")[0]
            if label not in done and label not in unbuilt:
                labels[label] = True
    running = len(labels)

    # Only Bazel's own count of the tests it runs (`3 / 9 tests`) says
    # what is pending: a filter (`--test_tag_filters`) takes out tests
    # that are configured all the same, and its total grows as Bazel
    # finds test targets, so never less than what is finished or running.
    progress = state.get("progress") or []
    at = status_row(progress)
    said = _tests_said(progress[at]) if at >= 0 else None
    if build_progress(state)[2] or said == None:
        return (running, None) if running else None
    finished = c.get("tests_passed", 0) + c.get("tests_failed", 0) + c.get("tests_flaky", 0) + c.get("tests_skipped", 0)
    total = max(said, finished + running)
    return (running, max(0, total - finished - running))

def action_split(state: dict) -> dict:
    """A build's actions by what became of each, for the bar, the Actions
    rows and Metrics, in one unit, Bazel's actions, summing to its done
    count: `done` of `total` (Bazel's `[n / m]`, `more` while it analyses),
    and of them `ran`, `local` (Bazel's action cache in the output base
    said the outputs were still valid), `disk` and `remote` (a cache
    served the action), `internal` (no spawn: symlinks, manifests, file
    writes), `failed` (the actions the event stream says failed,
    `failed_actions`), `blocked` (counted done but never run, as an action
    they need failed) and `other` (what fits none of them); `disk_on` and
    `remote_on` say which caches the build was given (`cache_kinds`).

    Once the build has ended with its `BuildMetrics` (`final`), `local` is
    `action_cache_statistics.hits` (else an `action cache hit` runner) and
    what the done count has past the parts (what Bazel already knew up to
    date in memory) but in a build that failed for more than its tests,
    `internal` `runner_count`'s internal ones, and the rest of
    `actions_executed` had spawns: by action from the execution log the
    Dashboard read (`log_kinds`: an action a cache hit where all its
    spawns were, one whose spawns differ `other`), else by
    `runner_count`'s spawns (which count a retried test's every attempt),
    clamped to the actions that spawned;
    what of `actions_executed` the log has no action for is `other` too. Bazel (7 to 9) counts a failed action among
    the internal ones, but one whose outputs were not created under the
    runner its spawn ran in (`counted_internal`): each is taken out of
    where it was counted; in a build that failed for more than its tests,
    what the done count has past the parts is `blocked`, what needed what
    failed (an action, a missing source). A failed build's total is at least what it created, and its
    bar never full of what did not fail (`action_bar`).

    While it runs with every action published (`--dashboard:actions=all`,
    the default), the live split is the events' and the execution log's
    (`_published_split`), `local` the done count's gap, `estimated`; with
    only the execution log, `ran`, `disk` and `remote` are its actions and
    `rest` what else is done, which only `BuildMetrics` tells apart. With
    no execution log to read (`own_execution_log`), only the done count is
    `known`. `counted` once there is a done count."""
    done, total, more = build_progress(state)
    c = state.get("counts") or {}
    inv = state.get("inv") or {}
    summary = _get(inv.get("build_metrics"), "action_summary")
    executed = _get(summary, "actions_executed")
    caches = cache_kinds(inv)
    split = dict(done = max(0, done), total = max(0, total, done), more = more, counted = done >= 0, known = False, final = False, estimated = False, ran = 0, local = 0, disk = 0, remote = 0, internal = 0, other = 0, rest = 0, failed = 0, blocked = 0, unreported = 0, failed_build = False, running = bool(state.get("building")), running_now = running_count(state), disk_on = caches[0], remote_on = caches[1])
    kinds = c.get("log_kinds") or {}
    logged = state.get("exec_log_read") and not own_execution_log(inv) and (kinds.get("ran", 0) + kinds.get("disk", 0) + kinds.get("remote", 0) + kinds.get("mixed", 0)) > 0
    if not state.get("building") and type(executed) == "int":
        internal, disk, remote, ran, hit_runner = 0, 0, 0, 0, 0
        for runner in _get(summary, "runner_count", None) or []:
            name = str(_get(runner, "name", "") or "")
            n = _get(runner, "count", 0) or 0
            if name == "internal":
                internal += n
            elif name == "action cache hit":
                hit_runner += n
            elif name == "disk cache hit":
                disk += n
            elif _cache_runner(name):
                remote += n
            elif name != "total":
                ran += n
        if logged:
            ran, disk, remote = kinds.get("ran", 0), kinds.get("disk", 0), kinds.get("remote", 0)
        internal = min(internal, executed)
        spawned = executed - internal
        disk = min(disk, spawned)
        remote = min(remote, spawned - disk)
        ran = min(ran, spawned - disk - remote)
        other = spawned - disk - remote - ran
        stats = _get(summary, "action_cache_statistics")
        local = (_get(stats, "hits", 0) or 0) if stats != None else hit_runner

        # A failed action is counted where it ran, or among the internal
        # ones; it is taken out of there.
        failures = failed_actions(state)
        failed = len(failures)
        from_internal = min(internal, len([r for r in failures if r.get("counted_internal") != False]))
        internal -= from_internal
        left = failed - from_internal
        for key in ("ran", "other", "disk", "remote"):
            have = {"ran": ran, "other": other, "disk": disk, "remote": remote}[key]
            took = min(left, have)
            left -= took
            if key == "ran":
                ran -= took
            elif key == "other":
                other -= took
            elif key == "disk":
                disk -= took
            else:
                remote -= took
        whole = ran + local + disk + remote + internal + failed + other
        succeeded = build_succeeded(inv)

        # What Bazel counted done past what it executed or found in its
        # action cache: in a build that failed (but for its tests), those
        # that needed what failed (an action, a missing source) and so
        # never ran; else what it already knew up to date in memory (a warm
        # server's rerun), the local action cache's too.
        gap = max(0, done - whole)
        outcome = str(_get(_get(inv.get("build_finished"), "exit_code"), "name", "") or "")
        blocked = gap if not succeeded and outcome != "TESTS_FAILED" else 0
        local += gap - blocked
        whole += gap
        split.update(counted = True, known = True, final = True, ran = ran, local = local, disk = disk, remote = remote, internal = internal, failed = failed, blocked = blocked, other = other, more = False, failed_build = not succeeded)
        split["done"] = max(done, whole)
        created = _get(summary, "actions_created", 0) or 0
        if succeeded:
            split["total"] = split["done"]
        else:
            split["total"] = max(split["total"], split["done"], created if done < 0 else 0)
        return split
    if done < 0 or own_execution_log(inv):
        return split
    if c.get("actions_seen") and state.get("building"):
        return _published_split(state, split, done)
    disk = min(kinds.get("disk", 0), done)
    remote = min(kinds.get("remote", 0), done - disk)
    ran = min(kinds.get("ran", 0) + kinds.get("mixed", 0), done - disk - remote)
    rest = done - ran - disk - remote

    # Ended without its BuildMetrics (the stream cut short): the actions
    # it said failed, out of the rest, then what ran.
    failed = 0 if state.get("building") else len(failed_actions(state))
    took = min(failed, rest)
    rest -= took
    ran = max(0, ran - (failed - took))
    split.update(known = True, ran = ran, disk = disk, remote = remote, rest = rest, failed = failed, done = max(done, ran + disk + remote + rest + failed), failed_build = failed > 0)
    split["total"] = max(split["total"], split["done"])
    return split

def _published_split(state: dict, split: dict, done: int) -> dict:
    """`action_split` while a build runs with every action it executes
    published (`--build_event_publish_all_actions`, the `actions` arg's
    default): the events say which ran no spawn (`internal`: no command
    line) and which spawned, each of those `ran` until the execution log
    says a cache served it (`disk`, `remote`) or its spawns differ
    (`other`); the actions that failed apart; what the done count has past
    them, with no event at all, is the local action cache's, `estimated`
    (it posts none), or `other` once an action failed (Bazel counts done
    the actions that needed it, which post none either). Events ahead of Bazel's count take from `ran`, never
    from the others. A gap is the action cache's only as far as it
    outlasted the count it opened at (`settled`, `note_progress`): Bazel's
    count can lead the events by a frame, and until it has, that part is
    `unreported` (done, its kind not said yet), so the estimate rarely
    drops and the done count is Bazel's."""
    c = state.get("counts") or {}
    kinds = c.get("log_kinds") or {}
    failed = len(failed_actions(state))
    internal = c.get("actions_internal", 0)
    spawned = max(0, c.get("actions_seen", 0) - internal)
    disk = min(kinds.get("disk", 0), spawned)
    remote = min(kinds.get("remote", 0), spawned - disk)
    other = min(kinds.get("mixed", 0), spawned - disk - remote)
    ran = spawned - disk - remote - other
    seen = ran + disk + remote + other + internal + failed
    if seen > done:
        cut = min(seen - done, ran)
        ran -= cut
        seen -= cut
    gap = max(0, done - seen)
    settled = min(gap, state.get("settled", 0))

    # Once an action failed, Bazel counts done those that needed it too,
    # which no event tells from the action cache's.
    local = settled if not failed else 0
    other += settled - local
    split.update(known = True, published = True, estimated = True, ran = ran, disk = disk, remote = remote, other = other, internal = internal, failed = failed, local = local, unreported = gap - settled, done = max(done, seen))
    split["total"] = max(split["total"], split["done"])
    return split

def cache_kinds(inv: dict) -> tuple:
    """`(disk, remote)`: whether the build was given a disk cache
    (`--disk_cache`) and a remote one (`--remote_cache` or
    `--remote_executor`), each flag's last value counting, from the whole
    command line Bazel parsed (rc files' flags in it); an empty value is
    none."""
    options = inv.get("options_parsed")
    flags = _get(options, "cmd_line") or _get(options, "explicit_cmd_line") or []
    last = {}
    for flag in flags:
        name, eq, value = str(flag).partition("=")
        if name in ("--disk_cache", "--remote_cache", "--remote_executor") and eq:
            last[name] = value != ""
    return (bool(last.get("--disk_cache")), bool(last.get("--remote_cache") or last.get("--remote_executor")))

def _events_seen(state: dict) -> int:
    """The actions a build's events have reported so far, every action
    published: those executed and those that failed."""
    return (state.get("counts") or {}).get("actions_seen", 0) + len(failed_actions(state))

def failed_actions(state: dict) -> list:
    """The build's actions its event stream reports failed, as the
    Failures list has them: not a test's (`TestRunner`, which Tests
    counts), nor a target's analysis failure."""
    return [r for r in state.get("fails") or [] if r.get("kind") == "action" and r.get("detail") != "TestRunner"]

def hit_count(split: dict) -> int:
    """The actions a cache answered: the local action cache's, the disk
    cache's and the remote one's."""
    return split["local"] + split["disk"] + split["remote"]

def hit_percent(split: dict):
    """`hit_count` of the done count, the actions the label counts, a
    whole percent, rounded to the nearest, but 1 for any and 99 for all
    but some, so only none says 0% and only every one 100%; None for no
    actions."""
    done = split["done"]
    if not done:
        return None
    hits = min(hit_count(split), done)
    pct = (hits * 200 + done) // (2 * done)
    if hits and not pct:
        return 1
    if hits < done and pct == 100:
        return 99
    return pct

def actions_text(split: dict) -> list:
    """A finished build's actions, the most first: `8,590 actions · 3,148
    cache hits (37%)` (`hit_percent`), shorter forms after; none before
    it counted."""
    if not split["counted"]:
        return []
    n = _thousands(split["done"])
    actions = n + (" action" if split["done"] == 1 else " actions")
    if not split["final"]:
        return [actions, n]
    hits = _thousands(hit_count(split))
    pct = hit_percent(split)
    said = " (%d%%)" % pct if pct != None else ""
    return ["%s · %s cache hits%s" % (actions, hits, said), "%s · %s hits%s" % (n, hits, said), actions, n]

# The bar's segments, in order: glyph (each its own shape, apart without
# colour), role, and the keys in `action_split` they sum. `rest` is the
# rest of the done count while a build runs with only the execution log;
# `failed` only a failed build's.
_SEGMENTS = [
    ("█", "accent", ["ran"]),
    ("▓", "success", ["disk", "remote"]),
    ("▒", "muted", ["local"]),
    ("▞", "muted", ["rest", "other"]),
    ("░", "subtle", ["internal"]),
    ("╳", "error", ["failed"]),
    ("○", "muted", ["blocked"]),
    ("┈", "border", ["unreported"]),
]

# A done count nothing tells apart: no execution log to read.
_DONE = ("━", "")

# Not done yet.
_UNDONE = ("─", "border")

def tests_text(progress) -> str:
    """`test_progress` in words as the Tests row has them, its `─` a
    coloured key: `─ 3 running · 5 pending`, a part there is none of left
    out; empty for none."""
    if not progress:
        return ""
    running, pending = progress
    parts = []
    if running:
        parts.append(_counts("%d running", running))
    if pending:
        parts.append(_counts("%d pending", pending))
    if not parts:
        return ""
    return _c(_UNDONE[1]) + _UNDONE[0] + _RESET + " " + " · ".join(parts)

def action_bar(split: dict, width: int) -> str:
    """`width` cells of a build's actions (`action_split`): a segment of
    each kind there is, in `_SEGMENTS` order, then what is not done (at
    least a cell of it for a failed build none of whose actions failed);
    the done count alone (`_DONE`) where nothing tells its kinds apart."""
    parts = []
    if split["known"]:
        for glyph, role, keys in _SEGMENTS:
            n = 0
            for key in keys:
                n += split[key]
            parts.append((n, glyph, role))
    else:
        parts = [(split["done"], _DONE[0], _DONE[1])]
    undone = max(0, split["total"] - split["done"])

    # A failed build with no action failed (analysis, a test, a cancel):
    # a cell of what it never reached, never a full bar.
    if split.get("failed_build") and not split["failed"] and not undone:
        undone = 1
    parts.append((undone, _UNDONE[0], _UNDONE[1]))
    cells = shares([n for n, _, _ in parts], width)
    if not _total(cells):
        return _c(_UNDONE[1]) + _UNDONE[0] * width + _RESET
    out = ""
    for (n, glyph, role), k in zip(parts, cells):
        if k:
            out += (_c(role) + glyph * k + _RESET) if role else glyph * k
    return out

def bar_label(state: dict, split: dict, cols: int) -> str:
    """The bar's right end: while a build runs, `3,830 of 8,563 actions`
    (`+` while Bazel analyses), `3,830/8,563+` where that is more than half
    the row; at its end, its actions (`actions_text`); before it counts,
    what it is doing (`phase_word`)."""
    if not split["counted"]:
        return phase_word(state)
    done, total = _thousands(split["done"]), _thousands(split["total"])
    if split["final"]:
        forms = actions_text(split)
    elif state.get("building"):
        more = "+" if split["more"] else ""
        forms = ["%s of %s%s actions" % (done, total, more), "%s/%s%s" % (done, total, more)]
    else:
        forms = ["%s of %s actions done" % (done, total), "%s/%s" % (done, total)]
    for form in forms:
        if text_width(form) <= cols // 2:
            return form
    return forms[-1]

def bar_row(state: dict, cols: int) -> str:
    """The row of the bar (`action_bar`) and its label (`bar_label`), as
    wide as the screen, for `state` (a build `build_view` picked)."""
    split = action_split(state)
    label = bar_label(state, split, cols)
    if text_width(label) > cols - 3:
        label = fit(label, max(0, cols - 3)).rstrip(" ")
    width = max(0, cols - 3 - text_width(label))
    return " " + action_bar(split, width) + " " + label + " " * max(0, cols - 2 - width - text_width(label))

def short_count(n: int) -> str:
    """`n` in at most four or five cells: `526`, `1.2k`, `48k`, `1.2M`,
    rounded down."""
    if n < 1000:
        return str(n)
    if n < 10000:
        return "%d.%dk" % (n // 1000, n % 1000 // 100)
    if n < 1000000:
        return "%dk" % (n // 1000)
    return "%d.%dM" % (n // 1000000, n % 1000000 // 100000)

def action_parts(split: dict) -> list:
    """The Actions rows' parts, each `(glyph, count, words, role, how)`,
    the bar's glyphs their legend, `how` "" or `dim` (a configured cache
    with no hits, at the end) or `estimate` (the local action cache while
    a build runs, `~`) or `muted` (`unreported`): ran; the cache hits (`hits`: the local action
    cache, always at the end; disk and remote where the build was given
    that cache, or hit it, live only once they have a hit, `▓` once before
    the first); failed, blocked, internal, `other` where there is any, and
    while it runs what Bazel counted done with no event yet (`? 3
    unreported`); while it
    runs with only the execution log, the rest of the done count as
    `other`; with nothing to tell them apart, the done count; while it
    runs, what is not done yet, the bar's `─`: the actions running now
    (`running_count`, at most what is not done) and the rest pending, so
    they sum to the bar's total; none before it counts."""
    if not split["counted"]:
        return []
    remaining = []
    undone = split["total"] - split["done"]
    if split.get("running") and undone > 0:
        now = min(split.get("running_now", 0), undone)
        remaining = [(_UNDONE[0], now, "running", _UNDONE[1], ""), ("" if now else _UNDONE[0], undone - now, "pending", "" if now else _UNDONE[1], "")]
    if not split["known"]:
        return [(_DONE[0], split["done"], "done", _DONE[1], "")] + remaining
    final = split["final"]
    parts = [("█", split["ran"], "ran", "accent", "")]
    if final or split["local"]:
        parts.append(("▒", split["local"], "local action cache", "muted", "estimate" if split.get("estimated") else ""))
    glyph = "▓"
    for key, on in (("disk", split["disk_on"]), ("remote", split["remote_on"])):
        if split[key] or (final and on):
            parts.append((glyph, split[key], key, "success", "" if split[key] else "dim"))
            glyph = ""
    if split["failed"]:
        parts.append(("╳", split["failed"], "failed", "error", ""))
    parts.append(("○", split["blocked"], "blocked", "muted", ""))
    parts.append(("░", split["internal"], "internal", "subtle", ""))
    parts.append(("▞", split["other"] + split["rest"], "other", "muted", ""))
    parts.append(("?", split["unreported"], "unreported", "", "muted"))
    return [p for p in parts if p[1] or p[4] == "dim" or (p[2] == "local action cache" and final)] + remaining

def _part_text(part: tuple, level: int) -> tuple:
    """`(plain, drawn)` of one part at `level` (0 whole, 1 `local`, 2
    `short_count` as well): its glyph coloured, a dimmed or estimated one
    muted, `~` before an estimate."""
    glyph, n, words, role, how = part
    if level >= 1 and words == "local action cache":
        words = "local"
    count = ("~" if how == "estimate" else "") + (_thousands(n) if level < 2 else short_count(n))
    plain = (glyph + " " if glyph else "") + count + " " + words
    if how:
        return (plain, _c("muted") + plain + _RESET)
    return (plain, ((_c(role) + glyph + _RESET) if role and glyph else glyph) + plain[len(glyph):])

_HITS = ["local action cache", "disk", "remote"]

# The Actions rows' parts in their order, by their words.
_PART_ORDER = [(n, True) for n in ["done", "ran", "local action cache", "disk", "remote", "failed", "blocked", "internal", "other", "unreported", "running", "pending"]]

# The live parts that keep their place at 0 once shown: glyph and role.
_HELD = {"unreported": ("?", ""), "running": ("─", "border"), "pending": ("", ""), "other": ("▞", "muted"), "internal": ("░", "subtle")}

# What goes first where the Actions rows have no room for every part: what
# counts nothing (the end's `0 local` too) and the caches with no hits,
# then internal, other, pending; then the hits become one total, which
# goes next; then running, unreported, ran, blocked; failed last of all.
_DROP_ORDER = ["zero", "internal", "other", "pending", "collapse", "hits", "running", "unreported", "ran", "blocked", "failed"]

def _drop(parts: list, k: int) -> list:
    """`parts` with `_DROP_ORDER`'s first `k` applied: each drops what it
    names, `zero` the parts that count nothing and the caches with no
    hits, `collapse` turns the hits into one `▒ 2,200 hits`, `hits` drops
    that."""
    out = list(parts)
    for what in _DROP_ORDER[:k]:
        if what == "zero":
            out = [p for p in out if (p[1] or p[4] == "held") and p[4] != "dim"]
        elif what == "collapse":
            hits = [p for p in out if p[2] in _HITS]
            if hits:
                total = 0
                for p in hits:
                    total += p[1]
                at = [i for i, p in enumerate(out) if p[2] in _HITS][0]
                out = [p for p in out[:at] if p[2] not in _HITS] + [("▒", total, "hits", "muted", "estimate" if [p for p in hits if p[4] == "estimate"] else "")] + [p for p in out[at:] if p[2] not in _HITS]
        else:
            out = [p for p in out if p[2] != what]
    return out

def action_results(split: dict, room: int, rows = 3, short = False, stack = False, memory = None) -> list:
    """The Actions rows, `room` cells wide past their `Actions` lead, at
    most `rows`: `█ ran`, then `cache hits N:` and the hits after it
    (`action_parts`), failed, blocked, internal, other, then what runs and
    is pending (`─ 2 running · 4 pending`, on one row where they fit),
    what follows the hits a row after them. Where the hits do not fit
    beside ran they get a row of their own, after ran and failed, as they
    do where `stack` (a screen under 70 columns), and wrap after their
    label where they are wider than a row, what follows going on with the
    last of them; their label is `hits N:` where `cache hits N:` fits no
    `rows`, none where that doesn't either, and none once no hit is left.
    The local action cache is `local` where `short` (a screen under 100
    columns) or the whole words fit no `rows`. Where nothing fits, the
    caches with no hits go, then internal; then the rows are packed tight
    and parts go in `_DROP_ORDER` (a zero count, internal, other, pending,
    then the hits as one `▒ N hits` total, then that, running, unreported,
    ran, blocked, and failed last), counts going to `short_count` before
    pending does. With `memory` (the build's, while the pane's size holds)
    each part is measured at the widest it has been and no fewer go than
    went before, so parts don't come and go as counts move. No part is
    cut in half but one wider than a row alone."""
    parts = action_parts(split)
    if not parts:
        return []

    # The end's rows are its own: no live drop memory carries over.
    if split["final"]:
        memory = None

    # A live part shown this build keeps its place at 0, dimmed, so the
    # parts don't come and go as Bazel's counts move (`? 0 unreported`).
    if memory != None:
        named = [p[2] for p in parts]
        for name in memory.get("shown", []):
            if name not in named and name in _HELD:
                glyph, role = _HELD[name]
                parts.append((glyph, 0, name, role, "held"))
        order = [n for n, _ in _PART_ORDER]
        parts = sorted(parts, key = lambda p: order.index(p[2]) if p[2] in order else len(order))
    hits_all = [p for p in parts if p[2] in _HITS]
    total = 0
    for p in hits_all:
        total += p[1]
    approx = "~" if [p for p in hits_all if p[4] == "estimate"] else ""

    # The hits' label: `cache hits N:`, else `hits N:`, else none.
    form = [0]

    def text(unit, level):
        # `(plain, drawn)` of a unit: its parts joined, the hits after
        # their label and total.
        texts = [_part_text(p, level) for p in unit[1]]
        plain = " · ".join([t[0] for t in texts])
        drawn = " · ".join([t[1] for t in texts])
        if unit[0] != "hits":
            return (plain, drawn)
        label = ["cache hits ", "hits ", ""][form[0]]
        if label:
            label += "%s%s:" % (approx, _thousands(total) if level < 2 else short_count(total))
        if not unit[1]:
            return (label, _c("muted") + label + _RESET)
        if not label:
            return (plain, drawn)
        return (label + " " + plain, _c("muted") + label + _RESET + " " + drawn)

    def width_of(row, level):
        width = text_width(" · ".join([text(u, level)[0] for u in row]))

        # What a part has been widest this build counts, so a part kept
        # doesn't go for a digit, and a part gone doesn't come and go.
        if memory != None:
            for u in row:
                for p in u[1]:
                    width += max(0, memory["widest"].get(p[2], 0) - text_width(_part_text(p, level)[0]))
        return width

    def pack(parts, level, fit, tight = False):
        # The units of `parts` on rows `fit` measures, or None past `rows`.
        hits = [p for p in parts if p[2] in _HITS]
        others = [p for p in parts if p[2] not in _HITS]
        lead = [("one", [p]) for p in others if p[2] == "ran"]
        failed = [("one", [p]) for p in others if p[2] == "failed"]
        tail = [("one", [p]) for p in others if p[2] not in ("ran", "failed", "running", "pending")]
        undone = [p for p in others if p[2] in ("running", "pending") and p[1]]
        rest = tail + ([("one", undone)] if undone and text_width(" · ".join([_part_text(p, fit)[0] for p in undone])) <= room else [("one", [p]) for p in undone])

        # The hits on as few rows as they fit: their label with as many as
        # fit after it, the rest after them.
        group = []
        if hits:
            k = len(hits)
            for _ in range(len(hits)):
                if k == 0 or width_of([("hits", hits[:k])], fit) <= room:
                    break
                k -= 1

            # A label with no hit after it fits nothing: a shorter one, or none.
            if k == 0 and form[0] < 2:
                return [[]] * (rows + 1)
            group = [("hits", hits[:k])] + [("hit", [p]) for p in hits[k:]]
        beside = tight or not group or (not stack and len(group) == 1 and width_of(lead + group, fit) <= room)
        packed = [[]]
        for unit in (lead + group + failed + rest) if beside else (lead + failed + group + rest):
            after = packed[-1] and packed[-1][-1][0] == "hits" and not tight
            if packed[-1] and ((not beside and unit[0] == "hits") or (after and unit[0] != "hit") or width_of(packed[-1] + [unit], fit) > room):
                packed.append([])
            packed[-1].append(unit)
        return packed

    def fits(packed, fit):
        return len(packed) <= rows and all([width_of(r, fit) <= room for r in packed])

    # Whole counts with every part, then without the caches with no hits,
    # then without internal too, then in thousands; the fewest rows that
    # fit, the wholest words and the longest label among them.
    no_dim = [p for p in parts if p[4] != "dim"]
    no_internal = [p for p in no_dim if p[2] != "internal"]
    stages = [(parts, [(0, 0), (1, 1)]), (no_dim, [(0, 0), (1, 1)]), (no_internal, [(0, 0), (1, 1), (1, 2)])]
    best = None
    for kept, levels in stages:
        for f in range(3):
            for level, fit in levels:
                if short and level == 0:
                    continue
                form[0] = f
                packed = pack(kept, level, fit)
                if fits(packed, fit) and (best == None or len(packed) < len(best[1])):
                    best = (fit, packed, f)
            if best != None:
                break
        if best != None:
            break
    if best == None:
        # Parts go in `_DROP_ORDER`, as few as will do (never fewer than
        # went before at this size, `memory`), the rows packed tight;
        # counts go to thousands before what says what happened, and the
        # hits become one total before any goes.
        start = memory["dropped"] if memory != None else 0
        for k in range(start, len(_DROP_ORDER) + 1):
            kept = _drop(parts, k) or [p for p in parts if p[2] == "failed"][:1]
            for fit in ([1] if k < 3 else [1, 2]):
                for f in range(3):
                    form[0] = f
                    packed = pack(kept, 1, fit, tight = True)
                    if len(packed) <= rows and all([width_of(r, fit) <= room or len(r) == 1 for r in packed]):
                        best = (fit, packed, f)
                        if memory != None:
                            memory["dropped"] = k
                        break
                if best != None:
                    break
            if best != None:
                break
        if best == None:
            form[0] = 2
            best = (2, pack(kept, 1, 2, tight = True)[:rows], 2)
    fit, packed, f = best
    form[0] = f
    if memory != None:
        for row in packed:
            for u in row:
                for p in u[1]:
                    memory["widest"][p[2]] = max(memory["widest"].get(p[2], 0), text_width(_part_text(p, fit)[0]))
                    if p[2] not in memory.setdefault("shown", []):
                        memory["shown"].append(p[2])
    out = []
    for row in packed:
        drawn = fit if width_of(row, fit) <= room else 2
        line = ""
        for u in row:
            piece = text(u, drawn)

            # A part wider than the row is drawn alone, cut at the box.
            if line and text_width(_plain(line) + " · " + piece[0]) > room:
                break
            line += (" · " if line else "") + piece[1]
        out.append(line)
    return out

def build_outcome(inv: dict) -> str:
    """What a finished build's `BuildFinished` says that ✔/✗ cannot:
    `interrupted`, `ended without a result` without one, else nothing."""
    finished = inv.get("build_finished")
    if finished == None:
        return "ended without a result"
    name = str(_get(_get(finished, "exit_code"), "name", "") or "")
    return "interrupted" if name == "INTERRUPTED" else ""

def build_succeeded(inv: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether a build's `BuildFinished` says it succeeded: one that crashed
    or was cut off without it, or was interrupted, did not."""
    finished = inv.get("build_finished")
    return finished != None and str(_get(_get(finished, "exit_code"), "name", "") or "") == "SUCCESS"

def end_build(state: dict, now_ms: int):
    """A build's end, inside `wait()`: its execution log was read to its end
    before then, so the build folded to a row (`fold_build`; the task goes
    on, and its finished screen shows its builds) keeps that it was."""
    state["exec_log_read"] = True
    fold_build(state, now_ms)
    state["building"] = False

def fold_build(state: dict, now_ms: int):
    """Keep the build that just ended as a folded row (`build_row`) and what
    its tabs show, for `<`/`>` to pick it again."""
    failed, blocked = _failure_counts(state)
    started = state.get("inv", {}).get("build_started")
    state.setdefault("builds", []).append(dict(
        command = str(_get(started, "command", "") or "") or state.get("build_command", "") or "bazel",
        actions = actions_text(action_split(dict(state, building = False))),
        failed = failed,
        blocked = blocked,
        ok = failed == 0 and not state["counts"]["tests_failed"] and not state.get("build_failed") and build_succeeded(state.get("inv") or {}),
        outcome = build_outcome(state.get("inv") or {}),
        ms = max(0, now_ms - state.get("build_start_ms", now_ms)),
        snapshot = dict({k: state.get(k) for k in _BUILD_KEYS}, building = False, blocked_cache = None, entries_cache = None, own_cache = None, ties_cache = None, excerpts = {}),
    ))

# What a folded build keeps for its tabs.
_BUILD_KEYS = ["counts", "inv", "slowest", "spawn_slowest", "critical", "pages", "fails", "outputs", "test_counts", "last_attempts", "spread", "root_causes", "bes_base", "bazel_uuid", "exec_log_read", "done_total", "bep_done_total", "log_path", "bazel_errors"]

def build_row(build: dict, cols: int) -> str:
    """A folded build's row: its verdict, command, its actions as the
    finished bar says them (`actions_text`: from its `BuildMetrics`, else
    the last count Bazel drew), failures and time, muted but for the
    counts' colours."""
    mark = (_c("verdict.success") + "✓") if build["ok"] else (_c("verdict.failure") + "✗")
    parts = [build["command"]]
    if build.get("actions"):
        parts.append(build["actions"][0])
    if build["failed"]:
        parts.append(_c("error") + _counts("✗%d", build["failed"]) + _RESET + _c("muted"))
    if build["blocked"]:
        parts.append(_counts("○%d", build["blocked"]))
    if build.get("outcome"):
        parts.append(build["outcome"])
    parts.append(elapsed(build["ms"]))
    row = " " + mark + _RESET + " " + _c("muted") + " · ".join(parts)
    return _cut_escaped(row, cols) + _RESET

def _counts(template: str, args) -> str:
    """`template % args` with every count in `args` (a tuple, or one value)
    written as `_thousands` writes it: `%d` takes a count's text."""
    values = args if type(args) == "tuple" else (args,)
    return template.replace("%d", "%s") % tuple([_thousands(v) if type(v) == "int" else v for v in values])

def _thousands(n: int) -> str:
    """`n` with commas between its thousands, as Bazel writes its counts."""
    digits = str(n)
    out = ""
    for i, d in enumerate(digits.elems()):
        if i and (len(digits) - i) % 3 == 0:
            out += ","
        out += d
    return out

def panes_shown(state: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether the build panes are open: while a build runs, and on the
    finished screen of a task that ran one build of its own (`build`,
    `test`); else folded to a row a build, unless `p` asked for them."""
    if not built(state):
        return False
    if state.get("panes") != None:
        return state["panes"]
    if state.get("building") or not state.get("builds"):
        return True
    return state["ended"] and state.get("task") in _ONE_BUILD_KINDS and len(state["builds"]) == 1

def built(state: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether a build has started (or left anything to show): before one,
    the build tabs are muted and say so."""
    c = state.get("counts") or {}
    return bool(state.get("building") or state.get("builds") or state.get("fails") or state.get("outputs") or c.get("configured") or c.get("failures"))

def _header(state: dict, cols: int, bar: bool) -> str:
    """The header: the task, how long it has run, and at its right end the
    first of `phase_status` that fits whole (`bar` whether the bar is
    drawn, which then has the count)."""
    spin = _SPINNER[state["frames"] % len(_SPINNER)] if not state["ended"] else ("✔" if state.get("verdict", True) else "✗")
    took = elapsed(state["now_ms"] - state.get("task_start_ms", state["start_ms"]))
    title = " %s aspect %s · %s " % (spin, state["task"], took)

    # The task and how long it has run whole; the status in what is left,
    # shortened first.
    title = fit(title, min(text_width(title), cols)).rstrip(" ")
    if text_width(title) < cols:
        title += " "
    room = max(0, cols - text_width(title))

    # Whole or not at all: a status cut to a bare number says nothing.
    right = ""
    for shown in phase_status(state, bar):
        if shown and text_width(shown) <= room - 2:
            right = shown + " "
            break

    # A status bar: the whole row, the time it has run included, in the
    # theme's `status_bar`.
    pad = " " * max(0, room - text_width(right))
    return _c("status_bar") + title + pad + right + _RESET

def _top(state: dict, cols: int, rows: int) -> list:
    """The rows above the console: the header (`_header`), then, unless
    zoomed (`z`) or showing the keys (`?`), the builds that ended folded to
    a row each, and while the panes are open, the bar of the build's
    actions (`bar_row`, the picked build's), the links, and the running
    actions, oldest first and coloured by age, beside the Results pane, the
    list (failures or actions) under its counts."""
    out = [""]
    if state.get("zoom") or state.get("help"):
        out[0] = _header(state, cols, False)
        return out

    # The builds that ended, a row each, the newest last, the older
    # counted; not the one whose panes are open.
    open_panes = panes_shown(state)
    folded = list(state.get("builds", []))
    if open_panes and not state.get("building") and folded:
        folded = folded[:-1]
    if len(folded) > _MAX_FOLDED:
        out.append(_c("muted") + fit(_counts(" +%d earlier", (len(folded) - _MAX_FOLDED + 1)), cols) + _RESET)
        folded = folded[len(folded) - _MAX_FOLDED + 1:]
    state["folded_at"] = len(out)
    for b in folded:
        out.append(build_row(b, cols))
    state["folded_rows"] = len(folded)
    out[0] = _header(state, cols, False)
    if not open_panes:
        return out

    # The panes get what the console pane and the keys row leave (the bar,
    # while a build runs or once it counted, and the links row going first
    # under `_SHORT_ROWS`), and go when that is not two rows.
    limit = rows - len(out) - 2 - 4
    if limit < 2:
        return out
    view = build_view(state)
    if rows >= _SHORT_ROWS and limit >= 3 and (state.get("building") or action_split(view)["counted"]):
        limit -= 1
        out.append(bar_row(view, cols))
        out[0] = _header(state, cols, True)

    if state["pages"] and (rows >= _SHORT_ROWS or limit >= 6):
        limit -= 1
        out.append(links_row(state["pages"], cols))

    # Running actions | targets and tests.
    actions = sorted(running_actions(state["progress"]), key = lambda r: -action_seconds(r))
    running, started = action_counts(state["progress"])
    running = max(len(actions), running)
    finished = state["ended"] and not actions and (state.get("slowest") or state.get("critical"))
    if finished:
        actions = slowest_rows(state)
    right_w = min(46 if cols >= 140 else 44, max(cols // 3, 39 if cols >= 80 else (32 if cols >= 70 else cols // 2)))
    left_w = cols - right_w
    c = state["counts"]
    built_n, targets_failed, targets_blocked = target_counts(state)
    failed = _c("error") if targets_failed else ""
    tests_failed = _c("error") if c["tests_failed"] else ""
    blocked_text_ = (_counts(", %d blocked", targets_blocked)) if targets_blocked else ""
    summary = [
        _counts("Targets  %d configured", c["configured"]),
        _counts("         %s%d built%s, %s%d failed%s%s", (_c("success"), built_n, _RESET, failed, targets_failed, _RESET, blocked_text_)),
    ] + (["Tests    " + _tests_words(c)] if _has_tests(c) else [])
    wide = max([text_width(_plain(r)) for r in summary]) <= right_w - 4
    if not wide:
        # Narrow: the failures first, as what matters is what failed.
        summary = target_rows(failed, targets_failed, built_n, targets_blocked, c["configured"], right_w - 4) + ([
            "Tests   " + " ".join([p for p in [
                _counts("%s✗%d%s", (tests_failed, c["tests_failed"], _RESET)) if c["tests_failed"] else "",
                _counts("%s✓%d%s", (_c("success"), c["tests_passed"], _RESET)) if c["tests_passed"] or not (c["tests_failed"] or c.get("tests_flaky") or c.get("tests_skipped")) else "",
                _counts("%s~%d%s", (_c("warning"), c["tests_flaky"], _RESET)) if c.get("tests_flaky") else "",
                _counts("⊘%d", c["tests_skipped"]) if c.get("tests_skipped") else "",
            ] if p]) + tests_cached_text(c),
        ] if _has_tests(c) else [])

    # As tall as the most Results rows there can be, for the whole build;
    # the list scrolls in it (`actions_scroll`, the wheel over it), and
    # unscrolled counts the rest, Bazel's unlisted ones too, on its last row.
    pane = max(1, min(limit, rows // 3, results_capacity(wide)))

    # Tests running and pending while a build runs, the actions' `─`
    # words: on the Tests row where it has room, else on a row under it.
    tests_rows = []
    tests_now = tests_text(test_progress(state))
    if tests_now:
        indent = " " * (9 if wide else 8)
        if text_width(_plain(summary[-1]) + " · " + _plain(tests_now)) <= right_w - 4:
            summary[-1] += " · " + tests_now
        elif text_width(indent + _plain(tests_now)) <= right_w - 4:
            tests_rows = [indent + tests_now]
        else:
            # Too narrow for one row: running on one, pending under it.
            tests_rows = [indent + part for part in tests_now.split(" · ")]

    # The build's actions, one unit with the bar's, its glyphs their keys;
    # the critical path; the failures, whose row a click takes to them.
    lead = "Actions  " if wide else "Actions "
    split = action_split(state)
    critical = (("Critical path %s" if wide else "Crit %s") % state["critical"]) if state.get("critical") else None
    failed_n, blocked_n = _failure_counts(state)
    failed_row = None
    if failed_n or blocked_n:
        failed_row = _counts("Failed   %s✗ %d%s%s", (_c("error"), failed_n, _RESET, (_counts("  ○ %d blocked", blocked_n)) if blocked_n else "")) if wide else _counts("%s✗%d%s ○%d", (_c("error"), failed_n, _RESET, blocked_n))

    # The actions get every row the pane has free; too many for it (a short
    # terminal): the critical path goes first, then the actions fold to
    # fewer rows, then the tests' running rows; the failures' row stays,
    # last.
    most = max(1, pane - len(summary) - len(tests_rows) - (1 if failed_row != None else 0))

    # Too narrow for a part beside the lead (40 columns): the lead on a
    # row of its own, the parts under it.
    apart = right_w - 4 - len(lead) < 12
    for _ in range(most + 4):
        room_now = right_w - 5 if apart else right_w - 4 - len(lead)
        rows_now = max(1, most - 1) if apart else most
        actions_rows = action_results(split, room_now, rows_now, short = cols < 100, stack = cols < 70, memory = None if split["final"] else _actions_memory(state, room_now, rows_now, pane))
        if apart and actions_rows:
            actions_rows = [""] + actions_rows
        wanted = len(summary) + len(actions_rows) + len(tests_rows) + len([r for r in (critical, failed_row) if r != None])
        if wanted <= pane:
            break
        if critical != None:
            critical = None
        elif most > 1:
            most -= 1
        elif tests_rows:
            tests_rows = []
        else:
            break
    rest = tests_rows + [(("Actions" if apart else lead) if i == 0 else (" " if apart else " " * len(lead))) + row for i, row in enumerate(actions_rows)] + ([critical] if critical != None else [])
    summary = (summary + rest)[:max(0, pane - (1 if failed_row != None else 0))]
    failed_i = -1
    if failed_row != None:
        failed_i = len(summary)
        summary.append(failed_row)

    listed, edge = scrolled_rows(actions, pane, state.get("actions_scroll", 0), 0 if finished else running)
    state["actions_scroll"] = min(state.get("actions_scroll", 0), max(0, len(actions) - pane))
    actions = listed
    results = "Results"

    # Started but not yet running (queued for a remote executor or a full
    # local pool) are counted beside the running ones, as Bazel's line has.
    head = "Running (%d of %d started)" % (running, started) if started > running else "Running (%d)" % running
    if finished:
        head = slowest_title(state)
    out.append(_box_top(head, left_w) + _box_top(results, right_w))

    # Where the failures' count is drawn, for a click to show their tab;
    # where the list is, for the wheel over it.
    state["failed_at"] = None
    state["actions_at"] = (len(out), len(out) + pane - 1, left_w)
    for i in range(pane):
        if i == failed_i:
            state["failed_at"] = (len(out), left_w)
        a = actions[i] if i < len(actions) else ""
        s = summary[i] if i < len(summary) else ""

        # `summary` rows carry colour: pad them by their visible length.
        visible = text_width(_plain(s))
        s_cell = _c("border") + "│ " + _RESET + s + " " * max(0, right_w - 4 - visible) + _c("border") + " │" + _RESET
        if visible > right_w - 4:
            s_cell = _box_row(_plain(s), right_w)
        colour = (lambda text: _c("muted") + text + _RESET) if a == _NOTHING_RAN else _aged(a)
        out.append(_box_row(action_row(a, left_w - 4), left_w, colour, edge = edge[i] if edge else None) + s_cell)
    out.append(_box_bottom(left_w) + _box_bottom(right_w))

    return out

# The Results rows a build may come to have past its targets and tests: the
# tests running and pending (one or two where the Tests row has no room for
# them), its actions (as many as its free rows hold: ran, the cache hits,
# the rest, what runs and is pending), critical path and failures.
_OPTIONAL_RESULTS = 8

def _actions_memory(state: dict, room: int, rows: int, pane: int) -> dict:
    """The build's Actions-rows `memory` (`action_results`): what each part
    has been widest, which have been shown and how many stages have gone,
    holding while the Results pane (`room` cells, `pane` rows) is no
    bigger; the next build, or a bigger pane, starts over."""
    memory = state.get("actions_memory")
    if memory == None or room > memory["room"] or pane > memory["pane"] or memory["build"] != state.get("build_start_ms"):
        memory = {"room": room, "pane": pane, "widest": {}, "dropped": 0, "shown": [], "build": state.get("build_start_ms")}
    memory.update(room = room, pane = pane)
    state["actions_memory"] = memory
    return memory

def results_capacity(wide: bool) -> int:
    """The most rows the Results pane can have: targets (two rows wide) and
    tests, then every optional one (`_OPTIONAL_RESULTS`). The panes are
    that tall for the whole build, so nothing below them moves."""
    return (3 if wide else 2) + _OPTIONAL_RESULTS

def scrolled_rows(rows: list, height: int, scroll: int, total = 0):
    """`(shown, edge)`: `rows` scrolled `scroll` from the top in a pane
    `height` tall, and its scrollbar (None when they fit). Unscrolled with
    more than fit, the last shown row counts the rest, of `total` when that
    is more (rows Bazel did not list)."""
    total = max(total, len(rows))
    scroll = max(0, min(scroll, max(0, len(rows) - height)))
    edge = scrollbar(len(rows), height, max(0, len(rows) - scroll - height))
    if scroll == 0 and total > height:
        shown = rows[:height - 1]
        return (shown + [_counts("… and %d more", (total - len(shown)))], edge)
    return (rows[scroll:scroll + height], edge)

def shown_tips(state: dict) -> list:
    """The Tips tab's rows, most urgent first: the task's tips the screen
    shows (`SURFACE_DASHBOARD`), and in its place each silenced this run,
    as `(tip, silenced)`."""
    silenced = state.get("silenced_tips", {})
    stored = state.get("tips", [])
    ids = [t["id"] for t in stored]

    # A tip silenced and then undone here: the Tips feature dropped it on
    # silencing, so it is shown from what was kept until it is stored again.
    restored = [t for id, t in state.get("restored_tips", {}).items() if id not in ids and id not in silenced]
    rows = [(t, False) for t in stored + restored if t["id"] not in silenced]
    rows += [(t, True) for t in silenced.values()]
    return sorted(rows, key = lambda r: severity_rank(r[0].get("severity")))

def tips_count(state: dict) -> int:
    """How many tips the tab shows, the silenced not counted."""
    return len([1 for _, silenced in shown_tips(state) if not silenced])

def tips_role(state: dict) -> str:
    """The role the tab's count is drawn in: its most urgent tip's."""
    rows = [t for t, silenced in shown_tips(state) if not silenced]
    return "tip." + rows[0].get("severity", "note") if rows else "muted"

def tips_lines(state: dict, width: int) -> list:
    """The Tips tab: each tip as `TIP[id] severity: title`, the chosen one
    lit with its body under it, a silenced one muted saying so (or why it
    could not be), then how to silence a tip."""
    rows = shown_tips(state)
    if not rows:
        return [_c("muted") + "No tips" + _RESET]
    at = max(0, min(state.get("tip_selected", 0), len(rows) - 1))
    out = []
    for i, (tip, silenced) in enumerate(rows):
        mark = "▸ " if i == at else "  "
        error = state.get("silence_errors", {}).get(tip["id"])
        if silenced:
            line = _c("muted") + "%sTIP[%s] silenced on this machine · s to undo: %s" % (mark, tip["id"], tip.get("title", "")) + _RESET
        else:
            severity = tip.get("severity", "note")
            line = "%s%s[%s]%s %s%s%s: %s" % (mark + _c("tip.label") + "TIP" + _RESET, _c("tip.id"), tip["id"], _RESET, _c("tip." + severity), severity, _RESET, tip.get("title", ""))
        out.append(line)
        if error:
            out.append("    " + _c("error") + "couldn't save to ~/.aspect/config.json: " + error + _RESET)
        if i == at and not silenced:
            for body in (tip.get("body") or "").split("\n"):
                if body:
                    out.append("    " + _c("muted") + body + _RESET)
    out += ["", _c("muted") + "s silences a tip on this machine · for everyone: --tips:silence=<id> or .aspect/config.axl · aspect.build/go/tips" + _RESET]
    return out

def toggle_silence(state: dict, row):
    """Silence the tip of `row` on this machine, or undo it, through
    `state["silencer"]` (`silence_tip`/`unsilence_tip`, `~/.aspect/config.json`);
    a write that fails says why on its row and changes nothing."""
    tip, silenced = row
    silencer = state.get("silencer")
    if silencer == None:
        return
    error = silencer(tip["id"], not silenced)
    errors = state.setdefault("silence_errors", {})
    if error:
        errors[tip["id"]] = error
        return
    errors.pop(tip["id"], None)
    gone = state.setdefault("silenced_tips", {})
    restored = state.setdefault("restored_tips", {})
    if silenced:
        gone.pop(tip["id"], None)
        restored[tip["id"]] = tip
    else:
        gone[tip["id"]] = tip
        restored.pop(tip["id"], None)

def console_height(state: dict, cols: int, rows: int) -> int:
    """How many lines the console shows at `cols` by `rows`: a page, for the
    keys that scroll by one."""
    return max(1, rows - len(_top(state, cols, rows)) - 3)

def render(state: dict, cols: int, rows: int) -> str:
    """The whole screen for `state`, `cols` by `rows`, as one string: `_top`,
    then the console pane (the console or a log, scrolled, or the keys while
    `?` shows them) and the search bar, or the keys row while it is closed,
    the keys shown or the countdown running."""
    out = _top(state, cols, rows)

    # Which console rows hold which folded line: none but the console's.
    state["row_lines"] = {}
    if state.get("tab", "console") != "console" and state["view"] == None and not state.get("help"):
        return _frame_of(state, _tab_pane(state, out, cols, rows), rows, cols)

    # The console, scrolled; the keys over whatever is open, which is left
    # as it is for when they close.
    at_top = False
    view = None if state.get("help") else state["view"]
    search = state["search"]
    log = shown_lines(state)
    log_h = max(1, rows - len(out) - 3)
    pattern = search["pattern"]
    scroll = 0
    body_w = max(1, cols - 4)
    wrapping = state.get("wrap", True) and not state.get("help") and not (view != None and view.get("peek"))
    if state.get("help"):
        # From the row ↑↓ have scrolled them to, a last row counting the rest
        # when they do not fit; the console's scroll left as it is.
        log = help_rows(state, body_w)
        top = min(state.get("help_top", 0), max(0, len(log) - log_h))
        state["help_top"] = top
        log = log[top:]
        if len(log) > log_h:
            rest = len(log) - log_h + 1
            log = log[:log_h - 1] + [_counts("… %d more: ↓", rest)]
        pattern = None
        scroll = max(0, len(log) - log_h)
    else:
        # Scrolled no further than the top: wrapped, as far as the oldest
        # lines that fill the pane.
        top_lines = log_h
        if wrapping:
            filled = 0
            for i, line in enumerate(log):
                filled += len(wrap_line(line, body_w))
                if filled >= log_h:
                    top_lines = i + 1
                    break
        state["scroll"] = min(state["scroll"], max(0, len(log) - top_lines))
        scroll = state["scroll"]
        at_top = scroll > 0 and scroll == max(0, len(log) - top_lines)
        if scroll == 0:
            state["unseen"] = 0
            state["unseen_errors"] = 0
        if search["back"] >= len(log):
            search["back"] = -1
    end = len(log) - scroll
    end = max(min(end, len(log)), min(len(log), log_h))
    start = max(0, end - log_h)
    jumped = len(log) - 1 - search["back"] if search["back"] >= 0 and pattern != None and search["mode"] == "jump" and not state.get("help") else -1
    centred = False
    if jumped >= 0 and state.get("last_size") not in (None, (cols, rows)):
        # Resized under a held match: centred on it again.
        search["centre"] = True
    state["last_size"] = (cols, rows)
    if wrapping:
        # The lines whose rows fill the pane up to `end`; at the top, the
        # oldest from their first row on.
        end = max(0, min(len(log), len(log) - scroll))
        filled = 0
        start = end
        for i in range(end - 1, -1, -1):
            # At the top, every line from the oldest: one taller than the
            # pane does not hide those above it.
            if filled >= log_h and not at_top:
                break
            filled += len(wrap_line(log[i], body_w))
            start = i
        for i in range(end, len(log)):
            if filled >= log_h:
                break
            filled += len(wrap_line(log[i], body_w))
            end = i + 1

        # A match jumped to, scrolled to in lines, may sit above the rows
        # that fill the pane: centred on it in rows instead.
        if jumped >= 0 and search.get("centre") and (jumped <= start or jumped >= end) and jumped < len(log):
            filled = len(wrap_line(log[jumped], body_w))
            start = jumped
            for i in range(jumped - 1, -1, -1):
                rows_i = len(wrap_line(log[i], body_w))
                if filled + rows_i > log_h // 2:
                    break
                filled += rows_i
                start = i

            # Below it only whole lines, so the next frame, filling back
            # from `end`, has it in view still.
            end = jumped + 1
            for i in range(jumped + 1, len(log)):
                rows_i = len(wrap_line(log[i], body_w))
                if filled + rows_i > log_h:
                    break
                filled += rows_i
                end = i + 1
            state["scroll"] = len(log) - end
            centred = True

            # Until a key moves the view, a frame keeps the match's first
            # row in it.
            search["anchored_view"] = True
    search["centre"] = False
    up = len(log) - end
    held = search["back"] >= 0 and search["pattern"] != None and search["mode"] == "jump" and view == None
    where = ("on the match, J to follow" if held else "following") if scroll == 0 else "%d line%s up" % (up, "" if up == 1 else "s")
    if state.get("unseen") and view == None:
        errors = state.get("unseen_errors", 0)
        where += _counts(" · %d new%s, J to follow", (state["unseen"], (_counts(" (%d error%s)", (errors, "" if errors == 1 else "s"))) if errors else ""))
    if state.get("dropped") and view == None:
        where += _counts(" · %d older not kept", state["dropped"])

    # The pattern finding or filtering, which a query that does not compile
    # is not; a match found, which of them it is, oldest first.
    found = ""
    matches = len(log)
    current = len(log) - 1 - search["back"] if search["back"] >= 0 and pattern != None and search["mode"] == "jump" else -1
    if pattern != None and search["mode"] == "jump":
        matches = len(pattern.filter(log))
        found = " · /%s/ %s%d" % (pattern.pattern, ("%d of " % len(pattern.filter(log[:current + 1]))) if current >= 0 else "", matches)
    elif pattern != None:
        found = " · /%s/ %d" % (pattern.pattern, matches)
    name = ("the end" if view["cut"] and not view.get("peek") else "") if view != None else "Console"
    title = "%s%s%s · %s" % (name, _FILTER_TITLES[state["filter"]], found, where)
    if state.get("help"):
        title = "Keys · ↑↓ scroll · any other key closes"
        out.append(_box_top(title, cols))
    else:
        # The tab names the console; its status follows.
        status = title.removeprefix("Console").lstrip(" ·")
        out.append(tab_top(state, status, cols, len(out)))
    state["pane"] = (len(out), len(out) + log_h - 1)
    line_links = {} if state.get("help") else state.get("line_links", {})
    shown = log[start:end]
    colour = _console_row(state, pattern)
    colour_current = _console_row(state, pattern, True)
    bar_edge = scrollbar(len(log), log_h, up) if not state.get("help") else None
    drawn = []
    drawn_at = []
    first_row = -1
    for i, line in enumerate(shown):
        links = line_links.get(line)
        if view != None and view.get("peek"):
            # A peek's rows, highlighted, cut as `fit` cut their text.
            drawn.append((line, _peek_paint(view["hl"][start + i]), False, None, False))
            drawn_at.append(start + i)
            continue
        if not wrapping:
            drawn.append((line, (colour_current if start + i == current else colour)(line), start + i == current, links, False))
            drawn_at.append(start + i)
            continue
        kind = severity(line)
        if start + i == jumped:
            first_row = len(drawn)
        for j, (piece, at) in enumerate(wrap_line(line, body_w)):
            paint = colour_current if start + i == current else colour
            drawn.append((piece, paint(line, at), j == 0 and start + i == current, _piece_links(links, at, len(piece)), j > 0))
            drawn_at.append(start + i)

    # Wrapped, more rows than fit: the newest, but for the top of the
    # console, where the oldest line shows from its first row.
    if len(drawn) > log_h:
        top_first = (start == 0 and up > 0) or centred
        window = 0 if top_first else len(drawn) - log_h
        if first_row >= 0 and first_row < window and search.get("anchored_view"):
            # Never below a held match's first row while the view is where
            # its jump put it.
            window = first_row
        drawn = drawn[window:window + log_h]
        drawn_at = drawn_at[window:window + log_h]

    # Which rows go on from the one above, for a copy to join them again.
    state["continued"] = {}
    state["page_h"] = log_h

    # Which of the folded view's lines each row is, for a click on a fold.
    folded_view = view == None and not state.get("help") and folding(state) and state.get("fold_seen", 0) == len(state.get("log", [])) and bool(state.get("folded"))
    first = len(out)
    for i in range(log_h):
        if folded_view and i < len(drawn_at):
            state["row_lines"][first + i] = drawn_at[i]
        text, paint, mark, links, more = drawn[i] if i < len(drawn) else ("", None, False, None, False)
        if more:
            state["continued"][len(out)] = True
        out.append(_box_row(text, cols, paint, mark, bar_edge[i] if bar_edge else None, links))
    out.append(_box_bottom(cols))
    if search["open"] and not state.get("help") and state.get("countdown_ms") == None:
        out.append(_search_bar(search, matches, cols))
    else:
        out.append(keys_line(state, cols))

    return _frame_of(state, out, rows, cols)

# The main pane's tabs, by the number key that shows each.
_TABS = [("console", "Console"), ("failures", "Failures"), ("metrics", "Metrics"), ("invocation", "Invocation"), ("tips", "Tips")]

# The tabs about a build, muted before one starts.
_BUILD_TABS = ["failures", "metrics", "invocation"]

# The build events the Invocation and Metrics tabs read.
_INVOCATION_KINDS = ["build_started", "options_parsed", "workspace_status", "build_metadata", "build_finished", "build_metrics"]

def target_counts(state: dict):
    """`(built, failed, blocked)` targets, from the failure entries: the
    targets with an entry of their own that is no test's and no pattern's
    are failed (`_own_labels`), each once however many entries it has; one
    that failed for a failed dependency (`blocked_targets`) is blocked."""
    found = failure_entries(state)
    failed = {}
    for e in found["entries"]:
        if e["group"] != 2 and not e["pattern"] and e["label"]:
            failed[e["label"]] = True
    return (state["counts"]["built"], len(failed), len(blocked_targets(state)))

def _failure_counts(state: dict):
    """`(failed, blocked)`: the failure entries but a flaky test's (as the
    failure summary counts them, and the Failures list has their rows), and
    the targets not built for a failed dependency."""
    found = failure_entries(state)
    return (len([e for e in found["entries"] if not e["flaky"]]), len(blocked_targets(state)))

# A heading in the Metrics and Invocation tabs.

def _shown_parts(parts: list, room: int) -> list:
    """The shown tab's name and crumbs, `(text, level)`, to fit `room`
    cells: whole, else the middle crumbs as `…`, else the root too, else
    the last crumb cut from its left, so the deepest always shows."""
    whole = [(p, i) for i, p in enumerate(parts)]
    tries = [whole]
    if len(parts) > 2:
        tries.append([whole[0], ("…", None), whole[-1]])
    if len(parts) > 1:
        tries.append([("…", None), whole[-1]])
    for parts_tried in tries:
        if 2 + text_width(" ▹ ".join([p for p, _ in parts_tried])) <= room:
            return parts_tried
    return [(_tail(parts[-1], max(1, room - 2)), len(parts) - 1)]

def tab_top(state: dict, status: str, cols: int, row: int) -> str:
    """The main pane's top border: its tabs, always in the same order, the
    shown one in reverse video (the failures counted, `✗` failed and `○`
    blocked) and named by its crumbs (a log or a file opened in it, the
    current one bold); then, at the border's right end, set off by dashes
    so it never reads as a tab, muted (`muted`), what the shown one says
    of itself
    (`status`: following, lines up, a run replayed). Where each tab and
    crumb is drawn is kept (`tab_spans`, on row `row`) for a click to take
    it, or go back to it, and the pointer to light it. Too narrow, the
    status is cut, then goes, then the other tabs, then the shown one's
    middle crumbs."""
    failed, blocked = _failure_counts(state)
    shown = state.get("tab", "console")
    names = {}
    for tab, name in _TABS:
        if tab == "failures" and (failed or blocked):
            name += _counts(" ✗%d", failed) + (_counts(" ○%d", blocked) if blocked else "")
        if tab == "tips" and tips_count(state):
            name += " %d" % tips_count(state)
        names[tab] = name
    muted = [] if built(state) else _BUILD_TABS
    parts = [names[shown]] + crumbs(state)
    whole = 3 + text_width("".join([" " + names[t] + " " for t, _ in _TABS if t != shown])) + text_width(" " + " ▹ ".join(parts) + " ")

    # Every tab where the shown one keeps the room it is drawn in: `room`
    # below leaves it the row but its own two cells and the others'.
    tabs = [t for t, _ in _TABS] if whole < cols else [shown]
    text = _c("border") + "┌─" + _RESET
    at = 2
    spans = []
    for tab in tabs:
        if tab != shown:
            label = " " + names[tab] + " "
            spans.append((at, at + text_width(label), tab))
            at += text_width(label)
            if tab == "tips":
                count = tips_count(state)
                label = (" Tips " + _c(tips_role(state)) + "%d" % count + _RESET + " ") if count else (_c("muted") + " Tips " + _RESET)
            text += (_c("muted") + label + _RESET) if tab in muted else label
            continue
        after = tabs[tabs.index(shown) + 1:]
        room = cols - at - 2 - text_width("".join([" " + names[t] + " " for t in after]))
        drawn = _shown_parts(parts, room)
        text += _c("active") + " "
        at += 1
        for k, (part, level) in enumerate(drawn):
            if k:
                text += " ▹ "
                at += 3
            last = k == len(drawn) - 1
            width = text_width(part)
            if level == 0 and len(parts) == 1:
                spans.append((at, at + width, shown))
            elif level != None and not last:
                spans.append((at, at + width, ("crumb", level)))
            text += (_c("strong") + part) if last and len(parts) > 1 else part
            at += width
        text += " " + _RESET
        at += 1
    state["tab_row"] = row
    state["tab_spans"] = spans

    # At least a dash on each side of the status, so it never touches a tab.
    rest = max(0, cols - at - 1)
    info = (" " + fit(status, rest - 4).rstrip(" ") + " ") if status and rest > 7 else ""
    lead = rest - text_width(info) - (1 if info else 0)
    return text + _c("border") + "─" * max(0, lead) + _RESET + (_c("muted") + info + _RESET + _c("border") + "─" if info else _c("border")) + "┐" + _RESET

_SECRET_WORDS = ("header", "token", "password", "passwd", "pwd", "secret", "credential", "auth", "key", "cookie", "session")

def _secret_name(name: str) -> bool:
    """Whether a flag's or setting's `name` names what may be a secret."""
    lower = name.lower()
    return any([word in lower for word in _SECRET_WORDS])

def _redacted(flag: str) -> str:
    """`flag` with what may be a secret's value hidden: a header's, a
    token's, a password's or a key's, an env var's value, a URL's
    user and password. A value that is itself a setting named so
    (`--define=API_TOKEN=x`, `--test_arg=--password=x`, `--copt=-DKEY=x`)
    has its own value hidden, as is what follows one with a space
    (`--test_arg=--token x`)."""
    name, eq, value = flag.partition("=")
    if not eq:
        return flag
    lower = name.lower()
    if _secret_name(name):
        return name + "=<REDACTED>"
    if lower.endswith("_env") or lower in ("--client_env", "--action_env", "--repo_env", "--test_env", "--host_action_env"):
        return name + "=" + value.partition("=")[0] + ("=<REDACTED>" if "=" in value else "")
    inner, inner_eq, _ = value.partition("=")
    if inner_eq and "://" not in inner and _secret_name(inner):
        return name + "=" + inner + "=<REDACTED>"
    head, space, _ = value.partition(" ")
    if space and "://" not in head and _secret_name(head):
        return name + "=" + head + " <REDACTED>"
    scheme, sep, rest = value.partition("://")
    if sep and "@" in rest.partition("/")[0]:
        value = scheme + "://<REDACTED>@" + rest.partition("@")[2]
    return name + "=" + value

def _redacted_args(flags: list) -> list:
    """`flags` each `_redacted`, and the one after a secret-named flag
    passed in another's value that left its own value to the next argument
    (`--test_arg=--password` then `--test_arg=hunter2`) hidden too, where
    it is that same flag again. A bare flag is never taken to own the next
    argument: a boolean one (`--google_default_credentials`) takes none."""
    out = []
    owner = None
    for flag in flags:
        name, eq, value = flag.partition("=")
        if owner != None and eq and name == owner:
            out.append(name + "=<REDACTED>")
            owner = None
            continue
        out.append(_redacted(flag))
        secret = eq and "=" not in value and " " not in value and value.startswith("-") and _secret_name(value)
        owner = name if secret else None
    return out

def _row_of(name: str, value) -> str:
    """A `name  value` row of a tab, the names in a column."""
    return "  " + name + " " * max(1, 24 - len(name)) + str(value)

# The execution logs Bazel writes in a format of the caller's, beside which
# it writes no compact one for the Dashboard to read.
_OWN_EXEC_LOGS = ["--execution_log_binary_file", "--execution_log_json_file"]

# The false words Bazel reads (`parse_bool` in the CLI's rc reader).
_FALSE_WORDS = ["false", "f", "no", "n", "0"]

def own_execution_log(inv: dict):
    """The flag a build was given for an execution log of the caller's own
    (`_OWN_EXEC_LOGS`), which leaves the Dashboard none to read; None without
    one. Its last occurrence counts, as Bazel takes it, from the whole
    command line Bazel parsed (rc files' flags in it), read as the CLI's
    `other_format` reads it: an empty value, a false one and the `--no`
    form are none (the way to clear one an rc file set; Bazel 9 takes
    `=false` as a path and refuses `--no`, Bazel 10 reads both as off), the
    bare flag, a true value or a path one."""
    options = inv.get("options_parsed")
    flags = _get(options, "cmd_line") or _get(options, "explicit_cmd_line") or []
    last = {}
    for flag in flags:
        name, eq, value = str(flag).partition("=")
        for own in _OWN_EXEC_LOGS:
            if name == "--no" + own[2:] and not eq:
                last[own] = False
            elif name == own:
                last[own] = not eq or (value != "" and value.lower() not in _FALSE_WORDS)
    named = [name for name in _OWN_EXEC_LOGS if last.get(name)]
    return named[0] if named else None

# Bazel's action cache miss reasons newer than the CLI's protos, which read
# as their numbers.
_NEWER_MISS_REASONS = {"7": "DIGEST_MISMATCH"}

def _by_count(counts: dict) -> list:
    """`counts`' `(name, n)`, the most first, those of none left out."""
    return sorted([(k, n) for k, n in counts.items() if n], key = lambda kv: (-kv[1], kv[0]))

def action_lines(state: dict) -> list:
    """Metrics' Actions table, in `action_split`'s unit: the done count
    and its cache hits, each kind under its glyph with what makes it up
    (the runners' spawns that ran or were cached, `BuildMetrics`' at the
    end and the execution log's while it runs; the internal actions'
    mnemonics, said to be some where they do not sum), each in one column
    and none of a kind there is none of; Bazel's action cache with why it
    missed, and the execution log's spawns."""
    c = state["counts"]
    inv = state.get("inv") or {}
    summary = _get(inv.get("build_metrics"), "action_summary")
    split = action_split(state)
    out = [_c("heading") + "Actions" + _RESET]
    if split["counted"]:
        if split["final"]:
            out.append(_row_of("done", actions_text(split)[0]))
        else:
            out.append(_row_of("done", "%s of %s%s" % (_thousands(split["done"]), _thousands(split["total"]), "+ (Bazel is still analysing)" if split["more"] else "")))

    # Each runner's spawns, BuildMetrics' once the build has them.
    runners = {}
    if split["final"]:
        for runner in _get(summary, "runner_count", None) or []:
            name = str(_get(runner, "name", "") or "")
            if name not in _NOT_RUNNERS:
                runners[name] = runners.get(name, 0) + (_get(runner, "count", 0) or 0)
    else:
        runners = dict(c.get("runners") or {})
    kinds = {}
    for data in _get(summary, "action_data", None) or []:
        mnemonic = str(_get(data, "mnemonic", "") or "")
        if mnemonic in _INTERNAL_MNEMONICS:
            kinds[mnemonic] = _get(data, "actions_executed", 0) or 0
    spawns = lambda n: _counts("%d spawn" + ("" if n == 1 else "s"), n)
    under = {
        "ran": [(name, spawns(n)) for name, n in _by_count({k: n for k, n in runners.items() if not _cache_runner(k)})],
        "disk": [(name, spawns(n)) for name, n in _by_count({k: n for k, n in runners.items() if k == "disk cache hit"})],
        "remote": [(name, spawns(n)) for name, n in _by_count({k: n for k, n in runners.items() if _cache_runner(k) and k != "disk cache hit"})],
        "local": [("", "Bazel's local action cache, or what it knew in memory, said the outputs on disk were still valid" + ("; estimated while it runs" if split.get("estimated") else ""))],
        "internal": [(name, _thousands(n)) for name, n in _by_count(kinds)],
        "other": [("", "spawns with mixed results" if split["final"] else "local action cache, internal, or not yet in the execution log")],
    }

    # The internal kinds Bazel names are only some of them.
    listed = 0
    for n in kinds.values():
        listed += n
    if split["final"] and kinds and listed < split["internal"]:
        under["internal"].append(("", _counts("and %d more of kinds this list does not name", split["internal"] - listed)))

    # Names and counts in columns, however long a mnemonic; a kind there is
    # none of left out, as in Results.
    parts = [p for p in action_parts(split) if p[1]]
    names = [len(words) + 2 for _, _, words, _, _ in parts] + [len(name) + 2 for _, n, words, _, _ in parts for name, _ in under.get(words.partition(" ")[0], []) if name]
    column = max([22] + [w + 1 for w in names])
    for glyph, n, words, role, how in parts:
        out.append("  " + ((_c(role) + glyph + _RESET) if role and glyph else (glyph or " ")) + " " + words + " " * (column - len(words)) + ("~" if how == "estimate" else "") + _thousands(n))
        for name, value in under.get(words.partition(" ")[0], []):
            out.append(("    " + name + " " * (column - len(name)) + value) if name else "      " + _c("muted") + value + _RESET)

    # Bazel's own action cache, and why it missed.
    stats = _get(summary, "action_cache_statistics")
    if stats != None:
        out.append("  action cache" + " " * (column + 2 - len("action cache")) + _counts("%d hits · %d misses", (_get(stats, "hits", 0) or 0, _get(stats, "misses", 0) or 0)))
        reasons = {}
        for miss in _get(stats, "miss_details", None) or []:
            n = _get(miss, "count", 0) or 0
            if n:
                reasons[str(_get(miss, "reason", "") or "")] = n

        # A reason newer than the CLI's protos reads as its number.
        for reason, n in _by_count(reasons):
            reason = _NEWER_MISS_REASONS.get(reason, reason)
            said = ("reason " + reason) if reason.isdigit() else reason.lower().replace("_", " ")
            out.append("    " + said + " " * max(1, column - len(said)) + _thousands(n))
    if c.get("spawns"):
        pct = c["spawn_hits"] * 100 // c["spawns"]
        out.append(_row_of("spawns", _counts("%d · %d from a cache (%d%%) · %d run", (c["spawns"], c["spawn_hits"], pct, c["spawns"] - c["spawn_hits"]))))
    elif own_execution_log(state.get("inv") or {}):
        out.append(_row_of("spawns", "not read: this build writes its own execution log (%s)" % own_execution_log(state.get("inv") or {})))
    elif state.get("exec_log_read"):
        out.append(_row_of("spawns", "0 run: the execution log has none"))
    else:
        out.append(_row_of("spawns", "after the build, from its execution log"))
    if summary != None:
        out.append(_row_of("actions created", _thousands(_get(summary, "actions_created", 0) or 0)))
    return out

def metrics_lines(state: dict) -> list:
    """The Metrics tab: the build's actions (`action_lines`), tests (what
    a cache served from their results), targets, timing and memory."""
    c = state["counts"]
    bm = state.get("inv", {}).get("build_metrics")
    out = action_lines(state)
    out += ["", _c("heading") + "Tests" + _RESET]
    runs = c.get("test_runs", 0)
    out.append(_row_of("runs", _counts("%d · %d from a cache%s", (runs, c.get("test_cached", 0), (_counts(" (%d%%)", (c["test_cached"] * 100 // runs))) if runs else ""))))
    out.append(_row_of("results", _counts("%d passed · %d failed · %d flaky · %d skipped", (c["tests_passed"], c["tests_failed"], c.get("tests_flaky", 0), c.get("tests_skipped", 0)))))
    built_n, failed, blocked = target_counts(state)
    shown, _ = _failure_counts(state)
    out += ["", _c("heading") + "Targets" + _RESET]
    out.append(_row_of("configured", c["configured"]))
    out.append(_row_of("built", _counts("%d · %d failed · %d blocked by a dependency", (built_n, failed, blocked))))
    out.append(_row_of("failures listed", _counts("%d (failed actions and test attempts, in Failures)", shown)))
    timing = _get(bm, "timing_metrics")
    out += ["", _c("heading") + "Time" + _RESET]

    # A folded build's own time; the running one's since it started.
    picked = picked_build(state)
    builds = state.get("builds", [])
    took = builds[picked]["ms"] if 0 <= picked and picked < len(builds) else state["now_ms"] - state.get("build_start_ms", state["start_ms"])
    out.append(_row_of("elapsed", elapsed(took)))
    if state.get("critical"):
        out.append(_row_of("critical path", state["critical"]))
    for field, name in (("analysis_phase_time_in_ms", "analysis"), ("execution_phase_time_in_ms", "execution"), ("cpu_time_in_ms", "CPU")):
        ms = _get(timing, field, 0) or 0
        if ms:
            out.append(_row_of(name, elapsed(ms)))
    heap = _get(_get(bm, "memory_metrics"), "peak_post_gc_heap_size", 0) or 0
    if heap:
        out += ["", _c("heading") + "Memory" + _RESET, _row_of("peak heap after GC", "%d MiB" % (heap // (1024 * 1024)))]
    slow = [] if state.get("building") else slowest_rows(state)
    if slow:
        out += ["", _c("heading") + "Slowest" + _RESET] + ["  " + r for r in slow]
    return out

def invocation_lines(state: dict) -> list:
    """The Invocation tab: what Bazel was asked to do, by whom and where,
    as its build events say, flags that may hold a secret redacted."""
    inv = state.get("inv", {})
    started = inv.get("build_started")
    out = [_c("heading") + "Invocation" + _RESET]
    out.append(_row_of("command", str(_get(started, "command", "") or state["task"])))
    out.append(_row_of("invocation id", str(_get(started, "uuid", "") or state.get("bazel_uuid", "") or "")))
    out.append(_row_of("Bazel", str(_get(started, "build_tool_version", "") or "")))
    out.append(_row_of("workspace", str(_get(started, "workspace_directory", "") or "")))
    finished = inv.get("build_finished")
    if finished != None:
        code = _get(finished, "exit_code")
        out.append(_row_of("exit", "%s (%d)" % (str(_get(code, "name", "") or ""), _get(code, "code", 0) or 0)))
    for label, url in state.get("pages", []):
        out.append(_row_of(label, url))
    options = inv.get("options_parsed")
    explicit = _redacted_args([str(f) for f in (_get(options, "explicit_cmd_line") or [])])
    if explicit:
        out += ["", _c("heading") + "Flags given" + _RESET] + ["  " + f for f in explicit]
    startup = _redacted_args([str(f) for f in (_get(options, "explicit_startup_options") or [])])
    if startup:
        out += ["", _c("heading") + "Startup options given" + _RESET] + ["  " + f for f in startup]
    status = _get(inv.get("workspace_status"), "item") or []
    if status:
        out += ["", _c("heading") + "Workspace status" + _RESET]
        out += [_row_of(str(_get(i, "key", "")), _redacted(str(_get(i, "key", "")) + "=" + str(_get(i, "value", ""))).partition("=")[2]) for i in status]
    metadata = _get(inv.get("build_metadata"), "metadata")
    if metadata:
        out += ["", _c("heading") + "Build metadata" + _RESET]
        out += [_row_of(str(k), _redacted(str(k) + "=" + str(metadata[k])).partition("=")[2]) for k in sorted(metadata)]
    return out

def show_tab(state: dict, tab: str):
    """Show the main pane's `tab`, as it was left: each tab keeps what it
    shows (a log or a file opened in it, its stack, its scroll). The
    failures' list takes the keys when nothing is open over it."""
    shown = state.get("tab", "console")
    if shown != tab:
        parked = state.setdefault("parked", {})
        parked[shown] = (state["view"], state.get("views", []), state["scroll"])
        state["view"], state["views"], state["scroll"] = parked.pop(tab, (None, [], 0))
        state["search"]["back"] = -1
    state["tab"] = tab
    state["focus"] = "failures" if tab == "failures" and state["view"] == None else "console"

def _crumb(state: dict, view: dict) -> str:
    """A view's crumb in the tab bar: a file's name and line; a failure's
    target, then its shard, run and attempt where there are several."""
    record = view.get("record")
    if view.get("peek") or record == None:
        return view["title"]
    if record["kind"] != "test":
        return record["label"] + (" ▹ " + record["detail"] if record.get("detail") else "")
    key = (record["label"], record["config"])
    runs, shards = state["test_counts"].get(key, (0, 0))
    seen_runs, seen_shards = state["spread"].get(key, (1, 1))
    runs, shards = max(runs, seen_runs), max(shards, seen_shards)
    attempts = state["last_attempts"].get((record["label"], record["config"], record["run"], record["shard"]), 1)
    parts = [record["label"]]
    if shards > 1:
        parts.append("shard %d/%d" % (record["shard"], shards))
    if runs > 1:
        parts.append("run %d/%d" % (record["run"], runs))
    if attempts > 1:
        parts.append("attempt %d/%d" % (record["attempt"], attempts))
    return " ▹ ".join(parts)

def _shown_record(state: dict):
    """The failure whose log the shown tab has open, under any file
    peeked from it; None for none."""
    first = state.get("views", [])
    view = first[0][0] if first else state["view"]
    return view.get("record") if view != None else None

def crumbs(state: dict) -> list:
    """The shown tab's views as crumbs, its root first: what the tab bar
    names it by."""
    out = [_crumb(state, v) for v, _ in state.get("views", [])]
    if state["view"] != None:
        out.append(_crumb(state, state["view"]))
    builds = len(state.get("builds", [])) + (1 if state.get("building") else 0)
    if state.get("tab") in _PICKED_TABS and builds > 1 and state["view"] == None:
        out.append("%d of %d" % (picked_build(state) + 1, builds))
    return out

# The tabs that show one build, picked with `<`/`>`.
_PICKED_TABS = ["metrics", "invocation"]

def picked_build(state: dict) -> int:
    """Which build the Metrics and Invocation tabs show, oldest 0: the one
    `<`/`>` picked, else the newest (the running one, while one runs)."""
    newest = len(state.get("builds", [])) - (0 if state.get("building") else 1)
    picked = state.get("picked_build")
    return newest if picked == None else max(0, min(picked, newest))

def build_view(state: dict) -> dict:
    """`state` as the picked build's tabs read it: an older build's own
    counts, invocation and timings over the newest's."""
    i = picked_build(state)
    builds = state.get("builds", [])
    if i >= len(builds) or (i == len(builds) - 1 and not state.get("building")):
        return state
    view = dict(state)
    view.update(builds[i]["snapshot"])
    return view

def back_to(state: dict, level: int):
    """The shown tab back to its `level`th view (0 its own: the list or
    the console), as it was scrolled."""
    views = state.get("views", [])
    if level <= 0:
        state["view"], state["views"], state["scroll"] = None, [], 0
    elif level <= len(views):
        state["view"], state["scroll"] = views[level - 1]
        state["views"] = views[:level - 1]
    state["search"]["back"] = -1
    state["focus"] = "failures" if state.get("tab") == "failures" and state["view"] == None else "console"

def _tab_pane(state: dict, out: list, cols: int, rows: int) -> list:
    """`out` with the main pane showing the Failures, Metrics or Invocation
    tab, and the keys row under it. The failures as their list, chosen with
    ↑↓, Enter showing one's log; the others scrolled with ↑↓."""
    tab = state.get("tab", "console")
    log_h = max(1, rows - len(out) - 3)
    width = cols - 4
    if tab == "tips":
        lines = tips_lines(state, width)
        title = "↑↓ choose · s silence on this machine" if shown_tips(state) else ""
    elif not built(state):
        lines = [_c("muted") + "No build yet" + _RESET]
        title = ""
    elif tab == "failures":
        lines = (action_rows if state.get("list") == "actions" else failure_rows)(state, log_h, width)
        title = "Actions with output · ↑↓ choose · Enter show its log" if state.get("list") == "actions" else "↑↓ choose · Enter log · ^O " + ("one" if state.get("expand_failures") else "all")
    else:
        view = build_view(state)
        lines = metrics_lines(view) if tab == "metrics" else invocation_lines(view)
        top = min(state.get("tab_scroll", 0), max(0, len(lines) - log_h))
        state["tab_scroll"] = top
        lines = lines[top:]
        title = "↑↓ scroll" if len(lines) > log_h or top else ""
    out.append(tab_top(state, title, cols, len(out)))
    state["pane"] = (len(out), len(out) + log_h - 1)
    for i in range(log_h):
        line = lines[i] if i < len(lines) else ""
        visible = text_width(_plain(line))
        if visible > width:
            out.append(_box_row(_plain(line), cols))
        else:
            out.append(_c("border") + "│ " + _RESET + line + " " * (width - visible) + _c("border") + " │" + _RESET)
    out.append(_box_bottom(cols))
    out.append(keys_line(state, cols))
    return out

def keys_line(state: dict, cols: int) -> str:
    """The bottom row: the keys (`keys_row`), and at its right end the CLI's
    version, when it leaves the keys room (60 cells, as many as fit at
    once in that much)."""
    version = state.get("cli_version", "")
    tag = (" aspect " + version + " ") if version else ""
    if not tag or cols - len(tag) < 60:
        return _c("muted") + keys_row(state, cols) + _RESET
    return _c("muted") + keys_row(state, cols - len(tag)) + _RESET + _c("subtle") + tag + _RESET

def _frame_of(state: dict, out: list, rows: int, cols: int) -> str:
    """The frame of `out`'s rows: the link under the pointer lit, the
    selection drawn, each row cleared as it is written."""

    # Home, each row cleared before it is written: cleared after, a row as
    # wide as the screen would lose its last column, where the cursor waits.
    # No newline after the last row, which would scroll.
    shown = out[:rows]

    # As drawn, for a click's link and the selection's text; the selection
    # reversed over it.
    state["frame"] = shown
    state["cols"] = cols
    selected = {row: (a, b) for row, a, b in selection_rows(state, cols)}
    hover = state.get("hover")
    if hover != None and hover[1] == state.get("tab_row") and hover[1] < len(shown):
        for a, b, tab in state.get("tab_spans", []):
            if a <= hover[0] and hover[0] < b and tab != state.get("tab", "console"):
                shown = list(shown)
                on = _c("hover")
                shown[hover[1]] = mark_cells(shown[hover[1]], [(a, b, on, _RESET, on)])
    url = link_at(shown[hover[1]], hover[0]) if hover != None and hover[1] < len(shown) else None
    if url != None:
        # The whole link lit: its row, and the rows above and below a wrap
        # carried it onto.
        shown = list(shown)
        lit = [hover[1]]
        for step in (-1, 1):
            r = hover[1] + step
            for _ in range(len(shown)):
                if r < 0 or r >= len(shown) or not link_spans(shown[r], url):
                    break
                lit.append(r)
                r += step
        on, off = _hover_colour(state)
        for r in lit:
            shown[r] = mark_cells(shown[r], [(a, b, on, off, on) for a, b in link_spans(shown[r], url)])
    if selected:
        on, off = _selection_colour(state)
        shown = [mark_cells(row, [(selected[i][0], selected[i][1], on, off, on)]) if i in selected else row for i, row in enumerate(shown)]
    frame = state.get("osc", "") + "\x1b[H" + "\r\n".join(["\x1b[K" + row for row in shown])
    state["osc"] = ""
    if len(shown) < rows:
        frame += "\r\n\x1b[J"
    return frame

# Where Bazel's closing block starts: the lines a reader of the
# scrollback wants after the screen is gone (`report_lines`), its first
# `_CLOSING_HEAD` and last `_CLOSING_TAIL` (where `Executed N out of M
# tests` is) of them.
_CLOSING = ["INFO: Elapsed time:", "INFO: Build completed", "ERROR: Build did NOT complete", "INFO: Errors Bazel reported", "── Error recap (", "Executed "]
_CLOSING_HEAD = 5
_CLOSING_TAIL = 40

# The most lines of an error block kept for the scrollback.
_MAX_BLOCK = 20

# Errors and warnings kept past `_MAX_LOG`, so `e` finds them all.
_MAX_EARLY = 5000

def _closing(line: str) -> bool:
    line = bazel_line(line).lstrip(" ")
    for head in _CLOSING:
        if line.startswith(head):
            return True
    return False

def _folded_line(state: dict, n: int) -> str:
    """A fold's row: how many lines it holds and how to show them (a click
    while the screen has the mouse, Ctrl+O for every fold)."""
    how = "click or Ctrl+O shows them" if state.get("mouse") else "Ctrl+O shows them"
    return "  … +%d line%s (%s)" % (n, "" if n == 1 else "s", how)

def _tools_own(coloured) -> bool:
    """Whether a line as written (`coloured`) is coloured as a tool colours
    `INFO:` (the colon inside the colour, the space after), not as Bazel
    colours its own message (`INFO: ` inside): a tool's line, which no
    block of Bazel's starts with."""
    if not coloured:
        return False
    rest = coloured
    for _ in range(8):
        if not rest.startswith("\x1b["):
            break
        end = rest.find("m")
        if end < 0:
            return False
        rest = rest[end + 1:]
    head, colon, after = rest.partition(":")
    return bool(colon) and head.isalpha() and head.isupper() and after.startswith("\x1b[0m")

def _fold_line(state: dict, line: str):
    """Take `line` into the console's folded view (`folded`): the lines
    under an `INFO: From` (a successful action's output) counted on one
    row after it rather than shown, a tool's line coloured like one of
    Bazel's (`_tools_own`, from its colours) among them; a block a click
    opened (`fold_open`, by its `INFO: From` line's number since the
    console began) shown whole. Each row's block (`fold_head`) is its
    `INFO: From` line's index in `log` on that line's row and the count's,
    else -1: what a click on it toggles."""
    folded = state.setdefault("folded", [])
    src = state.setdefault("fold_src", [])
    head = state.setdefault("fold_head", [])
    at = state.get("fold_seen", 0)
    state["fold_seen"] = at + 1
    if bazel_line(line).startswith("INFO: From "):
        folded.append(line)
        src.append(at)
        head.append(at)
        state["fold_n"] = 0
        state["fold_at"] = at
        state["fold_shown"] = (state.get("log_base", 0) + at) in state.get("fold_open", {})
        return
    if state.get("fold_n", -1) >= 0 and (not _starts_block(line) or _tools_own(state.get("colour_of", {}).get(line))):
        state["fold_n"] += 1
        if state.get("fold_shown"):
            folded.append(line)
            src.append(at)
            head.append(-1)
        elif state["fold_n"] == 1:
            folded.append(_folded_line(state, 1))
            src.append(at)
            head.append(state["fold_at"])
        else:
            folded[-1] = _folded_line(state, state["fold_n"])
            src[-1] = at
        return
    state["fold_n"] = -1
    folded.append(line)
    src.append(at)
    head.append(-1)

def refold(state: dict):
    """The console's folded view built again from the lines kept, as a
    trim, a click on a fold or the mouse's toggle leaves it."""
    state["folded"] = []
    state["fold_src"] = []
    state["fold_head"] = []
    state["fold_n"] = -1
    state["fold_seen"] = 0
    for line in state.get("log", []):
        _fold_line(state, line)

def _rows_of(state: dict, lines: list) -> int:
    """How many screen rows `lines` take in the console, wrapped as drawn."""
    if not state.get("wrap", True):
        return len(lines)
    width = max(1, state.get("cols", 80) - 4)
    rows = 0
    for line in lines:
        rows += len(wrap_line(line, width))
    return rows

def toggle_fold(state: dict, row: int) -> bool:
    """A click on the console's `row`: on an `INFO: From` with lines folded
    under it, or on their count, shows them; on an open one's `INFO: From`,
    folds them again. That line stays on its row of the screen (the view
    moved by the rows that came or went below it; a row off at most where a
    wrapped line straddles the bottom), unless the console was following,
    which it goes on doing. Not in another tab, nor on an `INFO:
    From` with nothing under it yet. Whether it was one."""
    if state.get("tab", "console") != "console" or not folding(state):
        return False
    at = state.get("row_lines", {}).get(row)
    heads = state.get("fold_head", [])
    if at == None or at >= len(heads) or heads[at] < 0:
        return False
    head = heads[at]
    key = state.get("log_base", 0) + head
    opened = state.setdefault("fold_open", {})
    shown = key in opened
    folded = state.get("folded", [])
    if not shown and not (at + 1 < len(heads) and heads[at + 1] == head) and not folded[at].startswith("  … +"):
        # An `INFO: From` with nothing under it yet.
        return False

    # The rows between the clicked block's line and the view's bottom.
    scroll = state.get("scroll", 0)
    line_at = [i for i, h in enumerate(heads) if h == head][0]
    end = len(folded) - scroll
    below = _rows_of(state, folded[line_at + 1:end])
    if shown:
        opened.pop(key)
    else:
        opened[key] = True
    refold(state)
    folded = state["folded"]
    if scroll > 0:
        # As many rows below the line: the view's new bottom where they end,
        # or a row off where a wrapped line straddles that.
        filled, end, short, short_end = 0, line_at + 1, 0, line_at + 1
        for i in range(line_at + 1, len(folded)):
            if filled >= below:
                break
            short, short_end = filled, end
            filled += _rows_of(state, [folded[i]])
            end = i + 1
        if filled - below > below - short:
            end = short_end
        state["scroll"] = max(0, len(folded) - end)
    state["search"]["anchored_view"] = False
    say_note(state, "expanded · click again to fold" if not shown else "folded · click to expand")
    return True

def folded_to_lines(state: dict, scroll: int) -> int:
    """A scroll of the folded view (rows from its newest) as one of the
    lines kept, for a search, which reads them all: the bottom row's line,
    the last of a fold for its count's row."""
    src = state.get("fold_src", [])
    if not src:
        return scroll
    row = max(0, min(len(src) - 1, len(src) - 1 - scroll))
    return max(0, len(state.get("log", [])) - 1 - src[row])

def folding(state: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether the console shows its folded view: every line shown (`e`
    on all, no search filtering nor jumping), not opened with Ctrl+O, and
    not a log."""
    search = state["search"]
    return state.get("view") == None and state["filter"] == "all" and search["pattern"] == None and not state.get("unfolded")

def add_lines(state: dict, lines: list, links = None, colours = None, cli = False):
    """Take the console's new `lines`, and with them their `links` (each
    line's `[start, end, url]`s, as `output_feed()` gives them): kept (the
    newest `_MAX_LOG`, with each one's kind, `classify`'s, its errors and
    warnings past that kept for `e`, the rest counted in `dropped`), the
    first `_MAX_ERRORS` error blocks noted for the scrollback, Bazel's
    closing block from where it starts (`_CLOSING`), Bazel's cache tally
    read (`cache_hits`). Scrolled up, the console stays on the lines it
    shows, counting the new ones and the errors among them (`unseen`,
    `unseen_errors`); a log being viewed does not move. Counted from the
    end, the current match and where the bar opened move with them, the
    latter in lines the `e` filter shows, as a search that jumps lists
    them. `colours` are the lines as written, colour kept (the same text),
    drawn in their place (`colour_of`); `cli` lines are the CLI's own, each
    its own kind (never under an error of Bazel's), not Bazel's closing
    block."""
    shown = 0
    shown_errors = 0
    unsearched = 0
    folded_before = len(state.get("folded", []))
    if cli:
        kinds = [severity(line) for line in lines]
        state["block"] = ""
        for line in lines:
            note_phase(state, line)
    else:
        kinds, state["block"] = classify(lines, state.get("block", ""))
    colour_of = state.setdefault("colour_of", {})
    state.setdefault("kinds", [])
    closing = state.setdefault("closing", [])
    for i, line in enumerate(lines):
        kind = kinds[i]
        if len(line) > _MAX_LINE:
            # The rest is in Bazel's console log, on disk.
            line = line[:_MAX_LINE] + "… (%d more characters in the console log)" % (len(line) - _MAX_LINE)
        state["log_size"] = state.get("log_size", 0) + len(line)
        state["log"].append(line)
        state["kinds"].append(kind)
        coloured = colours[i] if colours != None and i < len(colours) else None
        if coloured and coloured != line and len(line) <= _MAX_LINE:
            colour_of[line] = coloured
        _fold_line(state, line)
        if links != None and i < len(links):
            keep_links(state, line, links[i])

        # Errors after Bazel's closing block began are its recap's.
        if kind == "error" and severity(line) == "error" and not _closing(line) and not closing:
            state["error_count"] += 1
            if len(state["errors"]) < _MAX_ERRORS:
                state["errors"].append([line])
        elif kind == "error" and severity(line) != "error" and state["errors"] and len(state["errors"][-1]) < _MAX_BLOCK and state["error_count"] <= _MAX_ERRORS:
            state["errors"][-1].append(line)
        if (closing or _closing(line)) and not cli:
            # Bazel's, not the CLI's own lines, which the scrollback
            # gets as they were printed: the head, and a tail kept short.
            if len(closing) < _CLOSING_HEAD:
                closing.append(line)
            else:
                tail = state.setdefault("closing_tail", [])
                tail.append(line)
                if len(tail) > 2 * _CLOSING_TAIL:
                    state["closing_cut"] = state.get("closing_cut", 0) + len(tail) - _CLOSING_TAIL
                    state["closing_tail"] = tail[-_CLOSING_TAIL:]
        if _shows(state["filter"], kind):
            unsearched += 1
            if console_lines(state, [line], [kind]):
                shown += 1
                if kind == "error":
                    shown_errors += 1
        cache = cache_hits(line)
        if cache != None:
            state["cache"] = cache
        plain = bazel_line(line)
        if plain.startswith("INFO: Elapsed time:") and "Critical Path: " in plain:
            state["critical"] = plain.partition("Critical Path: ")[2].strip().split(" ")[0]

    if folding(state):
        # Folded, a line under an `INFO: From` adds no row.
        shown = len(state["folded"]) - folded_before

    # The console parked under another tab, scrolled up: held where it was.
    parked = state.get("parked", {}).get("console")
    if parked != None and parked[0] == None and parked[2] > 0:
        state["parked"]["console"] = (parked[0], parked[1], parked[2] + shown)
    if state["view"] == None and state.get("tab", "console") == "console":
        search = state["search"]
        held = search["back"] >= 0 and search["mode"] == "jump" and search["pattern"] != None
        if search["open"] and search.get("anchored"):
            search["origin"] += unsearched
            search["origin_rows"] = search.get("origin_rows", 0) + (len(state["folded"]) - folded_before if state.get("folded") != None else unsearched)
        if state["scroll"] > 0:
            state["scroll"] += shown
            state["unseen"] += shown
            state["unseen_errors"] = state.get("unseen_errors", 0) + shown_errors
        if search["back"] >= 0:
            search["back"] += shown

            # A match jumped to holds the view, even one near the newest
            # line, until `J` follows again.
            if state["scroll"] == 0 and held:
                state["scroll"] = shown
                state["unseen"] += shown
    over = len(state["log"]) - _MAX_LOG
    if state.get("log_size", 0) > _MAX_LOG_SIZE:
        # Long lines: as many of the oldest as bring the text a tenth under
        # its budget, if that is more than the line count asks.
        excess = state["log_size"] - _MAX_LOG_SIZE * 9 // 10
        drop = 0
        for line in state["log"]:
            if excess <= 0:
                break
            excess -= len(line)
            drop += 1
        if drop > 0:
            over = max(over, drop - _MAX_LOG // 10, 1)
    if over > 0:
        # A tenth more than over, so what follows (refolding) runs once in
        # that many lines, not on every batch.
        over = min(len(state["log"]), over + _MAX_LOG // 10)
        state["trims"] = state.get("trims", 0) + 1
        early = state.setdefault("early", [])
        for line, kind in zip(state["log"][:over], state["kinds"][:over]):
            if kind:
                early.append((line, kind))
        if len(early) > _MAX_EARLY:
            state["early"] = early[-_MAX_EARLY:]
        for line in state["log"][:over]:
            state["log_size"] = state.get("log_size", 0) - len(line)
        state["log"] = state["log"][over:]
        state["kinds"] = state["kinds"][over:]
        kept = state.get("colour_of", {})
        state["colour_of"] = {line: kept[line] for line in state["log"] if line in kept}
        state["dropped"] = state.get("dropped", 0) + over
        state["log_base"] = state.get("log_base", 0) + over

        # Trimmed seed lines are still on the main screen: neither replayed
        # nor counted as not kept on leaving.
        cut = min(over, state.get("seeded", 0))
        state["seeded"] = state.get("seeded", 0) - cut
        state["seed_trimmed"] = state.get("seed_trimmed", 0) + cut
        refold(state)
    if len(state.get("line_links", {})) > _MAX_LOG:
        kept = {line: True for line in state["log"]}
        state["line_links"] = {line: spans for line, spans in state["line_links"].items() if line in kept}

# How long the finished screen waits for a key while nothing on it moves.
_IDLE_MS = 1000

# The keys that leave the finished screen, and leave at once during its
# countdown.
_LEAVE_KEYS = ["q", "enter", "esc"]

# The phase between the console's end and post-task, while the task does
# what follows its build.
_FINISHING = "Finishing…"

def keys_row(state: dict, cols: int) -> str:
    """The keys row, `cols` wide: what is going on when that is all there is
    to say (cancelling, the countdown, the keys overlay open, the task
    finishing after its build, when no key is read), else the keys for what
    has focus, the likeliest first, as many as fit before `? keys`."""
    if state["ended"] and state["cancelling"]:
        return fit(" Cancelled", cols)
    if state["cancelling"]:
        return fit(" cancelling… (Ctrl+C again to stop now)", cols)
    if not state["ended"] and state["phase"] == _FINISHING:
        return fit(" " + _FINISHING + " · Ctrl+C cancels", cols)
    if state.get("countdown_ms") != None:
        return fit(" Done ✔ · leaving in %ds · q, Enter or Esc leave now · any other key stays" % ((state["countdown_ms"] + 999) // 1000), cols)
    if state.get("help"):
        return fit(" ↑↓ scroll · any other key closes", cols)
    if state.get("note") and (state.get("note_until") == None or _clock(state) < state["note_until"]):
        return fit(" " + state["note"], cols)
    if state.get("tab") in ("metrics", "invocation"):
        return fit(" ↑↓ scroll · Tab next tab · Esc back · q " + ("leave" if state["ended"] else "cancel") + " · ? keys", cols)
    if state.get("tab") == "tips" and state["view"] == None:
        tips = shown_tips(state)
        chosen = tips[max(0, min(state.get("tip_selected", 0), len(tips) - 1))] if tips else None
        choose = ("↑↓ choose · s %s · " % ("undo" if chosen[1] else "silence")) if tips else ""
        return fit(" " + choose + "Tab next tab · Esc back · q " + ("leave" if state["ended"] else "cancel") + " · ? keys", cols)
    done = state["ended"]
    parts = [("Done ✔" if state.get("verdict", True) else "Done ✗")] if done else []
    parts += row_labels(state)
    text = " " + parts[0]
    for part in parts[1:]:
        if text_width(text) + 3 + text_width(part) + len(" · ? keys") > cols:
            break
        text += " · " + part
    return fit(text + " · ? keys", cols)

def on_key(state: dict, key: str, page: int) -> bool:
    """Apply a key to the dashboard's state; whether it leaves the finished
    screen. The mouse (`mouse_key`) first, then an `early` entry of `KEYS`
    (Ctrl+L); then the modes that take the keys: an Esc typed before a key
    as Alt with it, the `?` overlay open (↑↓, `k`/`j` and the wheel scroll
    it, any other key closes it), the search bar open (`_search_key`, every
    key but the arrows and the wheel). Else the first entry of `KEYS` that
    takes the key and applies now; a toggle's entry (`said`) has the keys
    row say, for `_SAID_MS`, the state it left and how to undo it. Any key
    clears what the last one said (`note`)."""

    if mouse_key(state, key):
        return False
    say_note(state, "")
    for k in KEYS:
        if k.early and key in k.keys and k.when(state):
            return k.do(state, key, page)

    # An Esc typed just before a key reads as Alt with it: apart from the
    # bar's own Alt toggles, it is the two keys.
    if key.startswith("alt-") and key not in _ALT_SCROLL and not (state["search"]["open"] and key in ("alt-c", "alt-w", "alt-backspace")):
        if on_key(state, "esc", page):
            return True
        return on_key(state, key[len("alt-"):], page)
    if state.get("help"):
        if key in ("up", "k", "wheel-up"):
            state["help_top"] = max(0, state.get("help_top", 0) - 1)
        elif key in ("down", "j", "wheel-down"):
            state["help_top"] = state.get("help_top", 0) + 1
        else:
            state["help"] = False
        return False
    if state["search"]["open"] and _search_key(state, key, page):
        return False
    for k in KEYS:
        if key in k.keys and k.when(state):
            leaves = k.do(state, key, page)
            if k.said != None:
                # What the toggle did and how to undo it, for a moment.
                say_note(state, k.said(state), _SAID_MS)
            return leaves
    return False

# How long the keys row says what a toggle did.
_SAID_MS = 2000

def say_note(state: dict, text: str, ms = None):
    """Have the keys row say `text` (`""` for nothing), until the next key,
    or for `ms` when given: the one way a note is set, so none keeps an
    older one's deadline."""
    state["note"] = text
    state["note_until"] = _clock(state) + ms if ms != None else None

def _clock(state: dict) -> int:
    """The time notes are timed by, in milliseconds: the screen's own clock
    (`clock`), which runs on the finished screen too, whose header time
    stays; else the frame's `now_ms`."""
    clock = state.get("clock")
    return clock() if clock != None else state.get("now_ms", 0)

# Key bindings, as data: one table (`KEYS`) drives `on_key`, the keys row
# (`keys_row`) and the `?` overlay (`help_rows`), so none of them can name a
# key another does not. An entry: the keys it takes (`keys`, none for one
# only documented, as the mouse's), whether it applies now (`when`), what
# it does (`do(state, key, page)`, whether it leaves the finished screen),
# its overlay row (`help`: the keys as shown, and what they do) in a
# `group`, shown when `shown` says (else when it applies), and its labels
# in the keys row (`row(state)`, a list). `on_key` runs the first entry
# that takes the key and applies; the order is the precedence (a tab's own
# keys before the console's), and an `early` one goes before the overlay
# and the search bar take the keys.

def _always(state):
    return True

def _bar_closed(state):
    return not state["search"]["open"]

def _no_view(state):
    return state["view"] == None

def _in_list_tab(state):
    return state.get("tab") in ("metrics", "invocation") and state["view"] == None

def _over_actions(state):
    at = state.get("actions_at")
    hover = state.get("hover")
    return at != None and hover != None and at[0] <= hover[1] and hover[1] <= at[1] and hover[0] < at[2]

def _has_items(state):
    return bool(_items(state))

def _has_other_list(state):
    return bool(state.get("outputs")) or state.get("list") == "actions"

def _tips_listed(state):
    return state.get("tab") == "tips" and state["view"] == None

def _failures_focused(state):
    return state["focus"] == "failures"

def _console_keys(state):
    return state["focus"] != "failures"

def _k_tab_number(state, key, page):
    show_tab(state, _TABS[int(key) - 1][0])
    return False

def _k_tab_cycle(state, key, page):
    names = [tab for tab, _ in _TABS]
    i = names.index(state.get("tab", "console")) if state.get("tab", "console") in names else 0
    show_tab(state, names[(i + (1 if key == "tab" else -1)) % len(names)])
    return False

def _k_panes(state, key, page):
    state["panes"] = not panes_shown(state)
    return False

def _k_tips(state, key, page):
    rows = shown_tips(state)
    if not rows:
        return False
    at = max(0, min(state.get("tip_selected", 0), len(rows) - 1))
    if key == "s":
        toggle_silence(state, rows[at])
    else:
        at = max(0, min(len(rows) - 1, at + (-1 if key in ("up", "k") else 1)))
    state["tip_selected"] = at
    return False

def _k_actions_wheel(state, key, page):
    # The wheel over the running actions scrolls them, not the console.
    state["actions_scroll"] = max(0, state.get("actions_scroll", 0) + (_WHEEL if key == "wheel-down" else -_WHEEL))
    return False

def _k_pick_build(state, key, page):
    newest = len(state.get("builds", [])) - (0 if state.get("building") else 1)
    state["picked_build"] = max(0, min(newest, picked_build(state) + (1 if key == ">" else -1)))
    state["tab_scroll"] = 0
    return False

def _k_tab_scroll(state, key, page):
    if key in ("K", "home", "J", "end"):
        state["tab_scroll"] = 0 if key in ("K", "home") else 1 << 30
        return False
    step = {"up": -1, "k": -1, "wheel-up": -_WHEEL, "down": 1, "j": 1, "wheel-down": _WHEEL, "pageup": -page, "pagedown": page, "b": -page, " ": page, "u": -page // 2, "d": page // 2}
    state["tab_scroll"] = max(0, state.get("tab_scroll", 0) + step[key])
    return False

def _k_esc(state, key, page):
    if state["view"] != None:
        # Back a crumb: to the view it was opened from, else the tab's own.
        back_to(state, len(state.get("views", [])))
    elif state.get("tab", "console") != "console":
        show_tab(state, "console")
    else:
        return state["ended"]
    return False

def _k_other_list(state, key, page):
    state["list"] = "failures" if state.get("list") == "actions" else "actions"
    if not _items(state) and state.get("tab") != "failures":
        state["focus"] = "console"
    return False

def _k_list(state, key, page):
    # The Failures list's rows (`listed_failures`, then the failed targets
    # without a log of their own: a blocked one opens what blocks it); a
    # row's record is the one its heading and excerpt tell.
    items = _items(state)
    pick = _pick(state)
    chosen = state.get(pick, 0)
    if state.get("list") == "actions":
        order = list(range(len(items)))
        at = min(chosen, len(order) - 1)
    else:
        listed = listed_failures(state)
        order = listed + [len(items) + k for k in range(_failure_total(state) - len(items))]
        at = min(_listed_position(state, listed, chosen), len(order) - 1)
    if not order:
        return False
    if key in ("up", "k", "wheel-up"):
        state[pick] = order[max(0, at - 1)]
    elif key in ("down", "j", "wheel-down"):
        state[pick] = order[min(len(order) - 1, at + 1)]
    elif key in ("K", "home", "J", "end"):
        state[pick] = order[0] if key in ("K", "home") else order[-1]
    elif order[at] >= len(items):
        _open_unlogged(state, order[at])
    else:
        _open_failure(state, order[at])
    return False

def _k_step_record(state, key, page):
    # The chosen record first, unless it is the one the Failures tab shows:
    # then a step. The pick may be past the logged records (on a target
    # without a log): the last of them stands in.
    items = _items(state)
    if not items:
        return False
    chosen = min(state.get(_pick(state), 0), len(items) - 1)
    show_tab(state, "failures")
    step = 1 if key == "]" else -1
    shown = _shown_record(state) == items[chosen]
    _open_failure(state, (chosen + step) % len(items) if shown else chosen)
    return False

def _k_step_attempt(state, key, page):
    # Another attempt, shard or run of the target shown, stepped from the
    # one shown (else the chosen one). One shown from a list it is no
    # longer in (another list, a newer build's) opens its target's first
    # record here, or the chosen one where it has none, without a step.
    items = _items(state)
    if not items:
        return False
    chosen = min(state.get(_pick(state), 0), len(items) - 1)
    show_tab(state, "failures")
    shown = _shown_record(state)
    if shown != None and shown not in items:
        same = [i for i, r in enumerate(items) if r["label"] == shown["label"]]
        _open_failure(state, same[0] if same else chosen)
        return False
    at = items.index(shown) if shown != None else chosen
    same = sorted([i for i, r in enumerate(items) if r["label"] == items[at]["label"]], key = lambda i: (items[i].get("shard", 1), items[i].get("run", 1), items[i].get("attempt", 1), i))
    _open_failure(state, same[(same.index(at) + (1 if key == "}" else -1)) % len(same)])
    return False

def _k_find(state, key, page):
    # Where the bar opened, in the lines a search that jumps lists (a kept
    # filter's scroll counts only its matches, so the newest), no higher
    # than the top page, as `render` draws it.
    search = state["search"]
    held = search["back"] >= 0 and search["mode"] == "jump" and search["pattern"] != None and state["view"] == None
    search["open"] = True
    if search["mode"] == "filter" and search["pattern"] != None:
        search["origin"] = 0
    else:
        search["origin"] = min(state["scroll"], max(0, len(shown_lines(state)) - page))

    # Folded, the view's rows; the search reads every line, so the origin
    # is kept in lines too, `origin_rows` to go back to.
    search["origin_rows"] = search["origin"]
    if folding(state):
        search["origin"] = folded_to_lines(state, search["origin"])
    search["anchored"] = search["origin"] > 0 or held
    return False

def _scroll_by(page: int) -> dict:
    """How far each scrolling key moves the console, up as positive."""
    half = max(1, page // 2)
    return {
        "up": 1,
        "k": 1,
        "wheel-up": _WHEEL,
        "shift-up": half,
        "alt-up": half,
        "u": half,
        "ctrl-u": half,
        "b": page,
        "pageup": page,
        "down": -1,
        "j": -1,
        "wheel-down": -_WHEEL,
        "shift-down": -half,
        "alt-down": -half,
        "d": -half,
        "ctrl-d": -half,
        " ": -page,
        "pagedown": -page,
    }

def _k_scroll(state, key, page):
    state["scroll"] = max(0, state["scroll"] + _scroll_by(page)[key])
    state["search"]["anchored_view"] = False
    return False

def _k_match(state, key, page):
    step_match(state, 1 if key == "n" else -1, page)
    return False

def _k_ends(state, key, page):
    if key in ("K", "home"):
        # The top, as far as `render` lets it (the oldest lines that fill
        # the pane, wrapped).
        state["scroll"] = 1 << 30
    else:
        state["scroll"] = 0
        state["search"]["back"] = -1
    state["search"]["anchored_view"] = False
    return False

def _k_filter(state, key, page):
    state["filter"] = _FILTERS[(_FILTERS.index(state["filter"]) + 1) % len(_FILTERS)]
    state["scroll"] = 0
    state["search"]["back"] = -1
    state["search"]["anchored_view"] = False
    return False

def _k_help(state, key, page):
    state["help"] = True
    state["help_top"] = 0
    return False

def _k_open(state, key, page):
    if key == "o":
        path, line = _view_file(state)
        say_note(state, open_in_editor(state, path, line))
    else:
        say_note(state, next_editor(state))
    return False

def _k_wrap(state, key, page):
    state["wrap"] = not state.get("wrap", True)
    state["search"]["anchored_view"] = False
    return False

def _failures_listed(state):
    return state.get("tab") == "failures" and state.get("list") != "actions" and state["view"] == None

def _k_expand_failures(state, key, page):
    state["expand_failures"] = not state.get("expand_failures", False)
    return False

def _k_unfold(state, key, page):
    # The lines folded under each `INFO: From`, shown or folded again, from
    # the newest line, as the rows change under the view; what clicks
    # opened forgotten, so folding again folds every one.
    state["unfolded"] = not state.get("unfolded", False)
    if state.get("fold_open"):
        state["fold_open"] = {}
        refold(state)
    state["scroll"] = 0
    state["search"]["anchored_view"] = False
    return False

def _k_zoom(state, key, page):
    state["zoom"] = not state.get("zoom", False)
    return False

def _k_mouse(state, key, page):
    if state.get("screen") != None:
        state["mouse"] = not state.get("mouse", False)
        state["screen"].mouse(state["mouse"])

        # The folds' rows say whether a click shows them.
        refold(state)
    return False

def _k_redraw(state, key, page):
    # The next frame is written though unchanged: the terminal was cleared
    # behind it.
    state["drawn"] = None
    return False

def _k_leave(state, key, page):
    if key == "enter" and state["view"] != None:
        return False
    if state["ended"]:
        return True
    if key == "q" and not state["cancelling"]:
        # The run's root token, cancelled once: Bazel gets its own interrupt
        # sequence from it, as for Ctrl+C. A SIGINT to the process group
        # would reach the Bazel client directly too, and count twice.
        state["cancelling"] = True
        if state.get("cancellation") != None:
            state["cancellation"].root.cancel()
        else:
            state["screen"].interrupt()
    return False

def _row_page(state):
    # The macOS Terminal sends Shift+↑↓ as plain arrows, unless its
    # profile's keyboard settings say otherwise.
    apple = state.get("env") != None and state["env"].var("TERM_PROGRAM") == "Apple_Terminal"
    return ["Fn+↑↓ ⌥↑↓ u d page" if apple else "PgUp PgDn ⇧↑↓ u d page"]

def _row_leave(state):
    if not state["ended"]:
        return ["q cancel"]
    if state["focus"] == "failures" or state["view"] != None:
        return ["q leave"]
    return ["q, Enter or Esc to leave"]

def _row_step(state):
    if not _items(state):
        return []
    if state["view"] == None:
        return ["Tab next tab"]
    return ["] [ next/prev action" if state.get("list") == "actions" else "] [ next/prev failure"]

def _row_open(state):
    name = _editor(state)[1]
    return ["o open in " + name] if name else []

def _row_other_list(state):
    if not _has_other_list(state):
        return []
    return ["a failures" if state.get("list") == "actions" else "a actions"]

def _key(keys, when, do, help = None, group = None, row = None, shown = None, early = False, said = None):
    """A binding: its `keys`, `when` it applies, what it does (`do`), its
    overlay row (`help`) and keys-row labels (`row`); `said`, for a toggle,
    the state it left and how to undo it, which the keys row says a moment
    after the key (`on_key`)."""
    return struct(keys = keys, when = when, do = do, help = help, group = group, row = row, shown = shown or when, early = early, said = said)

def _said_filter(state):
    return {
        "all": "every line · e errors and warnings only",
        "warnings": "errors and warnings only · e errors only",
        "errors": "errors only · e every line",
    }[state["filter"]]

def _documented(keys, text, group, shown = _always):
    """An overlay row for keys another layer reads (the mouse, the bar)."""
    return _key([], shown, None, help = (keys, text), group = group, shown = shown)

KEYS = [
    _key(["ctrl-l"], _always, _k_redraw, help = ("^L", "redraw the screen"), group = "Screen", early = True),
    _key(["1", "2", "3", "4", "5"], _bar_closed, _k_tab_number, help = ("1-5", "a tab: console, failures, metrics, invocation, tips"), group = "Screen"),
    _key(["tab", "shift-tab"], _bar_closed, _k_tab_cycle, help = ("Tab  ⇧Tab", "the next or previous tab: console, failures, metrics, invocation, tips (or click one)"), group = "Screen"),
    _key(["p"], _always, _k_panes, help = ("p", "the build panes open or folded, and back"), group = "Screen", said = lambda s: ("build panes open · p folds them" if panes_shown(s) else "build panes folded · p opens them") if built(s) else "no build panes yet: p opens them once a build runs"),
    _key(["up", "k", "down", "j", "s"], _tips_listed, _k_tips, help = ("s", "in Tips, ↑↓ choose a tip and s silences it on this machine, or undoes it"), group = "Failures", shown = lambda s: s.get("tab") == "tips" or tips_count(s) > 0),
    _key(["wheel-up", "wheel-down"], _over_actions, _k_actions_wheel),
    _key(["<", ">"], lambda s: s.get("tab") in _PICKED_TABS and s["view"] == None, _k_pick_build, help = ("<  >", "in Metrics and Invocation, the previous or next build"), group = "Screen", shown = lambda s: len(s.get("builds", [])) > 0),
    _key(["K", "home", "J", "end", "up", "k", "wheel-up", "down", "j", "wheel-down", "pageup", "pagedown", "b", " ", "u", "d"], _in_list_tab, _k_tab_scroll),
    _key(["esc"], _always, _k_esc, help = ("Esc", "back a crumb: a file to the log it was opened from, a log to its tab; a tab to the console"), group = "Leave", row = lambda s: ["Esc back"] if s["view"] != None else []),
    _key(["a"], _has_other_list, _k_other_list, help = ("a", "the list: failures, or actions that wrote output"), group = "Failures", row = _row_other_list, said = lambda s: "actions that wrote output listed · a lists the failures" if s.get("list") == "actions" else "failures listed · a lists the actions that wrote output"),
    _key(["up", "k", "wheel-up", "down", "j", "wheel-down", "K", "home", "J", "end", "enter"], _failures_focused, _k_list, help = ("Enter", "in the list: show the chosen log"), group = "Failures", row = lambda s: ["↑↓ choose", "Enter show its log", "Tab next tab", "Esc back"], shown = lambda s: _failure_total(s) > 0 or bool(_items(s))),
    _key(["]", "["], _has_items, _k_step_record, help = ("]  [  }  {", "the next or previous failure, its log in Failures; } { its next attempt or shard"), group = "Failures", row = _row_step),
    _key(["}", "{"], _has_items, _k_step_attempt),
    _key(["ctrl-f", "/"], _always, _k_find, help = ("/  ^F", "find: jumps to the newest match; n the next one down, N the previous, round the ends"), group = "Search", row = lambda s: ["/ find"]),
    _documented("  Tab", "in the bar: filter to the matches instead, and back", "Search"),
    _documented("  ^T  ^B", "in the bar: match case (else a capital does), whole words", "Search"),
    _documented("  ^W", "in the bar: delete a word", "Search"),
    _documented("  ^U  Enter  Esc", "in the bar: clear; keep and close; clear and go back", "Search"),
    _key(["up", "k", "down", "j"], _always, _k_scroll, help = ("↑ ↓  k j", "scroll a line; in a list, choose"), group = "Scroll"),
    _key(["shift-up", "alt-up", "u", "ctrl-u", "shift-down", "alt-down", "d", "ctrl-d"], _always, _k_scroll, help = ("⇧↑↓ ⌥↑↓  u d  ^U ^D", "scroll half a page (macOS Terminal sends ⇧↑↓ as ↑↓)"), group = "Scroll"),
    _key(["pageup", "pagedown", " ", "b"], _always, _k_scroll, help = ("PgUp PgDn  Space b", "scroll a page (PgUp/PgDn are Fn+↑↓ on a Mac)"), group = "Scroll", row = _row_page),
    _key(["wheel-up", "wheel-down"], _always, _k_scroll, help = ("wheel", "scroll"), group = "Scroll"),
    _key(["n", "N"], _always, _k_match, row = lambda s: ["n N next/prev match"] if s["search"]["pattern"] != None and s["search"]["mode"] == "jump" else []),
    _key(["K", "home", "J", "end"], _always, _k_ends, help = ("K  J", "the top; the end, following the newest (Home, End too)"), group = "Scroll", row = lambda s: ["K J top/end"]),
    _key(["e"], _always, _k_filter, help = ("e", "every line, errors and warnings, errors only"), group = "Search", row = lambda s: ["e filter"], said = _said_filter),
    _documented("drag", "select, and copy on release", "Screen"),
    _documented("click", "a file's link shows the file here (o: in your editor); a web link opens; any other is copied", "Screen"),
    _documented("click a fold", "an INFO: From or its +N count: show or fold its lines (^O all)", "Screen"),
    _key(["?"], _always, _k_help, help = ("?", "these keys"), group = "Screen"),
    _key(["o", "O"], _always, _k_open, help = ("o  O", "open the log (or the task's output) in your editor; pick the editor"), group = "Screen", row = _row_open),
    _key(["w"], _always, _k_wrap, help = ("w", "wrap long lines (the default), or cut them at the edge"), group = "Screen", row = lambda s: ["w " + ("cut" if s.get("wrap", True) else "wrap")], said = lambda s: "long lines wrapped · w cuts them" if s.get("wrap", True) else "long lines cut · w wraps them"),
    _key(["ctrl-o"], _failures_listed, _k_expand_failures, help = ("^O", "in Failures: every failure's excerpt under it, and only the chosen one's again"), group = "Failures", said = lambda s: "every excerpt shown · ^O only the chosen one's" if s.get("expand_failures") else "the chosen failure's excerpt shown · ^O every one's"),
    _key(["ctrl-o"], _always, _k_unfold, help = ("^O", "show the lines under each INFO: From (a successful action's output, folded to a count), and fold every one again"), group = "Screen", said = lambda s: "lines under each INFO: From shown · ^O folds them" if s.get("unfolded") else "lines under each INFO: From folded · ^O shows them"),
    _key(["z"], _always, _k_zoom, help = ("z", "the console fills the screen, and back"), group = "Screen", row = lambda s: ["z " + ("unzoom" if s.get("zoom") else "zoom")], said = lambda s: "console zoomed · z unzooms it" if s.get("zoom") else "console unzoomed · z zooms it"),
    _key(["m"], _always, _k_mouse, help = ("m", "the mouse to the terminal and back"), group = "Screen", said = lambda s: "mouse on: the wheel scrolls, a click picks · m gives it to the terminal" if s.get("mouse") else "mouse off: the terminal selects text · m takes it back"),
    _key(["q", "enter"], _always, _k_leave, help = ("q", "cancel the build; once it is done, leave"), group = "Leave", row = _row_leave),
]

# The overlay's groups, in order.
_KEY_GROUPS = ["Scroll", "Search", "Failures", "Screen", "Leave"]

# The keys row's labels, in the order they are shown, the likeliest first,
# by what has focus: each names an entry of `KEYS` by its first key.
_ROW_FAILURES = ["up", "q", "a"]
_ROW_CONSOLE = ["esc", "o", "q", "]", "ctrl-f", "n", "e", "pageup", "K", "a", "z", "w"]

def help_rows(state: dict, width = 0) -> list:
    """The `?` overlay's rows, grouped (`_KEY_GROUPS`), the keys that do not
    apply now left out, then what the actions' glyphs are (`_actions_key`,
    in rows of `width` cells)."""
    rows = []
    for group in _KEY_GROUPS:
        entries = [k for k in KEYS if k.group == group and k.help != None and k.shown(state)]
        if not entries:
            continue
        rows.append(group)
        rows += ["  " + fit(k.help[0], 20) + " " + k.help[1] for k in entries]
    return rows + _actions_key(width, state.get("all_actions", True)) + _key_rows("Tests", _TESTS_KEY, width)

# What the bar's and the Actions rows' glyphs and words are, for the `?`
# overlay, a line each.
_ACTIONS_KEY = [
    "█ ran: executed this build, locally, sandboxed, in a worker or remotely",
    "▒ local action cache: Bazel's local action cache, or what it already knew in memory, said the outputs on disk were still valid; nothing ran or downloaded. Live, it's estimated (~)",
    "▓ disk / remote: outputs from the --disk_cache directory, or from the remote cache (including a remote executor's cached result)",
    "cache hits: local action cache + disk + remote",
    "╳ failed: executed and failed (tests excluded)",
    "○ blocked: counted done but never run, as an action they need failed",
    "░ internal: Bazel bookkeeping with no command (runfiles trees, file writes, workspace status)",
    "▞ other: actions whose spawns had mixed results, or that the execution log has none for; while a build where an action failed runs, also what Bazel counted done with no event (blocked, or the local action cache's)",
    "? unreported: Bazel counted these done but hasn't said how yet; they settle into ran, a cache hit or failed (┈ on the bar)",
    "─ not done: running or pending",
    "actions: what Bazel checked this build. Targets whose inputs didn't change are skipped before this count, so it isn't the whole graph",
    "%: cache hits ÷ actions; targets Bazel skipped without checking aren't counted, so a warm and a fresh server aren't comparable",
]

# `other`'s key where a build does not publish every action: while it runs
# it cannot tell those apart.
_OTHER_KEY = "▞ other: actions whose spawns had mixed results; while it runs, also the local action cache, internal, or not logged yet"

def _actions_key(width: int, published = True) -> list:
    """The `?` overlay's rows saying what the actions' glyphs and words
    are: a heading, then a line each, wrapped to `width` cells (whole
    without a width); without every action `published` (`actions =
    "failed"`), what `other` is while a build runs."""
    rows = ["Actions"]
    for part in _ACTIONS_KEY if published else [_OTHER_KEY if p.startswith("▞ other:") else p for p in _ACTIONS_KEY]:
        lines = [""]
        for word in part.split(" "):
            if width and lines[-1] and text_width(lines[-1] + " " + word) > width - (2 if len(lines) == 1 else 4):
                lines.append("")
            lines[-1] += (" " if lines[-1] else "") + word
        rows += ["  " + lines[0]] + ["    " + l for l in lines[1:]]
    return rows

# What the Tests row says past its counts (`test_progress`, `tests_cached_text`).
_TESTS_KEY = ["─ running · pending: test targets", "(N cached): every run from a cache"]

def _key_rows(title: str, parts: list, width: int) -> list:
    """`title`, then `parts` as many to a row of `width` cells as fit (all
    on one without a width), indented under it."""
    rows = [[]]
    for part in parts:
        if width and rows[-1] and 2 + text_width(" · ".join(rows[-1] + [part])) > width:
            rows.append([])
        rows[-1].append(part)
    return [title] + ["  " + " · ".join(row) for row in rows]

def row_labels(state: dict) -> list:
    """The keys row's labels for what has focus (`_ROW_FAILURES`,
    `_ROW_CONSOLE`), each from its entry in `KEYS`."""
    order = _ROW_FAILURES if state["focus"] == "failures" else _ROW_CONSOLE
    out = []
    for name in order:
        for k in KEYS:
            if k.row != None and k.keys and (k.keys[0] == name):
                out += k.row(state)
                break
    return out

# Under this many rows the links row goes first, so the panes, the console
# and the keys row keep their place.
_SHORT_ROWS = 16

# The smallest terminal the screen draws in.
_MIN_COLS = 40
_MIN_ROWS = 12

def refusal(std):
    """Why the screen does not take this terminal, for a line on stderr, or
    "" when it does; None when it is not one to ask (not a terminal, CI),
    where Bazel's console is what is wanted. Under a coding agent the
    screen would wait for keys nobody types, so it refuses even when
    enabled."""
    io = std.io
    if not (io.stdout.is_tty and io.stderr.is_tty) or std.env.var("CI"):
        return None
    if under_agent(std):
        return "dashboard off: a coding agent is running this; Bazel's console is shown instead"
    term = std.env.var("TERM") or ""
    if term in ("", "dumb"):
        return "dashboard off: TERM is %s; Bazel's console is shown instead" % ("dumb" if term else "not set")
    size = std.term.size()
    if size == None:
        return None
    if size[0] < _MIN_COLS or size[1] < _MIN_ROWS:
        return "dashboard off: the terminal is %dx%d, under %dx%d; Bazel's console is shown instead" % (size[0], size[1], _MIN_COLS, _MIN_ROWS)
    return ""

# How many of Bazel's errors and of the failures the scrollback lists.
_REPORT_FAILURES = 20

def _log_paths(state: dict, record: dict) -> list:
    """Each of a failure's logs, `(stream, path)`, where it reads now (as
    `_log_path` finds one)."""
    out = []
    for name, value in record["files"]:
        path = _log_path(state, dict(record, files = [(name, value)]))
        if path:
            out.append((name, path))
    return out

def _paths_text(state: dict, record: dict) -> str:
    """A failure's logs for the report: the one path, or each by its
    stream."""
    paths = _log_paths(state, record)
    if len(paths) == 1:
        return "  " + paths[0][1]
    return "".join(["  %s: %s" % (name, path) for name, path in paths])

def _log_path(state: dict, record: dict) -> str:
    """Where a failure's log reads now: the history's copy of an action's
    (`kept_action_log`), which outlives Bazel's own, before Bazel's; "" for
    none."""
    build = state.get("build")
    if record["kind"] == "action" and build != None:
        kept = build.kept_action_log(record.get("output", "")) or {}
        for name, _ in record["files"]:
            if name in kept:
                return kept[name]
    for _, value in record["files"]:
        for path in log_paths(state, record, value):
            if state["read"](path, 0) != None:
                return path
    return ""

def _flaky(state: dict, record: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether `record` is a failed attempt of a test that passed in the end."""
    return record["kind"] == "test" and bool(state["counts"].get("flaky", {}).get(record["label"]))

def replay_lines(state: dict, tty: bool) -> list:
    """The console as the screen showed it, for the scrollback once the
    screen is gone: every line it kept, Bazel's labels coloured on a
    terminal, the CLI's own as it wrote them (unmarked), after a line
    counting the oldest it did not keep; not what the task wrote before the
    screen was taken (`seeded`), which the main screen shows already."""
    out = []
    not_kept = state.get("dropped", 0) - state.get("seed_trimmed", 0)
    if not_kept > 0:
        out.append(_counts("… %d older lines not kept: aspect history show for all of it", not_kept))
    for line in state["log"][state.get("seeded", 0):]:
        out.append(state.get("colour_of", {}).get(line, line) if tty else line)
    return out

def report_lines(state: dict, task_id: str, tty: bool, replayed = False, linked = None, excerpt = None) -> list:
    """What the scrollback keeps once the screen is gone, for a person and
    for an agent reading a cut capture's tail alike: Bazel's closing block
    as it wrote it (its test summary, `Elapsed time`, `Build completed`),
    its head and its tail, unless the console was `replayed` just above it
    (`replay_lines`); the flaky tests' failed attempts; the results pages;
    and last the failure summary (`failure_summary`, as the console prints
    it without the Dashboard: each failure once with what Bazel said of
    it, the counts, Bazel's raw log and `aspect history show`). The summary
    goes only here, never to the console the screen shows nor to the task's
    output it dumps, so a run replayed from the history shows it no more
    than the screen did. `linked` links the summary's paths and `excerpt`
    picks a test log's lines (`failure_summary`), in the screen's width."""
    out = []
    bold = _c("strong") if tty else ""
    reset = _RESET if tty else ""
    tail = state.get("closing_tail", [])
    cut = state.get("closing_cut", 0) + max(0, len(tail) - _CLOSING_TAIL)
    closing = state.get("closing", []) + ([_counts("… %d more of Bazel's lines", cut)] if cut else []) + tail[-_CLOSING_TAIL:]
    if not replayed:
        out.extend(closing)
    flaky = [rec for rec in state["fails"] if _flaky(state, rec)]
    if flaky:
        yellow = _c("warning") if tty else ""
        out.append(bold + "Flaky" + reset + " (passed on a later attempt):")
        for rec in flaky[:_REPORT_FAILURES]:
            path = _log_path(state, rec)
            out.append("  %s~%s %s  %s%s" % (yellow, reset, rec["label"], failure_detail(state, rec, flaky_note = False), ("  " + path) if path else ""))
    for label, url in results_pages(state["bes_base"], state["bazel_uuid"], state["update"]):
        out.append("%s: %s" % (label, link(url, url) if tty else url))
    return out + failure_summary(state, task_id, tty, state.get("log_path", ""), linked, excerpt, state.get("cols") or 100, state.get("bazel_exit"))

def summary_state(read, env = None, ansi = None) -> dict:
    """A fresh state for `failure_summary` to read, collected by the build
    event hooks the screen uses (`count_event`, `note_event`,
    `note_blocked`) without a screen: the plain console's. `read` reads a
    file's tail (`std.fs.try_read_tail`), `env` is `std.env`, for finding
    a test's log, and `ansi` the compiled `ANSI` pattern, to read a kept
    log's text as an error quotes it."""
    state = dict(attempt_state(0), read = read, env = env, ansi = ansi, build = None, exec_root = "", output_dirs = {}, search = new_search(), list = "failures", ended = False, cancelling = False, log_path = "", task = "", root_causes = {}, blocked_cache = None, building = True)
    return state

# The most root causes the failure summary lists, the rest counted.
_SUMMARY_MAX = 10

def summary_counts(state: dict) -> str:
    """The failure summary's counts: targets built, failed and blocked,
    tests passed, failed, flaky and skipped, after `interrupted · ` where
    the build was cut short or the task cancelled before it returned
    (between its builds too)."""
    c = state["counts"]
    built_n, targets_failed, targets_blocked = target_counts(state)
    inv = state.get("inv") or {}
    interrupted = build_outcome(inv) == "interrupted" or bool(state.get("cancelling"))
    return _counts("%s%d built, %d failed%s · %d %s passed, %d failed%s%s", (
        "interrupted · " if interrupted else "",
        built_n,
        targets_failed,
        _counts(", %d blocked", targets_blocked) if targets_blocked else "",
        c["tests_passed"],
        "test" if c["tests_passed"] == 1 else "tests",
        c["tests_failed"],
        _counts(", %d flaky", c["tests_flaky"]) if c.get("tests_flaky") else "",
        _counts(", %d skipped", c["tests_skipped"]) if c.get("tests_skipped") else "",
    ))

def quoted_rows(line: str, width: int) -> list:
    """`line` as the summary quotes it in `width` cells: its tabs spaces to
    the next 8-column stop of its text, cut to `_MAX_QUOTED_BYTES`, wrapped
    at the last space that fits where the word after it fits a row (one
    wider than a row fills it mid-word), at most `_EXCERPT_ROWS` rows, `…`
    where it was cut; each row after the first two cells further in."""
    line = _expand_tabs(line)
    cut = len(line) > _MAX_QUOTED_BYTES
    if cut:
        line = line[:_MAX_QUOTED_BYTES]
    rows = []
    rest = line
    for _ in range(len(line) + 1):
        if not rest or len(rows) == _EXCERPT_ROWS:
            break
        room = max(1, width if not rows else width - 2)
        piece = wrap_line(rest, room)[0][0]
        if len(piece) < len(rest):
            space = piece.rfind(" ")
            word = rest[space + 1:].partition(" ")[0] if space > 0 else ""
            if space > 0 and text_width(word) <= max(1, width - 2):
                piece = piece[:space + 1]
            rows.append(piece.rstrip())
        else:
            rows.append(piece)
        rest = rest[len(piece):].lstrip(" ") if rows else rest
    if rest:
        cut = True
    if not rows:
        rows = [line]
    if cut:
        last = rows[-1].rstrip()
        room = width if len(rows) == 1 else width - 2
        if text_width(last) + 1 > room:
            last = last[:-1]
        rows[-1] = last + "…"
    return rows

def _expand_tabs(line: str) -> str:
    """`line` with each tab spaces to the next 8-column stop."""
    if "\t" not in line:
        return line
    pieces = line.split("\t")
    out = pieces[0]
    for piece in pieces[1:]:
        out += " " * (8 - text_width(out) % 8) + piece
    return out

def excerpt_rows(lines: list) -> tuple:
    """`(rows, more)` of an excerpt's `lines` as the summary and the
    Failures list show them: at most `_EXCERPT_LINES` rows, the lines left
    out between two shown ones a `_GAP_ROW` among them (counting as one;
    too many, the lines just before a gap go first, so the output's last
    lines stay), and `more`, every line left out, the gaps' (`… 3 more
    lines`, as an excerpt counts them) and those past the last row alike."""
    rows = []
    more = 0
    for line in lines:
        if not line:
            continue
        if _is_gap(line):
            more += int(line[len("… "):].partition(" ")[0].replace(",", ""))
            if rows and rows[-1] != _GAP_ROW:
                rows.append(_GAP_ROW)
            continue
        rows.append(line)
    if rows and rows[-1] == _GAP_ROW:
        rows = rows[:-1]

    # Too many with a gap: the lines just before it go, so the gap and the
    # output's last lines stay.
    for _ in range(len(rows)):
        gaps = [k for k, r in enumerate(rows) if r == _GAP_ROW]
        if len(rows) <= _EXCERPT_LINES or not gaps or gaps[-1] < 2:
            break
        rows = rows[:gaps[-1] - 1] + rows[gaps[-1]:]
        more += 1
    if len(rows) > _EXCERPT_LINES:
        more += len([r for r in rows[_EXCERPT_LINES:] if r != _GAP_ROW])
        rows = rows[:_EXCERPT_LINES]
        if rows[-1] == _GAP_ROW:
            rows = rows[:-1]
    return (rows, more)

def _test_excerpt(text: str, excerpt, timed_out: bool) -> tuple:
    """`(lines, more)` of a test's log for the summary: what `excerpt`
    (`std.excerpt`, the console recap's choice) picks once Bazel's preamble
    is left out, its last lines where nothing reads as an error; at most
    two of a timed-out test's. Its `… N more lines` between two spans stay
    in `lines`, for `excerpt_rows` to count with what it cuts; `more`
    counts the lines before the first span and after the last, so the two
    counts together are every line left out."""
    lines = [l for l in text.split("\n") if not any([l.startswith(p) for p in _TEST_LOG_PREAMBLE])]
    for _ in range(len(lines)):
        if lines[-1].strip():
            break
        lines = lines[:-1]
    if not lines:
        return ([], 0)
    if excerpt == None:
        picked = lines[-_EXCERPT_LINES:]
        return (picked, len(lines) - len(picked))
    got = excerpt("\n".join(lines), max_lines = 2 if timed_out else _EXCERPT_LINES, before = 1, after = 2, tail = 1)
    picked = list(got["lines"])
    between = 0
    for l in picked:
        if _is_gap(l):
            between += int(l[len("… "):].partition(" ")[0].replace(",", ""))
    shown = len([l for l in picked if not _is_gap(l)])
    return (picked, max(0, len(lines) - shown - between))

def _is_gap(line: str) -> bool:
    """Whether `line` is an excerpt's count of lines it left out."""
    return line.startswith("… ") and line.endswith((" more line", " more lines"))

def _attempts_text(n: int, of = None) -> str:
    """`failed 1 of 2 attempts`: `n` failed of `of` (all of them when
    not known)."""
    of = max(n, of or n)
    return _counts("failed %d of %d attempt", (n, of)) + ("" if of == 1 else "s")

def _seconds(ms: int) -> str:
    return "%d.%ds" % (ms // 1000, ms % 1000 // 100)

def _plain_text(state: dict, text: str) -> str:
    """`text` without its escape sequences (`state["ansi"]`, the compiled
    `ANSI` pattern) and with each CRLF or lone CR a newline, as an error's
    quoted body reads."""
    if state.get("ansi") != None:
        text = state["ansi"].replace(text, "")
    return text.replace("\r\n", "\n").replace("\r", "\n")

def _workspace(state: dict) -> str:
    """The workspace directory Bazel's `build_started` names, else ""."""
    started = (state.get("inv") or {}).get("build_started")
    return str(_get(started, "workspace_directory", "") or "")

def own_excerpt(state: dict, rec: dict, excerpt) -> tuple:
    """`(details, lines, log)` of a failed action from its record alone:
    its exit code (`exit 3`, none for 0), what `excerpt` (`std.excerpt`,
    the recap's choice) picks of its own log, escapes and CRs left out
    (`_plain_text`), its last lines without `excerpt`, and that log's path;
    no location, which only a console tie gives (`console_ties`)."""
    details = ["exit %d" % rec["exit_code"]] if rec.get("exit_code") else []
    path = _log_path(state, rec) if state.get("read") != None else ""
    text = _plain_text(state, _tail_text(state, path) or "").rstrip("\n")
    lines = []
    if text.strip() and excerpt != None:
        lines = list(excerpt(text, max_lines = _EXCERPT_LINES, before = 1, after = 2, tail = 1)["lines"])
    elif text.strip():
        lines = text.split("\n")[-_EXCERPT_LINES:]
    return (details, lines, path)

def test_group(state: dict, recs: list) -> dict:
    """A failed test's records as the summary and the Failures list tell
    it: `rec`, the last attempt of its first failed shard or run (`index`
    among `recs`), `details` for its heading (`shard 2 of 3 · run 1 of 2`,
    then `failed 2 of 2 attempts · 0.4s` or `timed out after 5.0s`),
    `others`, each other failed shard or run's last attempt with what names
    it (`shard 3 of 3 · run 2 of 2`), and whether it `timed_out`."""
    label = recs[0]["label"]
    key = (label, recs[0].get("config", ""))
    runs, shards = state["test_counts"].get(key, (0, 0))
    seen_runs, seen_shards = state["spread"].get(key, (1, 1))
    shards, runs = max(shards, seen_shards), max(runs, seen_runs)

    def named(at):
        return " · ".join((["shard %d of %d" % (at[0], shards)] if shards > 1 else []) + (["run %d of %d" % (at[1], runs)] if runs > 1 else []))

    parts = {}
    order = []
    for rec in recs:
        at = (rec.get("shard", 1), rec.get("run", 1))
        if at not in parts:
            order.append(at)
        parts.setdefault(at, []).append(rec)
    order = sorted(order)
    first = parts[order[0]]
    last = first[-1]
    attempts = len(first)

    # Every attempt Bazel made of that shard and run, the passed one of a
    # flaky test too (`last_attempts`, from its test results).
    tried = (state.get("last_attempts") or {}).get((label, recs[0].get("config", ""), order[0][1], order[0][0]), 0)
    tried = max([tried, attempts] + [r.get("attempt", 1) for r in first])
    details = [named(order[0])] if named(order[0]) else []
    timed_out = last.get("status") == "timeout"
    ms = last.get("duration_ms", 0) or 0
    if timed_out:
        details.append("timed out" + (" after " + _seconds(ms) if ms else ""))
    else:
        details.append(_attempts_text(attempts, tried))
        if ms >= 100:
            details.append(_seconds(ms))
    others = [(named(at), parts[at][-1]) for at in order[1:]]
    return {"rec": last, "index": [i for i, r in enumerate(recs) if r == last][-1], "details": details, "others": others, "timed_out": timed_out}

# What an entry says when Bazel's events give a failed target no reason.
_NO_REASON = "Bazel's events give no reason; its error is in the console"

def _utf8(codes: list):
    """The text UTF-8 bytes `codes` decode to, or None where they are no
    UTF-8."""
    out = []
    i = 0
    for _ in range(len(codes)):
        if i >= len(codes):
            break
        b = codes[i]
        if b < 0x80:
            out.append(chr(b))
            i += 1
            continue
        if b >= 0xf0 and b < 0xf5:
            size, cp = 4, b & 0x07
        elif b >= 0xe0 and b < 0xf0:
            size, cp = 3, b & 0x0f
        elif b >= 0xc2 and b < 0xe0:
            size, cp = 2, b & 0x1f
        else:
            return None
        if i + size > len(codes):
            return None
        for k in range(1, size):
            c = codes[i + k]
            if c < 0x80 or c > 0xbf:
                return None
            cp = (cp << 6) | (c & 0x3f)
        out.append(chr(cp))
        i += size
    return "".join(out)

def _utf8_bytes(cp: int) -> list:
    """Code point `cp`'s UTF-8 bytes."""
    if cp < 0x80:
        return [cp]
    if cp < 0x800:
        return [0xc0 | (cp >> 6), 0x80 | (cp & 0x3f)]
    if cp < 0x10000:
        return [0xe0 | (cp >> 12), 0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3f), 0x80 | (cp & 0x3f)]
    return [0xf0 | (cp >> 18), 0x80 | ((cp >> 12) & 0x3f), 0x80 | ((cp >> 6) & 0x3f), 0x80 | (cp & 0x3f)]

def repaired(text: str) -> str:
    """`text` as Bazel meant it. Its build events carry a non-ASCII
    string's UTF-8 bytes each as a Latin-1 character (`ÐµÐ` for `е`): one
    whose characters are all such bytes and decode as UTF-8 is given back
    decoded; any other text as it is."""
    if not text:
        return text
    codes = list(text.codepoints())
    if max(codes) > 255 or max(codes) < 128:
        return text
    return _utf8(codes) or text

def percent_decoded(text: str) -> str:
    """A URI's path with its `%XX` escapes decoded, as UTF-8; as it is
    where they decode to no UTF-8."""
    if "%" not in text:
        return text
    codes = []
    chars = [chr(c) for c in text.codepoints()]
    i = 0
    hexes = "0123456789abcdefABCDEF"
    for _ in range(len(chars)):
        if i >= len(chars):
            break
        c = chars[i]
        if c == "%" and i + 2 < len(chars) and chars[i + 1] in hexes and chars[i + 2] in hexes:
            codes.append(int(chars[i + 1] + chars[i + 2], 16))
            i += 3
            continue
        codes += _utf8_bytes(ord(c))
        i += 1
    return _utf8(codes) or text

def _traceback_lines(lines: list) -> list:
    """A Starlark `Traceback`'s excerpt, as the console's recap makes one:
    what comes before it, its first line, the first frame at a BUILD file
    and its code line, then the last `Error in …:` line, the lines between
    counted (`… N more lines`); any other text's lines as they are. The
    line that starts it may say more before it (`package contains errors:
    mf: Traceback …`)."""
    start = [k for k, l in enumerate(lines) if l.strip().endswith("Traceback (most recent call last):")]
    errors = [k for k, l in enumerate(lines) if l.strip().startswith("Error in ")]
    if not start or not errors or errors[-1] < start[0]:
        return lines
    error = errors[-1]
    build = [k for k in range(start[0], error) if lines[k].strip().startswith("File \"") and ("/BUILD\"" in lines[k] or "/BUILD.bazel\"" in lines[k] or lines[k].strip().startswith("File \"BUILD"))]
    keep = list(range(start[0] + 1)) + ([build[0]] + ([build[0] + 1] if build[0] + 1 < error else []) if build else []) + [error]
    out = []
    at = 0
    for k in keep:
        if k > at:
            out.append(_counts("… %d more line", k - at) + ("" if k - at == 1 else "s"))
        out.append(lines[k])
        at = k + 1
    if at < len(lines):
        out.append(_counts("… %d more line", len(lines) - at) + ("" if len(lines) - at == 1 else "s"))
    return out

def _referrers(state: dict) -> dict:
    """Each root cause Bazel names (`root_causes`) with the targets that
    name it, in their order."""
    out = {}
    for label, roots in (state.get("root_causes") or {}).items():
        for r in roots:
            out.setdefault(r, []).append(label)
    return out

def _target_entry(state: dict, rec: dict, referrers: dict, own: dict) -> bool:
    """Whether a target's record (`kind` `target`: an Aborted root cause, a
    target that failed with no action of its own) is an entry: always, but
    for a label that failed loading (an Aborted `unconfigured_label`) every
    target naming which is an entry itself (a missing source file, whose
    targets each say so)."""
    if rec["detail"] != "loading" or rec["pattern"]:
        return True
    names = referrers.get(rec["label"], [])
    return not names or [n for n in names if n not in own or n == rec["label"]] != []

def _own_labels(state: dict) -> dict:
    """The labels a failure record of their own makes an entry of, before
    any root cause decides: a failed action's, a test's, a target's that
    failed with no action of its own, an analysis root's; a label that only
    failed loading is decided by `_target_entry`."""
    key = len(state["fails"])
    cached = state.get("own_cache")
    if cached != None and cached[0] == key:
        return cached[1]
    own = {}
    for rec in state["fails"]:
        if rec["kind"] in ("action", "test") or (rec["kind"] == "target" and rec["detail"] != "loading"):
            own[rec["label"]] = True
    referrers = _referrers(state)
    for rec in state["fails"]:
        if rec["kind"] == "target" and rec["detail"] == "loading" and _target_entry(state, rec, referrers, own):
            own[rec["label"]] = True
    state["own_cache"] = (key, own)
    return own

def failure_entries(state: dict) -> dict:
    """The build's failures as the failure summary and the Failures list
    tell them, from Bazel's build events alone, in order (an `entry` each):
    the targets' first (a root cause whose analysis or loading failed, its
    description Bazel's; a target that failed with no action of its own, a
    missing source file's), then the failed actions, then the tests (the
    attempt `test_group` picks; a flaky one marked `flaky`), each in
    Bazel's order; then a failed target none of those is about and no root
    cause blocks (`_NO_REASON`); with none of those nor any of Bazel's other
    errors (`console_ties`) once a build Bazel failed has ended
    (`bazel_exit`), its exit code (`exited`). Each blocked target (`blocked_targets`)
    goes under the first entry of its first root cause (`blocked`); a root
    with no entry keeps its blocked targets in `unlisted`. An entry's
    heading and excerpt are `entry_text`'s. Cached until the failures, the
    root causes or the failed targets change."""
    fails = state["fails"]
    causes = state.get("root_causes") or {}
    key = (len(fails), len(causes), len(state["counts"]["failed_labels"]), state.get("bazel_exit"), bool(state.get("building")))
    cached = state.get("entries_cache")
    if cached != None and cached[0] == key:
        return cached[1]
    referrers = _referrers(state)
    own = _own_labels(state)
    tests = {}
    for i, rec in enumerate(fails):
        if rec["kind"] == "test":
            tests.setdefault(rec["label"], []).append(i)
    groups = [[], [], []]
    for i, rec in enumerate(fails):
        if rec["kind"] == "target":
            if _target_entry(state, rec, referrers, own):
                groups[0].append((i, rec))
        elif rec["kind"] == "action":
            groups[1].append((i, rec))
        elif tests.get(rec["label"]) != None:
            at = tests.pop(rec["label"])
            picked = at[test_group(state, [fails[j] for j in at])["index"]]
            groups[2].append((picked, fails[picked]))
    entries = []
    for group, found in enumerate(groups):
        for i, rec in found:
            entries.append({"index": i, "group": group, "label": rec["label"], "flaky": _flaky(state, rec), "pattern": rec.get("pattern", False), "blocked": []})
    blocked = blocked_targets(state)
    for label in state["counts"]["failures"]:
        if label not in own and label not in blocked:
            entries.append({"index": None, "group": 0, "label": label, "flaky": False, "pattern": False, "blocked": []})

    # A failed build always says why: with nothing else to list, an entry of
    # Bazel's exit code (`entry_text`: its raw log's last lines).
    code = state.get("bazel_exit")
    if not entries and code not in (None, 0) and not state.get("building") and not console_ties(state)["others"]:
        entries.append({"index": None, "group": 0, "label": "", "exited": code, "flaky": False, "pattern": False, "blocked": []})
    first = {}
    for e in entries:
        if e["label"] not in first:
            first[e["label"]] = e
    unlisted = {}
    for label, roots in blocked.items():
        if roots[0] in first:
            first[roots[0]]["blocked"].append(label)
        else:
            unlisted.setdefault(roots[0], []).append(label)
    result = {"entries": entries, "unlisted": unlisted}
    state["entries_cache"] = (key, result)
    return result

def _action_logs(state: dict, rec: dict) -> list:
    """An action's logs where they read now, `(stream, path)`: the
    history's copies (`kept_action_log`) before Bazel's own."""
    return _log_paths(state, rec) if state.get("read") != None else []

def entry_text(state: dict, e: dict) -> dict:
    """An entry's heading and excerpt (`kind`, `details`, `lines`, `log`,
    `extra`, `more`), the same in the failure summary and the Failures
    list, kept once worked out:

    - a failed action: its mnemonic and its command's exit code (its
      `failure_detail`'s `spawn_exit_code`), its own log's excerpt (what
      `std.excerpt` picks, `own_excerpt`) and that log;
    - a test: `test_group`'s heading and its chosen attempt's `test.log`
      excerpt; each other failed shard or run a line;
    - a target: `analysis` or `loading`, and Bazel's description of it (a
      `Traceback` cut as the console's recap cuts one); a target Bazel
      names as its own root cause with none says its package has an error;
      one that failed with no action of its own, what its `failure_detail`
      says (`missing input file '//a:b'`);
    - a failed target no event says more of: `_NO_REASON`;
    - Bazel's exit code: its raw log's last `ERROR:` lines, else, a crashed
      server's, its last lines but progress (`_last_words`).

    Once the build has ended, what Bazel's console ties to it
    (`console_ties`): its location, an action's status (`killed`) and the
    lines a tie quotes (`output 'x' was not created`, an attribute error),
    after its own.
    """
    cache = state.setdefault("excerpts", {})
    ended = not state.get("building")
    key = (e["index"], e["label"], ended)
    if key in cache:
        return cache[key]
    ties = console_ties(state) if ended else {"actions": {}, "targets": {}}
    fails = state["fails"]
    rec = fails[e["index"]] if e["index"] != None else None
    out = {"kind": "", "details": [], "lines": [], "log": "", "extra": [], "more": 0}
    if e.get("exited") != None:
        out.update(kind = "Bazel exited %d" % e["exited"], lines = _last_words(state), log = state.get("log_path", ""))
    elif rec == None:
        out["lines"] = [_NO_REASON]
    elif rec["kind"] == "action":
        details, lines, log = own_excerpt(state, rec, state.get("excerpt"))
        tied = ties["actions"].get(e["index"]) or {"location": "", "status": "", "lines": []}
        if tied["status"]:
            details = [_status_word(tied["status"])]
        out.update(kind = rec["detail"], details = details + ([tied["location"]] if tied["location"] else []), lines = tied["lines"] + lines, log = log)
    elif rec["kind"] == "test":
        recs = [r for r in fails if r["kind"] == "test" and r["label"] == rec["label"]]
        group = test_group(state, recs)
        out.update(kind = "test", details = group["details"])
        if not e["flaky"]:
            path = _log_path(state, group["rec"]) if state.get("read") != None else ""
            text = _tail_text(state, path)
            if text:
                out["lines"], out["more"] = _test_excerpt(text, state.get("excerpt"), group["timed_out"])
            elif any([type(v) == "string" and v.startswith("bytestream://") or (type(v) == "tuple" and str(v[0]).startswith("bytestream://")) for _, v in group["rec"]["files"]]):
                out["extra"].append("log is only in the remote cache")
            out["log"] = path
            for what, other in group["others"]:
                other_path = _log_path(state, other) if state.get("read") != None else ""
                out["extra"].append(what + " also failed" + (" · log " + other_path if other_path else ""))
    else:
        text = rec.get("description", "")
        if not text and (state.get("root_causes") or {}).get(rec["label"]) == [rec["label"]]:
            text = "its package has an error"
        lines = _traceback_lines([l for l in text.split("\n") if l.strip()]) if text else []
        tied = ties["targets"].get(rec["label"]) or {"location": "", "lines": []}
        lines += [l for l in tied["lines"] if l not in lines]
        workspace = _workspace(state)
        if workspace:
            lines = [l.replace(workspace + "/", "") for l in lines]
        out.update(kind = rec["detail"] if rec["detail"] != "target" else "", details = [tied["location"]] if tied["location"] else [], lines = lines)
    if out["lines"] or ended:
        cache[key] = out
    return out

# Bazel's own lines that say only that the build failed: in no entry and
# not among the other errors.
_BOILERPLATE = ("Build did NOT complete successfully", "Build failed. Not running tests", "command succeeded, but ")

# A Bazel `ERROR:` line in its colours.
_BAZEL_ERROR = "\x1b[31m\x1b[1mERROR: "

def _bazel_headers(state: dict, text: str) -> list:
    """Bazel's `ERROR:` lines in `text`, the console's kept blocks in
    order (`console_ties`), each `{start, end, plain, location, said}`: where
    the line starts and ends, its text without escapes and before the last
    CR (a redraw), its location (`pkg/BUILD.bazel:3:8`, `pkg/BUILD.bazel`,
    made relative to the workspace) and what it says after that. Where
    Bazel coloured its own (`_BAZEL_ERROR`) an uncoloured one is a tool's
    and left out."""
    ansi = state.get("ansi")
    workspace = _workspace(state)
    found = []
    at = 0
    for _ in range(len(text)):
        p = text.find("ERROR: ", at)
        if p < 0:
            break
        start = text.rfind("\n", 0, p) + 1
        end = text.find("\n", p)
        end = len(text) if end < 0 else end
        at = end + 1
        line = text[start:end]
        plain = (ansi.replace(line, "") if ansi != None else line).rpartition("\r")[2]
        if not plain.startswith("ERROR: "):
            continue
        plain = plain[len("ERROR: "):]
        head, sep, rest = plain.partition(": ")
        pieces = head.split(":")
        location = ""
        said = plain
        if sep and " " not in head and ((len(pieces) >= 2 and pieces[1].isdigit()) or head.endswith(("/BUILD", "/BUILD.bazel"))):
            location = head[len(workspace) + 1:] if workspace and head.startswith(workspace + "/") else head
            said = rest
        found.append({"start": start, "end": end, "plain": plain, "location": location, "said": said, "coloured": _BAZEL_ERROR in line})
    if [h for h in found if h["coloured"]]:
        found = [h for h in found if h["coloured"]]
    return found

def _log_texts(state: dict, rec: dict, stdout: bool):
    """An action's output as Bazel relays it to the console, from its logs
    as they read now, whole: its stderr, then, where the console carried
    Bazel's stdout too (`stdout`), its stdout; None where one is cut."""
    out = ""
    paths = dict(_action_logs(state, rec))
    for name in (("stderr", "stdout") if stdout else ("stderr",)):
        if name in paths:
            got = state["read"](paths[name], _MAX_LOG_BYTES)
            if got == None or got[1] > 0:
                return None
            out += got[0]
    return out

def _status_word(status: str) -> str:
    """How a heading says Bazel's `(Exit 3)` or `(Killed)`: `exit 3`,
    `killed`."""
    return "exit " + status[len("Exit "):] if status.startswith("Exit ") else status.lower()

def _relayed(state: dict, rec: dict, text: str, at: int, message: str, logs) -> int:
    """Where what Bazel relayed of an action ends in `text`, when what
    starts at `at` is exactly its `failure_detail` message, a newline, and
    its output (`logs`), or, for a stderr past Bazel's cap, Bazel's own
    line saying so for that very file (`stderr (<path>) <n> exceeds maximum
    size of …; skipping`); -1 when it is not."""
    if logs != None:
        needle = message + "\n" + logs
        return at + len(needle) if text[at:at + len(needle)] == needle else -1
    start = at + len(message) + 1
    if text[at:start] != message + "\n":
        return -1
    end = text.find("\n", start)
    end = len(text) if end < 0 else end
    line = text[start:end]
    for path in _too_long(state, rec):
        lead = "stderr (%s) " % path
        if line.startswith(lead) and " exceeds maximum size of " in line and line.endswith(" bytes; skipping"):
            return end + 1
    return -1

def _too_long(state: dict, rec: dict) -> list:
    """The paths Bazel names an action's stderr by (its event's URI, decoded),
    where that log is cut as read (past Bazel's own cap, which it then says
    it skipped)."""
    paths = dict(_action_logs(state, rec))
    if "stderr" not in paths:
        return []
    got = state["read"](paths["stderr"], _MAX_LOG_BYTES)
    if got == None or got[1] == 0:
        return []
    out = []
    for name, value in rec["files"]:
        if name == "stderr" and type(value) == "string":
            out += [p for p in log_paths(state, rec, value) if p not in out]
    return out

def _absorb_tails(state: dict, text: str, froms: list, spans: list, stdout: bool):
    """Extend each action output's span in `spans` (an `INFO: From` block of
    `text`, `froms`) over the `ERROR:` line Bazel wrote after it, when that
    line is the output's own held last line: when the block's output and
    that line are, exactly, the output of one action that succeeded and
    wrote some (`outputs`, which Bazel reports with
    `--build_event_publish_all_actions`), and the block's alone are not."""
    outputs = []
    for rec in state.get("outputs") or []:
        got = _log_texts(state, rec, stdout)
        if got:
            outputs.append(got)
    if not outputs:
        return
    for start, end in froms:
        body = text[text.find("\n", start) + 1:end]
        if end >= len(text):
            continue
        tail_end = text.find("\n", end)
        tail_end = len(text) if tail_end < 0 else tail_end + 1
        tail = text[end:tail_end]
        ansi = state.get("ansi")
        if not (ansi.replace(tail, "") if ansi != None else tail).rpartition("\r")[2].startswith("ERROR: "):
            continue

        # The tool's bytes, without Bazel's erase of its progress area.
        for _ in range(len(tail)):
            lead = [p for p in ("\r", "\x1b[1A", "\x1b[K") if tail.startswith(p)]
            if not lead:
                break
            tail = tail[len(lead[0]):]
        whole = body + tail
        if len([o for o in outputs if o == whole]) == 1 and body not in outputs:
            spans.append((end, tail_end))

def console_ties(state: dict) -> dict:
    """What Bazel's console adds to the failure entries, by exact ties
    alone, once the build has ended: from the blocks a `recap_errors(summary
    = True)` stage kept as Bazel wrote them (`bazel_errors`, from
    `build.output_bazel_errors()`: each of Bazel's `ERROR:` messages, each
    action's `INFO: From` output, each coloured `ERROR:` right after one),
    however long the console was. An action's `INFO: From` output is a
    tool's, never Bazel's, the `ERROR:` line Bazel wrote after it too when
    that is its held last line (`_absorb_tails`). Each Bazel `ERROR:` line
    (`_bazel_headers`) is given to at most one entry and never one of
    several that match:

    - a sandboxed action: the line's text from `failed: (<status>): ` on,
      and what follows it, are exactly its `failure_detail` message, a
      newline and its own output as Bazel relays it (its stderr, then its
      stdout where the console carried Bazel's stdout too), or, past
      Bazel's cap, Bazel's line saying it skipped that stderr (`_relayed`),
      at one line only; the line gives its location and status (`killed`);
    - a local or worker action (its message `local spawn failed for …`):
      the one line naming `target <label>)` with `(Exit <code>)` whose next
      lines are exactly its own output;
    - an action's outputs not created: the one `output '<path>' was not
      created` line for its primary output, quoted, and its location;
    - every action of a target: the location every line naming `target
      <label>)` gives, when they all give one;
    - an analysis or loading root: the one line with a location whose
      text after it is its description's first line (every line saying
      just that, a pattern's after `Skipping '<pattern>': ` too, claimed);
    - a target named by its own error (`<label>: …`, an attribute error):
      those lines, quoted, and their location when they all give one;
    - a target that failed with no action of its own: the one line ending
      `<label> failed: <message>`, else the one ending `failed:
      <message>`;
    - a root cause no line ties: the one line ending `and referenced by
      '<target>'` of the first target naming it alone, quoted without that
      end, and its location.

    Each line tied, saying exactly what an entry quotes, or naming a listed
    target as what failed (`… <label> failed: …`, `Analysis of target
    '<label>' … failed`), is claimed, with the output an action's tie spans; every
    other one but Bazel's boilerplate (`_BOILERPLATE`) is among `others`,
    `_other_errors`'. `actions` and `targets` hold each tie, by record
    index and by label; `not_kept` the bytes of Bazel's errors the stage's
    caps left out. Nothing before the build ends, or without the stage."""
    kept = state.get("bazel_errors")
    key = (len(state["fails"]), len((kept or {}).get("blocks") or []), bool(state.get("building")))
    cached = state.get("ties_cache")
    if cached != None and cached[0] == key:
        return cached[1]
    result = {"actions": {}, "targets": {}, "others": [], "not_kept": 0}
    if kept == None or state.get("building"):
        state["ties_cache"] = (key, result)
        return result
    result["not_kept"] = kept.get("not_kept", 0) or 0
    stdout = bool(kept.get("stdout"))

    # The blocks in Bazel's order, one text; an action's output under its
    # `INFO: From` is a tool's, whatever it says.
    text = ""
    spans = []
    froms = []
    for b in kept.get("blocks") or []:
        start = len(text)
        text += b["text"]
        if b["kind"] == "from":
            froms.append((start, len(text)))
            spans.append((start, len(text)))
    _absorb_tails(state, text, froms, spans, stdout)
    headers = [h for h in _bazel_headers(state, text) if not [sp for sp in spans if sp[0] <= h["start"] and h["start"] < sp[1]]]
    claimed = {}

    # The lines by what each says, so every tie is a lookup.
    by = {"status": {}, "target": {}, "said": {}, "plain": {}, "reason": {}, "failed": {}, "referrer": {}, "prefix": {}}
    for k, h in enumerate(headers):
        said = h["said"]
        what, failed, reason = said.partition(" failed: ")
        if failed:
            by["reason"].setdefault(reason, []).append(k)
            by["failed"].setdefault(what.rpartition(" ")[2], []).append(k)
            if reason.startswith("("):
                by["status"].setdefault(reason.partition("): ")[2], []).append(k)
        for marker in (" rule target ", "(from target "):
            at = said.find(marker)
            if at >= 0:
                named = said[at + len(marker):].partition(")")[0]
                by["target"].setdefault(named, []).append(k)
                break
        by["said"].setdefault(said, []).append(k)
        by["plain"].setdefault(h["plain"], []).append(k)
        if said.endswith("'") and " and referenced by '" in said:
            by["referrer"].setdefault(said.rpartition(" and referenced by '")[2][:-1], []).append(k)
        by["prefix"].setdefault(said.partition(": ")[0], []).append(k)

    def tie(found):
        return found[0] if len(found) == 1 else None

    def common(found):
        places = {headers[k]["location"]: True for k in found}
        return list(places.keys())[0] if len(places) == 1 else ""

    fails = state["fails"]
    for i, rec in enumerate(fails):
        if rec["kind"] != "action":
            continue
        out = {"location": "", "status": "", "lines": []}
        label = rec["label"]
        logs = _log_texts(state, rec, stdout)
        message = rec.get("failure", "")
        first = message.partition("\n")[0]
        if message and (logs != None or _too_long(state, rec)):
            found = []
            for k in by["status"].get(first, []):
                h = headers[k]
                at = text.find(first, h["start"], h["end"] + 1)
                end = _relayed(state, rec, text, at, message, logs) if at >= 0 else -1
                if end >= 0:
                    found.append((k, end))
            got = tie(found)
            if got != None:
                k, end = got
                claimed[k] = True
                spans.append((headers[k]["end"], end))
                out["location"] = headers[k]["location"]
                out["status"] = headers[k]["said"].partition(" failed: (")[2].partition("): ")[0]
        if not out["status"] and logs:
            found = []
            for k in by["target"].get(label, []):
                h = headers[k]
                if ("failed: (Exit %d)" % rec.get("exit_code", 0)) in h["said"] and text[h["end"] + 1:h["end"] + 1 + len(logs)] == logs:
                    found.append(k)
            k = tie(found)
            if k != None:
                claimed[k] = True
                spans.append((headers[k]["end"], headers[k]["end"] + 1 + len(logs)))
                out["location"] = headers[k]["location"]
                out["status"] = "Exit %d" % rec.get("exit_code", 0)
        made = rec.get("output", "").partition("/bin/")[2]
        if made:
            k = tie(by["said"].get("output '%s' was not created" % made, []))
            if k != None:
                claimed[k] = True
                out["location"] = out["location"] or headers[k]["location"]
                out["lines"].append(headers[k]["said"])

        # Every action of a target where its own lines name it alike.
        named = by["target"].get(label, [])
        for k in named:
            claimed[k] = True
        out["location"] = out["location"] or common(named)
        for k in by["reason"].get(first, []) if first and out["location"] else []:
            if headers[k]["location"] == out["location"]:
                claimed[k] = True
        result["actions"][i] = out

    referrers = _referrers(state)
    causes = state.get("root_causes") or {}
    for rec in fails:
        if rec["kind"] != "target":
            continue
        label = rec["label"]
        out = {"location": "", "lines": []}
        first = rec.get("description", "").partition("\n")[0]
        if first and rec["detail"] != "target":
            found = by["said"].get(first, []) + [k for k in by["plain"].get(first, []) + by["plain"].get("Skipping '%s': %s" % (label, first), []) if headers[k]["said"] != first]
            for k in found:
                claimed[k] = True
            k = tie([k for k in found if headers[k]["location"]])
            if k != None:
                out["location"] = headers[k]["location"]
        own = by["prefix"].get(label, [])
        for k in own:
            claimed[k] = True
            if headers[k]["said"] not in out["lines"]:
                out["lines"].append(headers[k]["said"])
        out["location"] = out["location"] or common(own)
        if rec["detail"] == "target" and first:
            k = tie([k for k in by["failed"].get(label, []) if headers[k]["said"].endswith(" failed: " + first)])
            if k == None:
                k = tie(by["reason"].get(first, []))
            if k != None:
                claimed[k] = True
                out["location"] = headers[k]["location"]
        for name in referrers.get(label, []):
            if name == label or causes.get(name) != [label]:
                continue
            found = by["referrer"].get(name, [])
            for k in found:
                claimed[k] = True
            k = tie(found)
            if k != None and not out["location"] and not first and not own:
                out["location"] = headers[k]["location"]
                out["lines"].append(headers[k]["said"].rpartition(" and referenced by '")[0])
        for line in out["lines"]:
            for k in by["said"].get(line, []):
                claimed[k] = True
        result["targets"][label] = out

    # A line naming a listed target as what failed (`Executing genrule
    # //an:missing failed: 1 input file(s) do not exist`, `Analysis of
    # target '//vis:vis' (config: 2d89340) failed`) is that entry's.
    for label in _own_labels(state).keys():
        for k in by["failed"].get(label, []):
            claimed[k] = True
        for k, h in enumerate(headers):
            if h["said"].startswith("Analysis of target '%s' " % label) and h["said"].endswith(" failed"):
                claimed[k] = True
    result["others"] = _other_errors(state, text, headers, claimed, spans)
    state["ties_cache"] = (key, result)
    return result

def _other_errors(state: dict, text: str, headers: list, claimed: dict, spans: list) -> list:
    """Bazel's `ERROR:` blocks no tie claimed (`console_ties`), in its
    order, each once: `{"lines": [...]}`, its line (the workspace's path
    left out of it) then the lines Bazel wrote with it (a `Traceback`'s
    frames), up to a blank line, a redraw, one in Bazel's colours or a
    line of Bazel's own (`_HEADERS`); never one inside an action's tied
    output, nor Bazel's boilerplate (`_BOILERPLATE`)."""
    workspace = _workspace(state)
    ansi = state.get("ansi")
    out = []
    seen = {}
    for k, h in enumerate(headers):
        if k in claimed or h["plain"].startswith(_BOILERPLATE) or [s for s in spans if s[0] <= h["start"] and h["start"] < s[1]]:
            continue
        lines = [h["plain"]]
        at = h["end"] + 1
        for _ in range(200):
            if at >= len(text):
                break
            end = text.find("\n", at)
            end = len(text) if end < 0 else end
            raw = text[at:end]
            plain = ansi.replace(raw, "") if ansi != None else raw
            if not plain.strip() or raw.startswith(("\x1b", "\r")) or plain.startswith(tuple(_HEADERS)) or plain.startswith(("FAILED:", "Use --")):
                break
            lines.append(plain)
            at = end + 1
        if workspace:
            lines = [l.replace(workspace + "/", "") for l in lines]
        said = "\n".join(lines)
        if said not in seen:
            seen[said] = True
            out.append({"lines": lines})
    return out

def _tab_extras(state: dict) -> list:
    """The Failures list's rows of no record of their own, after the
    records' (`listed_failures`), `(what, item)`: an entry of none
    (`("entry", entry)`: a failed target no event says more of, Bazel's
    exit code), each blocked target (`("blocked", label)`),
    then, once the build has ended, each of Bazel's other errors
    (`("other", block)`, `console_ties`)."""
    found = failure_entries(state)
    out = [("entry", e) for e in found["entries"] if e["index"] == None]
    for label in blocked_targets(state).keys():
        out.append(("blocked", label))
    for block in console_ties(state)["others"] if not state.get("building") else []:
        out.append(("other", block))
    return out

def _last_words(state: dict) -> list:
    """What Bazel's raw log (`log_path`) says last: its last `ERROR:` lines,
    else, a crashed server's, its last lines but progress (`Server
    terminated abruptly`, `FATAL: …`), at most `_EXCERPT_LINES`."""
    tail = [l for l in _plain_text(state, _tail_text(state, state.get("log_path", "")) or "").split("\n") if l.strip()]
    said = [l[len("ERROR: "):] for l in tail if l.startswith("ERROR: ")]
    if not said:
        said = [l for l in tail if not l.startswith(("[", "INFO: ", "WARNING: ", "Loading", "Analyzing", "Computing", "Starting local Bazel server", "Extracting Bazel installation", "  "))]
    return said[-_EXCERPT_LINES:]

def failure_summary(state: dict, task_id: str, tty: bool, log_path: str = "", linked = None, excerpt = None, width = 100, bazel_exit = None) -> list:
    """The end of a build's console, the same with the Dashboard or
    without: a `── Failures (N) ──` rule when anything failed, then an
    entry for each failure Bazel's build events report (`failure_entries`,
    its heading and excerpt `entry_text`'s): a heading (`✗ //pkg:t  Genrule
    · exit 1`: the label bold, its kind and details muted), then for the
    first `_EXCERPTED` an excerpt behind a muted `│`, at most
    `_EXCERPT_LINES` rows (each line cut to `_MAX_QUOTED_BYTES` or
    `_EXCERPT_ROWS` rows of `width`, then `…`; `│ …` where lines between
    two shown ones are left out; `│ … N more lines` counting every line
    left out), then a muted `log <path>`, then what else it has (another
    failed shard or run, a log only in the remote cache); every entry then
    names three of the targets it blocked (`○ blocked: //a, //b, //c +4
    blocked`), and a root cause with no entry its own line; a blank line
    apart. A flaky test has no entry. Then, under `── Other errors from
    Bazel ──`, each of Bazel's `ERROR:` blocks no entry claimed
    (`console_ties`: a BUILD file's syntax error, a macro's `Traceback`,
    `package contains errors`), as Bazel wrote it, a `Traceback` cut as
    an entry's is, capped as the entries are; `Failures (N)` counts the
    entries alone. With neither though Bazel exited non-zero
    (`bazel_exit`): `✗ Bazel exited N`, the raw log's last `ERROR:` lines,
    else, a crashed server's, its last lines but progress.

    Past `_SUMMARY_MAX` entries `… and N more failures · aspect history
    show <id>`; then the counts, Bazel's raw log (`log_path`), and where
    the rest is. Every line goes through `linked` (`ctx.std.io.linked`)
    when given, so its paths are links as the CLI's own lines on that
    terminal are. The `mono` theme (`NO_COLOR` too) draws `x` and `|` for
    `✗` and `│`."""
    bold = _c("strong") if tty else ""
    red = _c("error") if tty else ""
    muted = _c("muted") if tty else ""
    reset = _RESET if tty else ""
    ascii = theme.info()["name"] == "mono"
    cross = "x" if ascii else "✗"
    bar = "|" if ascii else "│"
    state = dict(state, log_path = log_path or state.get("log_path", ""), building = False, excerpt = excerpt or state.get("excerpt"), excerpts = {}, entries_cache = None, blocked_cache = None, own_cache = None, bazel_exit = bazel_exit if bazel_exit != None else state.get("bazel_exit"))
    found = failure_entries(state)
    entries = [e for e in found["entries"] if not e["flaky"]]

    def blocked_line(stopped):
        more = len(stopped) - 3
        return "  ○ blocked: " + ", ".join(stopped[:3]) + (_counts(" +%d blocked", more) if more > 0 else "")

    shown = []
    for e in entries:
        text = entry_text(state, e)
        rows, gaps = excerpt_rows(text["lines"])
        shown.append(dict(text, label = e["label"], rows = rows, more = text["more"] + gaps, blocked = e["blocked"]))

    out = []
    if shown:
        total = len(shown)
        listed = shown[:_SUMMARY_MAX]
        title = ("Failures (first %d of %d)" % (len(listed), total)) if total > len(listed) else ("Failures (%d)" % len(listed))
        out.append(red + bold + "── " + title + " ──" + reset)
        for i, e in enumerate(listed):
            if i:
                out.append("")
            said = ([e["kind"]] if e["kind"] else []) + e["details"]
            name = e["label"] or ("error" if not said else "")
            out.append(red + cross + reset + " " + bold + name + reset + (("  " if name else "") + muted + " · ".join(said) + reset if said else ""))
            if i < _EXCERPTED:
                for line in e["rows"]:
                    if line == _GAP_ROW:
                        out.append("  " + muted + bar + " …" + reset)
                        continue
                    for j, row in enumerate(quoted_rows(line, max(20, width - 4))):
                        out.append("  " + muted + bar + reset + " " + ("  " if j else "") + row)
                if e["more"]:
                    out.append("  " + muted + bar + reset + " " + _counts("… %d more line", e["more"]) + ("" if e["more"] == 1 else "s"))
                if e["log"]:
                    out.append("  " + muted + "log" + reset + " " + e["log"])
                for line in e["extra"]:
                    out.append("  " + muted + line + reset)
            if e["blocked"]:
                out.append(blocked_line(e["blocked"]))

        # Targets blocked by a root cause with no entry.
        for root in sorted(found["unlisted"].keys()):
            out.append("○ " + root + " " + blocked_line(found["unlisted"][root]).strip()[2:])
        if total > len(listed):
            out.append(_counts("… and %d more failure", total - len(listed)) + ("" if total - len(listed) == 1 else "s") + " · aspect history show %s" % task_id)
    others = console_ties(state)["others"]
    if others:
        if out:
            out.append("")
        out.append(red + bold + "── Other errors from Bazel ──" + reset)
        for i, block in enumerate(others[:_SUMMARY_MAX]):
            lines = _traceback_lines(block["lines"])
            for j, row in enumerate(quoted_rows(lines[0], max(20, width - 9))):
                out.append((red + "ERROR:" + reset + " " if not j else "         ") + row)
            if i < _EXCERPTED:
                rows, more = excerpt_rows(lines[1:])
                for line in rows:
                    if line == _GAP_ROW:
                        out.append("  " + muted + bar + " …" + reset)
                        continue
                    for j, row in enumerate(quoted_rows(line, max(20, width - 4))):
                        out.append("  " + muted + bar + reset + " " + ("  " if j else "") + row)
                if more:
                    out.append("  " + muted + bar + reset + " " + _counts("… %d more line", more) + ("" if more == 1 else "s"))
        if len(others) > _SUMMARY_MAX:
            out.append(_counts("… and %d more", len(others) - _SUMMARY_MAX) + " · aspect history show %s" % task_id)
    not_kept = console_ties(state)["not_kept"]
    if not_kept:
        out.append(muted + _counts("(%d bytes of Bazel's errors not kept)", not_kept) + reset)
    raw = (" · Bazel's raw log: " + log_path) if log_path else ""
    out.append(summary_counts(state) + raw)
    out.append("All of it: aspect history show %s" % task_id)
    return [linked(l) for l in out] if linked else out

def _dashboard_impl(ctx: FeatureContext):
    why = refusal(ctx.std)
    if why != "":
        if why:
            ctx.std.io.stderr.write(why + "\n")
        return

    state = {
        "screen": None,
        "task": "",
        "phase": "Starting Bazel…",
        "start_ms": 0,
        "now_ms": 0,
        "frames": 0,
        "progress": [],
        "log": [],
        "errors": [],
        "scroll": 0,
        "filter": "all",
        "search": new_search(),
        "re": None,
        "read": None,
        "ansi": None,
        "controls": None,
        "env": None,
        "fails": [],
        "outputs": [],
        "list": "failures",
        "selected": 0,
        "picked": 0,
        "focus": "console",
        "view": None,
        "help": False,
        "zoom": False,
        "mouse": ctx.args.mouse,
        "line_links": {},
        "select": None,
        "frame": [],
        "pane": (-1, -1),
        "excerpt": ctx.std.excerpt,
        "cols": 80,
        "osc": "",
        "note": "",
        "clock": lambda: int(monotonic() * 1000),
        "process": None,
        "unseen": 0,
        "cache": None,
        "countdown_ms": None,
        "exec_root": "",
        "output_dirs": {},
        "test_counts": {},
        "last_attempts": {},
        "spread": {},
        "cancelling": False,
        "all_actions": ctx.args.actions == "all",
        "ended": False,
        "counts": new_counts(),
        "error_count": 0,
        "fresh": False,
        "log_path": "",
        "pages": [],
        "bes_base": "",
        "bazel_uuid": "",
        "update": {},
    }

    # The first attempt's fields, as a retry's start them.
    for key, value in attempt_state(0).items():
        if key not in state:
            state[key] = value

    def _add_lines(lines, links = None, colours = None, cli = False):
        add_lines(state, lines, links, colours, cli)

    def _draw(screen, frame):
        """Write `frame` unless it is the one on the screen; whether it was
        written."""
        if frame == state.get("drawn"):
            return False
        screen.write(frame)
        state["drawn"] = frame
        return True

    def _add_output(screen):
        # The CLI's own lines, marked as such, their links moved with them.
        got = screen.output(with_links = True, with_colour = True)
        _add_lines([line for line, _, _ in got], [links for _, links, _ in got], [coloured for _, _, coloured in got], cli = True)

    def _take(task_ctx):
        """Take the terminal for the task, from its start: what it writes
        is captured into the console from here on."""
        state["screen"] = task_ctx.std.term.take()
        state["drawn"] = None

        # What the task wrote before the screen (the banner, its header),
        # first in the console; on the main screen already, so not
        # replayed on leaving.
        early = state["screen"].earlier()
        state["seeded"] = state.get("seeded", 0) + len(early)
        _add_lines([line for line, _, _ in early], [links for _, links, _ in early], [coloured for _, _, coloured in early], cli = True)

        # The mouse taken unless asked not to (`m` outlives a screen the
        # runtime took back), and its wheel as arrows while it is the
        # terminal's.
        state["screen"].wheel_keys(True)
        if state["mouse"]:
            state["screen"].mouse(True)
        state["re"] = task_ctx.std.re
        state["cli_version"] = task_ctx.std.env.aspect_cli_version() or ""
        state["read"] = task_ctx.std.fs.try_read_tail
        state["ansi"] = task_ctx.std.re.compile(ANSI)
        state["controls"] = task_ctx.std.re.compile(CONTROLS)
        state["env"] = task_ctx.std.env
        state["process"] = task_ctx.std.process
        state["io"] = task_ctx.std.io
        state["fs"] = task_ctx.std.fs
        state["syntax"] = task_ctx.std.syntax
        state["cancellation"] = task_ctx.cancellation
        state["task"] = task_ctx.task.kind
        state["start_ms"] = int(monotonic() * 1000)
        state["task_start_ms"] = state["start_ms"]
        state["silencer"] = lambda id, on: silence_tip(task_ctx, id) if on else unsilence_tip(task_ctx, id)
        state["tips_from"] = lambda: collect_tips_sorted(task_ctx, surface = SURFACE_DASHBOARD)

    def _pre(task_ctx):
        if task_ctx.task.kind not in _KINDS:
            task_ctx.std.io.stderr.write("Dashboard: for %s; %s shows its console\n" % (", ".join(_KINDS), task_ctx.task.kind))
            return
        if task_ctx.std.term.taken:
            return
        _take(task_ctx)
        _tick(state["screen"])

    def _tips():
        # The task's tips as they stand, for the Tips tab.
        if state.get("tips_from") != None:
            state["tips"] = state["tips_from"]()

    def _tick(screen):
        """One frame while no build draws them: the CLI's new lines, the keys
        waiting, the screen redrawn if it changed. Never inside itself: a key
        that waits (a click's opener) runs the idle hook."""
        if state.get("ticking"):
            return
        state["ticking"] = True
        _tips()
        state["now_ms"] = int(monotonic() * 1000)
        state["frames"] += 1
        _add_output(screen)
        cols, rows = screen.size()
        page = console_height(state, cols, rows)
        for _ in range(_MAX_KEYS):
            key = screen.key()
            if key == None:
                break
            on_key(state, key, page)
        _draw(screen, render(state, cols, rows))
        state["ticking"] = False

    def _idle(task_ctx):
        # Between builds, and before the first: a build's frames draw while
        # it runs, and the finished screen draws itself.
        screen = state["screen"]
        if screen == None or not screen.held or state.get("building") or state["ended"]:
            return
        _tick(screen)

    def _frame(task_ctx, build):
        # A build whose console is shown (its own `output =`) is left alone.
        feed = build.output_feed()
        if feed == None:
            return

        # The runtime gives a screen back on a hook's error.
        if state["screen"] != None and not state["screen"].held:
            state["screen"] = None
        if state["screen"] == None:
            return

        # The next build, or a retried attempt of this one: its own counts
        # and failures, in the task's console.
        now = int(monotonic() * 1000)
        if not state.get("building") or state["fresh"]:
            new_build(state, now)
            state["building"] = True
        state["build"] = build
        state["ended"] = False
        state["pages"] = results_pages(state["bes_base"], state["bazel_uuid"], state["update"])
        screen = state["screen"]
        state["now_ms"] = now
        state["frames"] += 1
        _add_lines(feed["lines"], feed.get("links"), feed.get("colours"))
        state["dropped"] = state.get("dropped", 0) + (feed.get("dropped", 0) or 0)

        # A frame that caught Bazel between drawing its progress area and
        # redrawing it keeps the last one, which the header shows too.
        if feed["progress"]:
            state["progress"] = feed["progress"]
            state["phase"] = feed["progress"][0]
            note_progress(state, feed["progress"])
        _add_output(screen)
        if state["frames"] % 10 == 0:
            _tips()
        cols, rows = screen.size()
        page = console_height(state, cols, rows)

        # Every key waiting, then one frame: a burst of wheel or drag
        # reports costs one render, never a backlog.
        for _ in range(_MAX_KEYS):
            key = screen.key()
            if key == None:
                break
            on_key(state, key, page)
        _draw(screen, render(state, cols, rows))

    def _event(task_ctx, event):
        kind = event.kind
        if kind == "build_started":
            state["bazel_uuid"] = str(_get(event.payload, "uuid", "") or "")
        elif kind == "options_parsed":
            state["bes_base"] = bes_results_url(event) or state["bes_base"]
        collect_event(state, event)

    def _update(task_ctx, update):
        if update.data:
            state["update"] = update.data

    def _attempt_end(task_ctx, exit_code):
        # The execution log is read to its end before an attempt ends.
        state["bazel_exit"] = exit_code
        state["fresh"] = True
        state["exec_log_read"] = True

    def _end(task_ctx, build):
        # Bazel's raw console, and what of it the failure summary and the
        # Failures list tie Bazel's lines from (`console_ties`).
        state["log_path"] = build.output_log or ""
        state["bazel_errors"] = build.output_bazel_errors()
        screen = state["screen"]
        if screen == None or not screen.held:
            return
        state["cancelling"] = state["cancelling"] or task_ctx.cancellation.root.cancelled
        state["build_failed"] = bool(build.output_fatal)

        end_build(state, int(monotonic() * 1000))
        state["progress"] = []
        cols, rows = screen.size()
        _draw(screen, render(state, cols, rows))

    def _stay_tips():
        # The end's tips, collected before the screen is given back, as the
        # Tips feature prints them only after.
        _tips()

    def _stay(task_ctx, screen, countdown_ms):
        """Keep the finished screen up, scrollable, until a leave key, or
        until the run is told to stop (a SIGTERM), which no key would end.
        With a `countdown_ms`, it leaves by itself once that has gone by,
        unless a key comes first, which only stops it. The screen is the state's meanwhile, for
        the keys that use it (`m`)."""
        state["screen"] = screen
        _stay_tips()
        _hold(task_ctx, screen, countdown_ms)
        state["screen"] = None

    def _hold(task_ctx, screen, countdown_ms):
        # Keys typed while it finished were typed at a screen that read none,
        # meaning to cancel: not to leave the finished one now.
        for _ in range(1 << 20):
            if screen.key() == None:
                break
        screen.interrupts(False)
        deadline = int(monotonic() * 1000) + countdown_ms if countdown_ms else None
        for _ in range(1 << 30):
            if task_ctx.cancellation.root.cancelled or not screen.held:
                return
            if deadline != None:
                state["countdown_ms"] = max(0, deadline - int(monotonic() * 1000))
                if state["countdown_ms"] == 0:
                    return
            _add_output(screen)
            cols, rows = screen.size()
            changed = _draw(screen, render(state, cols, rows))

            # Idle, a key a second; a frame that moves, or the countdown, ten.
            key = screen.key(timeout_ms = 100 if changed or deadline != None else _IDLE_MS)
            if key == "ctrl-c":
                return
            if key != None and deadline != None and key.startswith("mouse-move:"):
                # The pointer passing over the screen: lit, the countdown
                # left running.
                mouse_key(state, key)
            elif key != None and deadline != None:
                # A leave key leaves now; any other stops the countdown and
                # does nothing else.
                if key in _LEAVE_KEYS:
                    return
                deadline = None
                state["countdown_ms"] = None
            elif key != None:
                # This key and every one waiting behind it, then one frame.
                page = console_height(state, cols, rows)
                for _ in range(_MAX_KEYS):
                    if on_key(state, key, page):
                        return
                    key = screen.key()
                    if key == None or key == "ctrl-c":
                        break
                if key == "ctrl-c":
                    return

    def _post(task_ctx, outcome):
        screen = state["screen"]
        if screen == None:
            return
        state["screen"] = None
        if screen.held:
            # Not for a cancelled task (`q`, or Ctrl+C): the CLI is on its way
            # out, and would force the exit before a key came.
            if task_ctx.cancellation.root.cancelled:
                state["cancelling"] = True
            state["verdict"] = outcome.exit_code == 0

            # A task that failed before any build has nothing to browse:
            # gone at once, its error last in the scrollback.
            early = not built(state) and outcome.exit_code != 0
            if ctx.args.stay != "never" and task_ctx.std.io.stdin.is_tty and not state["cancelling"] and not early:
                state["ended"] = True
                state["progress"] = []
                state["phase"] = "Done"
                c = state["counts"]

                # A flaky test's failed attempt is no failure: it passed.
                passed = outcome.exit_code == 0 and not [r for r in state["fails"] if not _flaky(state, r)] and not c["failures"] and not c["tests_failed"]
                _stay(task_ctx, screen, _COUNTDOWN_MS if passed and ctx.args.stay == "failure" else 0)
            _add_output(screen)

            # The scrollback written as part of the release, so no line
            # written meanwhile shows twice or ahead of it (`_report`).
            rest = screen.release(replay_stderr = False, dump_lines = _MAX_LOG, older = _older(task_ctx))
            _add_lines([_plain(line) for line in rest], None, rest, cli = True)
        _report(task_ctx)

    def _older(task_ctx):
        """The dump's line for the lines it leaves out."""
        return "… {n} older lines: aspect history show " + task_ctx.task.id

    def _report(task_ctx):
        """What the scrollback keeps once the screen is gone: the task's
        output since the screen was taken, as `output.ansi` keeps it
        (`ctx.std.term.dump_output`), else the console as the screen showed
        it (`replay_lines`), then the failures, the counts and where the
        rest is (`report_lines`)."""
        err = task_ctx.std.io.stderr

        # The task's output as a run without the screen leaves it, from
        # output.ansi; the console as the screen showed it without one. Both
        # are in output.log already: not copied again.
        if not task_ctx.std.term.dump_output(max_lines = _MAX_LOG, older = _older(task_ctx)):
            for line in replay_lines(state, err.is_tty):
                err.write(line + "\n", copy = False)
        for line in report_lines(state, task_ctx.task.id, err.is_tty, replayed = True, linked = task_ctx.std.io.linked, excerpt = task_ctx.std.excerpt):
            err.write(line + "\n")
        gone = sorted(state.get("silenced_tips", {}).keys())
        if gone:
            err.write(_c("muted") + "Silenced on this machine: %s · undo: take it out of tips.silence in ~/.aspect/config.json" % ", ".join(gone) + _RESET + "\n")

    t = ctx.traits[BazelTrait]
    t.output_display = "none"
    if ctx.args.actions == "all":
        # Every action reported to this task alone, not to its BES sinks.
        publish_all_actions_unless_sent_out(t, state)

    # A processor needs a stage; the console's defaults may be off.
    t.output_stages.append(bazel.output.observe({"dashboard:error": r"^ERROR:"}))
    t.output_frame.append(_frame)
    t.output_end.append(_end)
    t.build_event.append(_event)

    def _spawn(task_ctx, entry):
        # The entry's kind is its `type`: a `spawn` here, as asked.
        count_spawn(state["counts"], _get(entry, "type"))
        note_spawn_time(state, _get(entry, "type"))

    t.exec_log_event.append(ExecLogHook(on_entry = _spawn, kinds = ["spawn"]))
    t.bazel_attempt_end.append(_attempt_end)
    ctx.traits[Phases].task_update.append(_update)
    ctx.hooks.pre_task(_pre)
    ctx.hooks.idle(_idle)
    ctx.hooks.post_task(_post)

Dashboard = feature(
    display_name = "Dashboard",
    summary = "Run build, test, lint, gazelle and format in a full-screen dashboard: the task's console, its builds' running actions and results, failures, metrics.",
    implementation = _dashboard_impl,
    enabled = False,
    args = {
        "stay": args.string(
            default = "always",
            values = ["always", "failure", "never"],
            bare = "always",
            description = "Whether the screen stays up once the task ends, until q, Enter, Esc or Ctrl+C: `always` (the default) stays whatever the outcome, to look at its tabs; `failure` stays when anything failed, and with nothing failed counts down 10 seconds and leaves, which q, Enter or Esc cut short and any other key stops; `never` leaves at once. Only when stdin is a terminal.",
        ),
        "mouse": args.boolean(
            default = True,
            description = "Have the screen take the mouse (the default): its wheel scrolls the console and never the terminal past the screen, a drag selects and copies, and a click opens a web link or a path in the editor --console:file-hyperlink-format names (any other link is copied). false leaves the mouse to the terminal, which then selects and opens links itself and sends the wheel as the arrow keys where it supports that. `m` switches between the two while the screen is up.",
        ),
        "actions": args.string(
            default = "all",
            values = ["failed", "all"],
            description = "Which actions Bazel reports to the screen: `all` (the default) adds --build_event_publish_all_actions for this task alone (BES backends the CLI streams to and a --build_event_binary_file get the extra events only if your own flags or rc ask; a --build_event_json_file or --build_event_text_file, which Bazel writes itself, gets them), so the Actions rows tell what ran, was a disk or remote cache hit or was internal while the build runs, and `a` lists the actions that wrote output; `failed` reports only those that failed (the screen then says `other` for the rest until the build's metrics come). A build with a --remote_cache or --remote_executor (unless --experimental_build_event_upload_strategy=local) or a --bes_backend of its own reports only failed actions either way: Bazel would send every action's event out itself, and with the cache down it stops the whole event stream at the first one.",
        ),
    },
)
